gests \"box is true\" should be interpreted as all statements on that box true, because statements have truth values; boxes don't. But then no solution, so maybe the actual intended statement K1 is \"either the top statement of each box is true or the white box is true\" where \"white box is true\" means \"the white box's statement is true\", leading to no solution? Could the puzzle be intentionally unsolvable? The user says \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" but maybe asks us to solve and final answer; they expect a solution. Could be a trick: no solution? But final format requires gem and T/F; maybe answer could be \"no solution\" but format doesn't allow. The instruction: \"Your task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\" It assumes solution. So must be solvable.\n\nLet's attempt to solve using formal logic if we define \"box is true\" as \"box contains gems\" and \"box with two false statements\" as \"box that displays exactly two false statements\" (false always). Then solution gem=White, B1=F,W1=F,K1=T. This is simple and unique. But is \"White box is true\" = \"White contains gems\" supported by statement K1? It says \"THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" not \"THE WHITE STATEMENT IS TRUE\" nor \"THE WHITE BOX'S STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\". In many logic puzzles, \"the white box is true\" could indeed mean \"the statement 'the gems are in the white box' is true\" because the actual state of world includes gem location; a proposition \"White box contains gems\" has a truth value. The phrase \"box is true\" is odd but could be a deliberate way to say \"White box is the correct one\". The rule \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" uses \"true\" for statements; but K1 itself is a statement, and its truth value can depend on the proposition \"White box is true\" where \"true\" might mean \"the White box's statement(s) are true\" or \"the White box contains gems\". The user gave rules: \"A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\" This explicitly says truth values depend on gem location AND truth values of statements. So \"White box is true\" likely refers to the truth value of the White box's statement(s), i.e., W1, because statements have truth values. But if so no solution, contradiction. Could \"White box is true\" refer to both? Let's model K1 with \"White box is true\" = \"White box contains gems\" because gem location is part of actual state; \"true\" could mean \"the statement 'White has gems' is true\". The phrase \"white box is true\" is not \"white box's statement is true\"; it's a statement about the box being the true one (containing gems). The rules mention gem location and truth values of every statement, so a statement can be about gem location (\"White box contains gems\") or about statements (\"W1 is true\"). K1's second disjunct \"the White box is true\" might be an abbreviation for \"the statement 'the White box contains the gems' is true\" — i.e., gem=White. The first disjunct \"the top statement of each box is true\" is explicitly about statements. The contrast suggests \"White box is true\" is not about statements on White box but about the box itself being the true/correct box (contains gems). I think this is likely intended because otherwise K1 would be redundant with W1 and puzzle unsolvable. Therefore solution gem=White, B1=F, W1=F, K1=T.\n\nNeed verify W1 false: \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" Since each box has one statement, no box has two false statements, so W1 false. But if W1 false, K1 true via \"White box is true\" = gem in White. B1 false because if gems in White, other two boxes relative to Blue: White has gems not empty, so first clause false; second clause true (White next to Blue contains gems). Conjunction false. K1 true because second disjunct true. At least one all-true: K1 box (Black) has K1 true => all statements on Black true. At least one all-false: Blue has B1 false, White has W1 false => both all-false. Remaining? Actually there are three boxes: Blue all-false, White all-false, Black all-true. Rule says at least one all-true, at least one all-false, remaining may be mix; here two all-false, one all-true, allowed? It says at least one all-true, at least one all-false, the remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix. With three boxes, if one all-true and two all-false, remaining (the second all-false? Actually after picking one all-true and one all-false, the third is all-false, allowed). So valid.\nNeed ensure exactly one gem: White.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning and final line exactly.\n\nBut before final, let's ensure no other solution under this interpretation? Suppose gem=Blue: B1 first clause true (White/Black empty), second false (no next box contains gems because White/Black empty) => B1 false. W1 false. K1 = first disjunct B1∧W1∧K1 = false; second \"White box is true\" = gem in White false => K1 false. All false => violates at least one true. So gem=Blue invalid.\nSuppose gem=Black: B1 first clause false (Black not empty); second \"box next to Blue contains gems\": if next=adjacent only White, false; if next=other, true but conjunction false. W1 false. K1 first false, second gem=White false => false. All false invalid.\nSuppose gem=White: B1 first clause false (White not empty); second true; conjunction false. W1 false. K1 first false, second true => true. At least one true (K1), at least one false (B1,W1). Valid.\nThus unique.\n\nNeed be wary: If \"box next to blue\" includes Black in B1, for gem=Black, second clause true but first false => B1 false anyway. So same.\n\nCould W1 be true if gem=White and there are two false statements **on the White box** because White contains gems and its statement W1 is false? No, false statements are not physical contents. So W1 false.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning:\n1. B1: Since exactly one box has gems, the two non-blue boxes can both be empty only if gems are in Blue. But then no box next to Blue contains gems. If gems are in White or Black, then at least one non-blue box is not empty. In all cases, conjunction false. So B1 = F.\n2. W1: Each box has exactly one statement; no box has exactly two false statements. Therefore the definite description \"box with two false statements\" does not exist, so W1 = F. (Alternatively, if W1 were true, there would have to be a box with two false statements, impossible under statement counts.)\n3. K1: First disjunct \"top statement of each box is true\" = B1∧W1∧K1. Since B1 and W1 are false, this disjunct is false regardless of K1. Thus K1's truth reduces to \"the White box is true\", i.e., the White box contains the gems. So K1 = (gem=White).\n4. Global at least one true: B1 and W1 are false; hence K1 must be true to satisfy at least one all-true box. Therefore gem=White. Then K1 true.\n5. At least one false satisfied by B1/W1 false.\n\nNeed final format exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T\n\nNeed ensure \"White box is true\" interpretation in reasoning: I should phrase \"the only way K1 can be true is if the second disjunct 'the White box is true' means the White box holds the gems\" but if user expected \"White box's statement true\" they'd object. However final solution likely gem=White. Need maybe mention \"interpreting 'the White box is true' as 'the White box contains the gems' (the only reading that makes the puzzle solvable)\"? But final answer should be decisive. The user didn't ask to discuss ambiguity. But step-by-step should be consistent. Could say \"K1's second disjunct refers to the White box being the true/correct box, i.e., containing the gems.\" That addresses ambiguity.\n\nBut wait: What if \"White box is true\" meant \"White box's statement W1 is true\" but W1 false, then no solution. Since puzzle is supposed solvable, \"White box is true\" must mean \"White box contains gems\". We can explicitly state that interpretation. However the user may expect using rules: \"box has all statements true\" not gem. Hmm. Let's examine exact wording of K1: \"EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\". It says \"THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\", not \"THE WHITE BOX'S STATEMENT IS TRUE\" nor \"THE WHITE BOX HAS THE GEMS\". In the rules, \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\" They might call such a box \"a true box\"? The phrase \"the White box is true\" might mean \"the White box has all its statements true\" = W1 true. But then K1 = W1 and no solution. Could the puzzle be intentionally relying on W1 being **not** false because \"the box with two false statements\" could be the White box if W1 itself is true? Let's revisit W1 with self-reference and global constraints maybe there is a fixed point where W1 true even though no box has two displayed false statements, because W1's truth creates a box with two false statements? No, a box's displayed statements are fixed; each box has one statement. Truth values don't change number of statements.\n\nUnless \"the box with two false statements\" refers to \"the box that contains two false statements **among the three boxes' statements**, meaning the box that has the gem is the one for which the statements on the other two boxes are both false\"? For example, if gems in White, statements on Blue and Black are false, so White is \"the box with two false statements\" (the two false statements are on the boxes other than White, but White \"has\" them in the sense they are associated with it?). Grammatically, \"the box with two false statements\" usually means statements displayed on that box. But could be \"the box that has two false statements [about it]\" as mentioned. Let's test if W1 true under \"box with two false statements\" = \"box that is referenced by exactly two false statements\" could yield another solution with K1=W1 maybe.\n\nWe need count references precisely:\nStatements:\nB1: \"The other two boxes relative to the Blue box are empty and a box next to the Blue box contains the gems.\" This statement references Blue? It uses \"the Blue box\" as reference; references White and Black as \"other two boxes\"; references \"a box next to the Blue box\" (White, possibly Black). If B1 false, which boxes have a false statement about them? It asserts White and Black empty; if false because White has gems, then the false part concerns White (and maybe Black? Black empty true). It asserts a box next to Blue contains gems; if gems in White, this part true; if gems in Blue, this part false concerning White/Black; if gems in Black and Black considered next, true; if gems in Black and next=adjacent only White, false concerning White? Hard.\nW1: \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" References the box with two false statements (unknown) and gems. If W1 false, it's a false statement about that unknown box? Circular.\nK1: \"Either the top statement of each box is true or the White box is true.\" References all top statements and White.\n\nCounting false statements about each box is ill-defined. So unlikely.\n\nWhat if \"box with two false statements\" means \"box that has two false statements **on its label**\" but each label has one statement; however the label itself maybe includes box name \"WHITE\" and statement? No.\n\nLet's consider possibility that the statements themselves are not the only statements; box labels \"BLUE\", \"WHITE\", \"BLACK\" might be statements? No.\n\nCould \"statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1\" mean each box has one statement **visible as top statement**, but there may be additional hidden statements? The prompt says \"Each box displays one or more statements.\" Then \"Here is the puzzle: BLUE (left) box: [B1] ...\" It might display only the top statement, with other statements omitted? But user explicitly says \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement