ment of each box is true or the White box is true\" = \"Either all the top statements are true, or the White box [is the one that contains the gems].\" This is natural because the puzzle asks which box contains gems; \"the White box is true\" = \"the White box is the correct/true box\". The rules about all statements true are separate; a box being \"true\" could mean it is the correct gem box. However the rule \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" uses \"TRUE\" for statements, not box. The statement K1 itself uses \"true\" in possibly ambiguous way; but since it's a statement on a box, its truth value is determined by actual state including gem location and truth values of statements. \"White box is true\" as \"White box contains gems\" refers to gem location. I think yes.\n\nLet's see if there is any alternative solution if W1 interpreted as \"the box with two false statements\" = \"the box that has exactly two false statements among all statements **written on the three boxes and on itself?**\" Let's attempt to solve equations with W1 variable not necessarily false? Suppose we allow a box to \"have two false statements\" if there are exactly two false statements among the three statements and they are both on the same box? Impossible because one per box. So W1 cannot be T. Therefore K1 must be T independent of W1? Actually if W1 false, K1 could be true only if second disjunct \"White box is true\" is true. Since first disjunct false (B1 false, W1 false), K1 = second disjunct. So second disjunct must be true. Thus \"White box is true\" must be true. If \"true\" meant W1 true, contradiction because W1 false. Therefore \"White box is true\" must mean gem=White. So logical necessity forces interpretation. This is a good argument: Given B1 and W1 false, to satisfy at least one true, K1 must be true; first disjunct false, so second disjunct true; hence \"White box is true\" must refer to White containing gems (the only remaining actual state that can be true while W1 statement false). Therefore gem=White. So even if \"White box is true\" ambiguous, the solution forces it to mean White contains gems. Nice.\n\nNeed write step-by-step concise but deductive:\n- Since each box has exactly one statement, no box can have two false statements, so W1 false.\n- B1 always false as argued.\n- To avoid all boxes false, K1 must be true.\n- K1's first part \"top statement of each box is true\" is false because B1 and W1 false, so K1's truth must come from \"the White box is true.\" Therefore White must be the gem box.\n- With gem=White, verify B1 false (White not empty), W1 false (no two-false-statement box), K1 true (White is true). At least one all-true (Black), at least one all-false (Blue and White). Exactly one gem. Consistent.\n\nNeed final line exactly one-line format no extra after. The user asked \"Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it): FINAL: ...\" So last line must be exactly that. We can include reasoning before and final line last.\n\nNeed ensure \"nothing after it\": final line no punctuation after. Use FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T\n\nLet's produce final answer with reasoning.", "content": "B1 is always false: if the gems were in Blue, the “box next to Blue contains gems” part would be false; if the gems were in White or Black, then the other two boxes relative to Blue are not both empty.\n\nW1 is false: each box has only one statement, so no box has two false statements.\n\nSo to satisfy “at least one box is all-true,” K1 must be true. The first part of K1 is false because B1 and W1 are false, so its truth must come from “the White box is true,” meaning the White box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
