o the blue box contains gems\" means either White or Black contains gems -> gem not Blue. These two conditions cannot both be true simultaneously! Because if other two empty => gem Blue; if box next to blue contains gems => gem White or Black. Contradiction, so B1 is always FALSE regardless gem location. Let's verify statement: \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\" = (White empty AND Black empty) AND (White has gems OR Black has gems). Since exactly one gem, first implies gem Blue, second implies gem not Blue. Impossible. So B1 = F always.\n\nThen at least one true and one false => W1 or K1 true (or both? At least one true among three, B1 false so W1∨K1 true). At least one false satisfied by B1.\n\nW1: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS.\" Need parse: \"the box with two false statements\" refers to a box that displays two false statements? But each box displays one statement. Hmm statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1. But statement W1 says \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" — There is no box with two false statements because each box has one statement. Wait but maybe \"the box with two false statements\" could refer to a box that has a display containing multiple statements? But problem explicitly says statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1 and label every statement id. So no box has two statements, thus no box has two false statements. Then W1 would be false if interpreted literally. But perhaps in puzzle original, each box has multiple statements; here simplified each has one but statement refers to box with two false statements maybe among statements across all boxes? Actually \"box with two false statements\" could mean the box whose statement? Wait each box has one statement, so no box has two false statements; W1 is false. But then we need at least one true; K1 must be true if B1/W1 false. Let's check K1 maybe.\n\nK1: \"EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE.\" Top statement of each box: B1, W1, K1 are top statements (only statements). \"Top statement of each box is true\" means B1∧W1∧K1 true. But B1 false, so first disjunct false. \"The White box is true\" means W1 true. So K1 = (B1∧W1∧K1) OR W1. Since W1 false? If W1 false, K1 = (false) OR false = false. Then all statements false, violates at least one true. Therefore W1 cannot be false if K1 is false? Wait K1 depends on W1. Let's solve self-reference.\n\nB1 = F always.\n\nW1 = \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" Need define. If no box with two false statements, W1 false. But maybe in context \"box with two false statements\" might refer to a box among three that has two false statements? Not possible as counts one. However self-referential puzzle may involve \"box with two false statements\" as an object that may exist if there are two false statements in one box? But no. Could be a mistranslation: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" means among the three boxes, there is a box that has exactly two false statements? But each box has only one statement, impossible. Unless \"statement\" in W1 refers to statements displayed on boxes collectively? Hmm.\n\nBut maybe the puzzle originally has each box containing multiple statements, but user says statement counts BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1. So W1 refers to \"the box with two false statements\" maybe means \"the box whose statement is false and...\"? No.\n\nLet's examine K1 self-reference: K1 = (B1∧W1∧K1) OR W1.\nUsing Boolean equation: K1 = W1 OR (B1∧W1∧K1). Since B1=F, B1∧...=F. Thus K1 = W1. So K1 truth equals W1 truth. Therefore W1 and K1 must have same truth value. Combined with at least one true (B1 false) => W1=K1=T. Thus W1 and K1 true. Therefore final gems must make W1 true: \"the box with two false statements has the gems\" = true. But if W1 true, then there must exist a box with two false statements, contradiction with one statement per box? Maybe m