ntioned in B1? Hmm no.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning:\n1. Analyze B1: It is a conjunction of two claims: \"White and Black are empty\" and \"White contains gems\" (assuming \"next to Blue\" = White, the only adjacent box). These two claims cannot both be true because exactly one box contains gems: if White contains gems, White is not empty; if White is empty, it cannot contain gems. Therefore B1 is false in every possible gem location.\nNeed mention \"even if 'next to' were interpreted as either White or Black, B1 would require another box nonempty while claiming both other boxes empty, impossible.\" Actually if \"next to Blue\" includes Black (circular), B1 = W empty ∧ K empty ∧ (W gems ∨ K gems), also impossible. So B1 false always.\n\n2. K1: \"Either the top statement of each box is true or the White box is true.\" Since first part includes B1 true, and B1 is always false, the first disjunct is false. Hence K1 is true exactly when the White box is true (contains the gems / is the true box). Thus K1 = White_gems.\n\n3. Need at least one all-true box. Since B1 false, Blue cannot be all-true. Since K1 = White_gems, Black is all-true iff White has gems. Therefore gems must be in White to satisfy at least one all-true. This also makes K1 true.\n\n4. With gems in White, evaluate W1: \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" Each box has one statement. Blue statement B1 is false; White statement W1 is to be determined; Black statement K1 is true. There is no box that has two false statements (any false statement count is at most one per box, and Blue/White have one each if W1 false; Black has zero). Therefore W1's claim is false: W1=F.\n\n5. Check all-true/all-false: Blue false => Blue all-false; White false => White all-false; Black true => Black all-true. Rules satisfied. Exactly one gem White.\n\nPotential issue: At step 4, if W1 true, then statements: B1=F, W1=T, K1=T -> only one false statement B1, still no box with two false statements, so W1 cannot be true. So W1 must be false. This is a self-consistency check: Suppose W1=true => there must be a box with two false statements; but B1 false, W1 true, K1 true => only one false statement total, so no box has two false statements, contradiction. Hence W1=false. Actually if W1=false, statements: B1=F,W1=F,K1=T => two false statements total (B1,W1) but they belong to different boxes, no single box has two false statements, so W1's claim false consistent. Good.\n\nNeed address \"box with two false statements\" as each box has one statement; no box can have two false statements. So W1 false.\n\nNeed final line exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T\n\nLet's ensure not over-explain \"White box is true\" = contains gems: In final reasoning, we need justify. Since first disjunct impossible due B1 false, K1 reduces to \"White box is true.\" The phrase \"White box is true\" in context of rules likely means White box's statements all true? But if so no solution. We should mention \"Here 'White box is true' must be read as 'the White box is the true/gem box' (otherwise the puzzle would have no consistent all-true box); equivalently, the only way K1 can be true is if White contains the gems.\" But is that acceptable? The user might expect \"White box is true\" = W1 true? The puzzle statement from game might define \"true box\" as \"contains gems\"? Wait rules: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\" They don't define \"box is true\" as contains gems; they define statements true. But K1's wording \"OR THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" might mean \"the White box is [all] true\" i.e., all statements on White true. However we found contradiction. Maybe the game actually uses \"true\" to mean \"genuine/contains gems\"? In many puzzle games, a \"true box\" might mean \"the box that is telling the truth\" (all statements true), not contains gems. But K1's first part already covers all top statements true; second part \"White box is true\" would be redundant if same. The fact it's not redundant suggests \"White box is true\" means something else, likely \"White box contains the gems\" (the statement 'White has gems' is true). But a box containing gems isn't \"true\"; however in natural language, \"the White box is the true one\" could mean \"the White box is the real one containing gems.\" The statement says \"THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" not \"THE WHITE STATEMENT IS TRUE\" or \"THE WHITE BOX IS ALL-TRUE\"; so I think okay to interpret as \"White box is the true container of gems.\"\n\nLet's verify if there is any alternative model if \"White box is true\" = W1 true but with W1 true somehow possible if \"box with two false statements\" refers to the two false statements total and box = White? Suppose K1=W1, B1 false. Need W1 true for all-true. W1 true means \"The box with two false statements has gems.\" The two false statements are B1 and K1? But K1=W1=true, so not false. Only B1 false. So no. Thus indeed no solution. So intended must be White_gems.\n\nLet's craft reasoning step-by-step with explicit statement IDs.\n\nNeed be concise but deductive. Desired oververbosity 5. We'll write step-by-step.\n\nPotential nuance: \"the other two boxes relative to the Blue box are empty\" = White and Black empty. \"A box next to the Blue box contains the gems\" = White contains gems (only adjacent). Thus B1 = (White empty ∧ Black empty) ∧ White gems. Since exactly one gem, if White gems then White not empty, so conjunction false; if White not gems, then White gems false; B1 false in all cases. We can state B1 always false.\n\nActually if gem=Black, White empty true, Black empty false, White gems false => B1 false. If gem=Blue, White empty true, Black empty true, White gems false => B1 false. If gem=White, White empty false, Black empty true, White gems true => B1 false. Yes.\n\nThen K1: first disjunct \"top statement of each box is true\" = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1. Since B1=false, first disjunct false. Therefore K1 ↔ \"White box is true\". If \"White box is true\" means White has gems, K1 ↔ G_White. To have at least one all-true box, Black must be all-true because Blue cannot (B1 false) and White cannot if W1? Wait White could be all-true if W1 true; but W1 true would require a box with two false statements, which may be impossible; but we haven't determined W1 yet. Let's order: We know B1 false, so Blue not all-true. So all-true must be White or Black. If White all-true, W1 true. But W1 true would require a box with two false statements. With B1 false and K1=White_gems? Let's analyze:\nAssume White all-true => W1=true, gem=White (since White all-true doesn't imply gem? White box all-true means W1 true; \"box with two false statements has gems\" would imply gem in some box with two false statements, not necessarily White. But exactly one gem; W1 true doesn't directly say White has gems unless the box with two false statements is White. Could White have two false statements? No, one statement. So W1 cannot be true. Therefore White cannot be all-true. Thus all-true must be Black => K1=true => White_gems=true. This proof avoids relying on W1 false beforehand.\n\nLet's do:\n- B1 always false => Blue not all-true.\n- W1 cannot be true: If W1 were true, \"the box with two false statements has the gems\" would require a single box containing two false statements. But each box has exactly one statement, so no box can contain two false statements. Thus W1 is false. Therefore White is not all-true.\n- Since at least one box all-true, it must be Black: K1=true.\n- K1 = first disjunct (B1∧W1∧K1) OR White_box_true. Since B1=false, first disjunct false, so K1=true implies White_box_true = true. Thus White box contains gems (or White box is the true/gem box).\n- Gem=White.\n- With gem=White, B1 false as evaluated; W1 false as argued; K1 true because White has gems (second disjunct true).\n\nNeed ensure W1 cannot be true: \"each box has exactly one statement, so no box with two false statements exists; hence W1's existential claim is false.\" This assumes \"two false statements\" means two statements on same box. Good.\n\nBut wait, if W1 true would require gem in a box with two false statements; since no such box, W1 false. This is independent of gem location. So White all-true impossible. Thus all-true must be Black. Then K1 true -> White_box_true -> White has gems. Great.\n\nPotential issue: If K1 true because first disjunct true? First disjunct B1∧W1∧K1 includes B1 true, but B1 always false, so first disjunct false. Thus K1 true only if White_box_true true. So White_box_true = White has gems. Yes.\n\nThen final.\n\nLet's prepare final response with reasoning and final line exactly.", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. B1 says: “White and Black are empty, AND a box next to Blue (White) contains the gems.”  \n   These two claims cannot both be true, because exactly one box has gems: if White has the gems, White is not empty. Therefore **B1 is always false**.\n\n2. K1 says: “Either the top statement of each box is true, or the White box is true.”  \n   Since B1 is always false, the first option “top statement of each box is true” is impossible.  \n   So K1 can only be true if the White box is true, i.e. the White box contains the gems.\n\n3. W1 says: “The box with two false statements has the gems.”  \n   But each box has exactly one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Therefore **W1 is false**.\n\n4. At least one box must be all-true. Blue is not all-true because B1 is false. White is not all-true because W1 is false. Therefore Black must be all-true, so **K1 is true**.\n\n5. Since K1 is true and the first option was impossible, the White box must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
