wo false statements total (W1,K1), but no box with two false statements on it; W1 false. G=WHITE solution B1=T,W1=F,K1=T has one false statement total. Both valid. So non-unique.\n\nWhat if W1 is interpreted as \"The box with two false statements [total among all statements] has the gems\" meaning if total false statements=2, then gems in the true box; if not, false. This is D. Two solutions: D solution 1 has total false=1 -> W1 false; D solution 2 has total false=2 -> W1 true. Both valid.\n\nWhat if W1's truth value itself being false contributes to total false count, so solution 1 has one false statement; solution 2 has two false statements. Both.\n\nCould there be a requirement \"There is exactly one all-true box and exactly one all-false box, and the remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix\" but with one statement per box, the remaining cannot be mix, so exactly one true and exactly one false impossible because third would be either true or false leading two true or two false. Unless the remaining box has **no statements**? No, each box has one statement. So no.\n\nCould it be that each box's \"truth value\" as a box is not simply its statement truth, but \"all statements true/false/mix\"; with one statement, all-true = statement true, all-false = statement false, mix impossible. So exactly one all-true and one all-false cannot coexist with three boxes each one statement because third would be all-true/all-false, making either two all-true or two all-false. Therefore the rule cannot require exactly one each; \"at least one each\" is necessary.\n\nGiven the puzzle as stated has multiple valid solutions under natural interpretations, maybe the correct response should be to point out ambiguity/non-uniqueness? But user asks for final answer in exact format. They likely expect a unique solution from intended interpretation. We need decide intended solution.\n\nLet's examine the exact wording: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" — In a setup with three statements total, the phrase \"the box with two false statements\" most naturally refers to a box that has two false statements **displayed on it**. Since each box displays one statement, there is no such box, so W1 is simply false. This is the most literal interpretation. Under literal interpretation, two solutions exist: G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F and G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T. Therefore the puzzle is not uniquely solvable as written. But the user says \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" — maybe they think W1 false always plus B1 existential is unique but overlooked second solution? Let's verify second solution G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T under literal W1=false:\n- Gem in WHITE.\n- B1: \"A box next to White is true.\" Neighbors: BLUE (B1=T) and BLACK (K1=T). Both true, so at least one true => B1=T. Correct.\n- W1: \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" There is no box with two false statements (each box one statement), so statement false. Correct.\n- K1: \"The gems are in a box that is false.\" Gem in WHITE; WHITE's statement W1=F, so WHITE box is false. Therefore K1=T. Correct.\n- Rules: Exactly one gem (White). At least one all-true: BLUE (B1=T), BLACK (K1=T). At least one all-false: WHITE (W1=F). Remaining after choosing BLUE true and WHITE false is BLACK true, allowed. All consistent.\nSo literal puzzle has at least two solutions. Unless \"A box next to the white box is true\" means exactly one neighbor true, G=WHITE solution invalid because two neighbors true; then unique G=BLUE. But \"A box\" not exactly one. Could the phrase \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" with \"A\" singular in a puzzle context be intended as \"Exactly one box next to White is true\"? Many puzzle statements use \"A box ... is true\" to mean \"There exists a box ... that is true\", not exactly one. If they wanted exactly one, they'd say \"Exactly one of the boxes next to the white box is true.\" The uppercase \"A\" might be emphasis, not \"exactly one\".\n\nWhat if \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" means \"The box next to the white box [on the side away from this box?] is true\"? For Blue statement, the box next to White could be Black? No, \"a box\" indefinite.\n\nLet's search memory: I recall a logic puzzle from Blue Prince where solution is \"White box has gems; Blue true; White false; Black true.\" Maybe because B1 refers to Black box being true (the box next to White on the right), W1 false because no box has two false statements, K1 true because White is false. This is elegant: The true statement B1 is true because Black is true; Black is true because White is false; White is false because no box has two false statements. This forms a chain and avoids self-reference of B1 being true because itself true; it uses Black true. The other solution G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F has B1 true because Blue itself is true (self-supporting) and K1 false because Blue true, which is also consistent but relies on B1 self-reference. Some puzzle systems might disallow self-supporting truth? They allow self-reference explicitly (\"including self- and cross-references\"), so self-support should be allowed. But maybe there is an implicit rule that statements must not be **self-justifying**? The statement B1 truth due to itself being true is a self-reference loop (B1 = B1 OR K1 with K1=F has both B1=T and B1=F as fixed points; B1=T is self-justifying). In some truth-value semantics, self-referential statements without grounding may be considered undefined/untrue? The rules say \"A statement's truth value is determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references)\" — this allows self-reference but doesn't specify least fixed point. However, many logic puzzle solvers assume statements can be self-referential but truth values must be consistent; both B1=T and B1=F are consistent when K1=F because B1 = B1 OR F is tautology. So both are valid classical fixed points. If they require **well-founded** truth (every statement's truth derivable from non-self-referential facts), then B1=T when K1=F is ungrounded (it asserts its own truth), while B1=F is also ungrounded? Actually B1=F with K1=F: Statement \"A box next to White is true\" asserts at least one of B1,K1 true. If B1=F and K1=F, then the statement is false; so B1=F is grounded by its own falsity? It's a liar-like? B1 = (B1 OR F) = B1, so both T and F are fixed points, neither grounded in non-self facts. In Kripke semantics, ungrounded statements may be undefined. If undefined not allowed, then K1=F and B1 unconstrained might be invalid because B1 lacks determinate truth? But rules say truth values every statement, so must be T/F. They might allow self-support? The G=WHITE solution has B1=T grounded by K1=T (Black true), K1=T grounded by W1=F (White false), W1=F grounded by no box with two false statements (fact about statement counts). That is fully grounded! The G=BLUE solution has B1=T grounded by itself? B1=T because B1 true (self), K1=F grounded by B1=T (Blue true), W1=F grounded by no box two false. So B1 is ungrounded/self-justifying. If the puzzle implicitly requires every statement's truth be grounded (not circular) or at least not rely on itself for its own truth, then G=BLUE solution invalid, leaving unique G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T. This might be the intended uniqueness! The rules mention \"including self- and cross-references\" but maybe they allow self-reference but not **self-support**? Actually they explicitly include self-references, so B1 referring to itself is allowed; but a statement's truth value must be determined by actual state, and if B1=T solely because B1=T, that's circular and may be considered not determined. However, in classical fixed-point logic, circularity is okay if consistent. But some puzzles disallow \"ungrounded\" statements as undefined, requiring each statement's truth be **forced** by facts. The G=BLUE solution has B1 unforced: B1 could be false with K1=F? Let's check if B1=F,K1=F,G=BLUE possible under literal W1=F:\nG=BLUE => K1 = (BLUE false) = (B1=F). If B1=F, then K1=T, contradiction. So actually B1=F,K1=F not possible for G=BLUE because K1 would be T. Wait K1=(gem in false box). If G=BLUE and B1=F, then K1=T. So K1=F requires B1=T. Thus B1=T is forced by K1=F and G=BLUE? Let's solve: G=BLUE, K1=F means \"gems are in a box that is false\" is false, so the gem box BLUE must not be false => BLUE true => B1=T. So B1=T is actually grounded by K1=F and gem location! K1=F is grounded by B1=T? Circular: K1=F because B1=T; B1=T because K1=F and?? B1 statement: at least one neighbor true. If B1=T, then Blue neighbor true, so B1 true. But B1=T also grounded by itself (since Blue is neighbor). But it's also consistent with K1=F. There is a circular loop B1<=>K1? K1=F iff B1=T; B1=T iff (B1=T OR K1=T). Given K1=F, B1=T satisfies because B1=T; B1=F would require K1=T but K1=F, so B1=F inconsistent. Thus B1=T is forced: If B1=F then K1=T (by K1=¬B1), then B1=T (by B1=B1∨K1), contradiction. Therefore B1=T is the only solution for G=BLUE; it is grounded by contradiction eliminating B1=F, not purely self-support. So G=BLUE solution is actually forced by equations, not ungrounded? Let's see dependencies: To determine B1, we considered K1=¬B1 and B1=B1∨K1. Substituting K1 gives B1 = B1 ∨ ¬B1 = T. Ah! For G=BLUE, K1=¬B1, so B1 = B1 ∨ ¬B1 = True tautologically. Wait B1 = B1 OR K1 = B1 OR ¬B1 = True always. So B1 is logically forced to be True by the equations! Indeed for G=BLUE, B1 must be T regardless of self because K1=¬B1 makes B1 = B1 ∨ ¬B1 = T. So B1=T is not ungrounded; it's a tautological consequence of K1's definition. Similarly K1=F follows. Thus G=BLUE solution is fully logically forced and grounded by the contradiction if B1=F. So self-support not an issue; it's forced by equations. Therefore G=BLUE is a valid deterministic solution under literal W1=F, not ungrounded.\n\nSo G=BLUE is robust: B1=T forced, K1=F forced, W1=F. G=WHITE solution B1=T,W1=F,K1=T also forced? G=WHITE => K1=¬W1= T (W1=F), then B1=B1∨T=T. So B1=T forced. Both deterministic. So two solutions.\n\nCould there be a constraint \"EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains gems; the other two are empty\" plus \"AT LEAST ONE box all true; AT LEAST ONE box all false; remaining may be all-true/all-false/mix\" — both solutions valid. Thus puzzle as written under literal W1=F is not uniquely solvable. But user says \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" maybe they expect us to use all rules including W1's content not literal.\n\nLet's try to find a unique solution by considering W1 not literal but as a statement that itself could be true/false and affects gem location; solve fully self-referentially with W1 meaning \"the box with two false statements\" = **the box that has two false statements among all statements in the puzzle, i.e., a box that is false and has a false neighbor?** We tried many. None unique except maybe W1 = \"the unique box with exactly two false statements among all three statements total has gems\" = D, which gave two solutions. What if W1 = \"the box with two false statements has the gems\" is interpreted as a **material implication**: If there is a box with two false statements, then it has gems; if no such box, statement is vacuously true? In logic, universal/conditional statements about nonexistent entities can be vacuously true. But the statement is \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" using definite description; not conditional. If treated as \"For any box X, if X has two false statements then X has gems\" (universal), then if no box has two false statements, W1 would be vacuously true, not false! Ah! This is another possible interpretation: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" could be read as a definite description (unique box) or as universal \"Any box with two false statements has the gems\". In natural language, \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" presupposes existence and uniqueness; if none, false. But in logic puzzles, sometimes definite descriptions with no referent are false. However, if treated as universal conditional, W1=true when no box has two false statements. That could yield unique solution! Let's explore.\n\nInterpretation E: W1 = \"For every box, if it has two false statements (on it), then it contains gems.\" Since each box has one statement, no box has two false statements, so the antecedent is false for all boxes, making W1 vacuously TRUE. Then W1=T always (because no box has two false statements). Let's solve with B1 existential and K1=(gem false), G≠BLACK.\n\nW1=T.\nCase G=BLUE: K1=¬B1. B1 equation B1=B1∨K1. If B1=T => K1=F ok. If B1=F => K1=T => B1=T contradiction. So B1=T,K1=F,W1=T. At least one T (B1,W1), at least one F (K1) valid. G=BLUE solution.\nCase G=WHITE: K1=¬W1 = F (since W1=T). B1 equation: K1=F => B1 any? B1 = B1∨F = B1, both possible. Need at least one all-false: K1=F only; need at least one T: W1=T, so ok. So B1 can be T or F? Check B1=F,W1=T,K1=F: B1 statement \"a box next to White is true\" — neighbors Blue B1=F and Black K1=F => both false, so B1=F correct. Valid. B1=T,W1=T,K1=F: B1 statement true because Blue itself true, valid. So two G=WHITE solutions.\nCase G=BLACK impossible.\nTotal three solutions: G=BLUE B1=T,W1=T,K1=F; G=WHITE B1=T,W1=T,K1=F; G=WHITE B1=F,W1=T,K1=F. Not unique.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"There exists a box with two false statements and it has gems\" (existential conditional): Since no box has two false statements, existential false => W1=F always, same as literal false -> two solutions.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" as **unique existence + gems**, false if none. We did.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"The box with two false statements\" refers to **a box that has exactly two false statements among the three statements total associated with it, i.e., the box itself is false and one of its neighbors is false** (variant 3) gave W1=false always effectively -> two solutions.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"The box with two false statements\" refers to **a box that has exactly two false statements among the three statements displayed on the three boxes, not necessarily on itself** i.e., if exactly two statements are false, then the remaining true statement's box is \"the box with two false statements\" (D). D gave two solutions.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" means **among the three boxes, the one that is false and has a false neighbor** (own false + at least one false neighbor) gave W1=false always -> two solutions.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"The box with two false statements\" refers to **the box that has exactly two false statements among all statements that are about that box**? Let's attempt count statements about each box:\n- Statements about Blue box: B1 itself? B1 about boxes next to White includes Blue and Black's truth, so B1 is about Blue (and Black). K1 could be about Blue if gems in Blue: \"gems are in a box that is false\" about gem box. W1 maybe about box with two false statements if Blue has two false statements.\nNot well-defined.\n\nCould there be a unique solution if we use **all three statements as simultaneous equations** with W1 = \"the box with two false statements has gems\" where \"two false statements\" refers to **exactly two of the three statements total are false**, and the box is the one whose statement is true; but also require that W1's truth value is part of the world, and the statement itself refers to \"the box with two false statements\" which if W1=true means exactly two false statements among B1,K1? Let's solve D exactly: W1 = (Tcount==1 AND G in true box). We found two solutions: S1 G=W,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T (Tcount=2) and S2 G=W,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F (Tcount=1). In S1, W1=false because Tcount=2. In S2, W1=true because Tcount=1 and G in W. Both valid. But wait S1 has gems in WHITE and W1=false, so WHITE box is false; K1=true because gems in false box; B1=true because K1 true. This satisfies all. S2 has gems in WHITE and W1=true, so WHITE box true; K1=false because gems not in false box; B1=false because no neighbor true (B1,F,K1,F). Both valid. So D non-unique.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"the box with two false statements has gems\" and **there must be exactly one box with two false statements** i.e., exactly one true statement exists, so Tcount=1; then W1 = (Tcount==1 ∧ G in true box). This is D but with Tcount==1 as a global constraint? Actually if W1 statement true, then Tcount=1; if W1 statement false, Tcount could be 2 or 3 or 0? But the phrase \"the box with two false statements\" might presuppose there are exactly two false statements; if not, W1 false. That's D. It allowed S1 with Tcount=2. If we impose a global rule that **exactly two statements are false** (not stated), then S1 eliminated, unique S2 G=W,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F. Could the rule \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" plus one statement per box implies total false statements must be either 1 or 2. It doesn't force 2. But maybe combined with W1's content \"the box with two false statements\" implies there must be two false statements for W1 to be true; but W1 can be false. No.\n\nLet's consider if any solution has exactly two false statements under literal W1=false? G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F has two false statements (W1,K1), so there are two false statements but no box with two false statements. W1=false because no single box has two false statements, but there are two false statements total. G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T has one false statement. If the puzzle intended \"the box with two false statements\" as \"there are two false statements in total; the box [that is true] has gems\", then G=BLUE would make W1 true (as D) but we saw D makes G=BLUE inconsistent because W1 true would change Tcount to 2? Wait in G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F, if W1 were true instead of false, then Tcount would be 2 (B1,W1 true), so there would not be two false statements total (only K1 false), so W1 would not be describing a state of two false statements; hence W1 cannot be true. Thus G=BLUE invalid under D. So D unique solutions are G=WHITE only. One with W1 true (Tcount=1,two false statements) and one with W1 false (Tcount=2, one false statement). If we additionally require the statement W1 itself to be true? Not stated. But maybe \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE\" is satisfied in S1 by Blue/Black, S2 by White; both.\n\nCould the puzzle be uniquely solvable if we require **exactly one all-true box and exactly one all-false box** plus allow the third to be mix? With one statement per box, no mix, so no solution as noted. What if a box with one statement can be considered \"mix\" if its statement is self-referential undefined? No.\n\nWait, the rule says \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" It doesn't say each box must be one of these; a box with one statement is either all-true or all-false, not mix. So with three boxes, if exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false, the third must be mix, impossible. Therefore exact-one-each cannot be intended. So at least-one each is correct.\n\nGiven all this, the only way to get unique under one-statement boxes is to interpret B1 as exactly-one neighbor true (or specific right box) and W1 as false always, yielding G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F. Or interpret B1 existential and W1 as some definition that eliminates S1/S2? Let's search for W1 definition that yields unique G=WHITE,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F maybe with B1 existential? D+require W1 true would do, but no rule. What about W1 = \"the box with two false statements has gems\" where \"two false statements\" means **the two false statements are the statements on the other two boxes**, i.e., if the other two statements are false, then this box has gems. For W1 to be true, the other two statements (relative to White) must be false and gems White. That's B (White neighbors false) yielded G=WHITE,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F as one solution, but also G=BLUE and G=WHITE with W1=false. If we require W1 true? Not stated. If we require at least one all-true and at least one all-false, B solutions:\n1 G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F (W1 false because B1 true so not both neighbors false)\n2 G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T (W1 false because B1 true)\n3 G=WHITE,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F (W1 true because both neighbors false and gems White)\nAll valid. Not unique.\n\nWhat if W1 = \"the box with two false statements has gems\" means **the box that has exactly two false statements among its own statement and the statements of the boxes adjacent to it** (C) gave three solutions including G=BLUE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=F where White and Black each have count 2 (W1 false because multiple); G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T where no count 2 (W1 false); G=WHITE,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F where White count 2 (W1 true). All valid.\n\nThus no W1 definition with B1 existential yields unique except maybe if W1 definition eliminates G=BLUE and one G=WHITE. D eliminated G=BLUE but left two G=WHITE. To eliminate S1 G=WHITE,B1=T,W1=F,K1=T, need W1 = something that would be true in S1 but is false? Actually S1 has W1=false; if W1 definition made W1=true in S1, then S1 invalid because W1 would be T but assigned F. What definition would make W1 true in S1? S1 has B1=T,K1=T,W1=F, gems White. There are two true statements, one false statement. No box with two false statements, no unique true box. So most definitions W1 false. To make W1 true, need two false statements somewhere. S1 has only one false statement, so any definition requiring two false statements (on a box, neighbors, total) would be false. So S1 remains valid. Thus unique requires eliminating S1 by some other rule, e.g., B1 exactly-one neighbor true (since S1 has two true neighbors). That seems most plausible.\n\nLet's test B1=exactly one neighbor true with various W1 definitions:\nB1 = (B1 XOR K1). We found K1 must F, B1 arbitrary? Actually equation B1 = B1 XOR K1 => K1=F; B1 a