gems in a box that is false\" = \"BLACK false\" = ¬K1, impossible. So gem not BLACK.\nThus gem must be WHITE.\nThis is acceptable.\n\nNeed be concise but deductive. Final line exact.", "content": "1. Each box has exactly one statement, so no box has two false statements. Therefore W1 (“The box with two false statements has the gems”) is **FALSE**. Thus the WHITE box is a false box.\n2. If the gems are in WHITE, then K1 (“The gems are in a box that is false”) is **TRUE**, because WHITE is false.\n3. Since K1 is true, the BLACK box is true. Therefore B1 (“A box next to the white box is true”) is **TRUE**, because BLACK is next to WHITE and is true.\n4. This gives the consistent truth assignment: B1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = T, with gems in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}
