ndent fact. \"Determined by actual state of the world\" means the gem location plus the content of statements (which are fixed) should determine truth values; self-referential loops can be resolved if there is unique fixed point? Not necessarily. However many puzzle games avoid ungrounded \"truth-teller\" loops by declaring them invalid because no determinate truth value. So G=Blue invalid because B1's truth is not determined independently; it's a self-supporting assumption.\nThe prompt says \"A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\" This is almost a definition of fixed point. It doesn't say \"well-founded.\" But \"determined\" might imply no free choice: given gem location, the truth values are forced. In G=Blue, truth values are forced (unique). So still.\n\nLet's see if G=Blue truth values are forced without circularity if we use equations:\nGiven G=Blue, W=false.\nK = \"gems in false box\" = \"Blue is false\" = ¬B.\nB = \"some neighbor true\" = B ∨ K = B ∨ ¬B = True.\nThus B=True, K=False. This derivation uses B = B∨¬B = True, which is a tautology: \"B or not B\" always true. So B is forced to true by pure logic regardless of external facts! Interesting: The equation B = B∨K with K=¬B reduces to B=True because B∨¬B is always true. This is not self-support; it's a logical theorem. Actually B = B∨¬B = True, so B is true necessarily. Then K=false. The truth of B is grounded in the logical impossibility of B=false (since if B=false then K=true, making B=true). This is a valid logical deduction, not an arbitrary self-loop. So G=Blue is well-determined by the equations: B must be true because assuming false leads contradiction. This is a standard fixed-point derivation. So ungroundedness not an issue? It is still circular in terms of dependency: B's truth relies on K's truth value, which relies on B's falsity; but the equation has unique solution. Many logics allow this: e.g., \"This statement is false or 1=1\" is true because if false then \"false or true\" true; if true then true. It's a valid statement true by logic. So G=Blue is fully logically determined.\n\nThus two solutions remain.\n\nLet's examine if G=White also logically determined:\nGiven G=White, W=false.\nK = \"White false\" = True.\nB = B∨True = True.\nSo unique.\n\nBoth are logically valid. The puzzle has two solutions. Could it be that \"EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems\" plus \"AT LEAST ONE box all true, AT LEAST ONE all false, remaining may be all-true/all-false/mix\" plus \"A statement's truth value determined by actual state\" actually implies there are exactly two possible worlds, so not uniquely solvable. The user may be testing if model notices ambiguity? But they demand final exact format; maybe they expect one and will grade exact? We need deliver best answer.\n\nMaybe there is a subtle issue: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" could mean \"A box next to the white box is [the] true [box]\" where \"true\" means \"the box that contains gems\"? No, rules define true box by statements, but in puzzle maybe \"true\" could mean \"genuine\" i.e., contains gems? No, rules: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" and \"A statement's truth value...\" so true refers to statements.\n\nCould \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" mean \"A box next to the white box is [a box that has all true statements]\" yes.\n\nWhat if \"A box next to the white box is true\" in a line of three could mean \"There is a box adjacent to White, and that box is true\" but because White has two adjacent boxes, the statement might be false if both are false, true if at least one true. Standard.\n\nLet's consider if \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" could be interpreted as \"The box with two false statements [among the three statements] has the gems\" i.e., there are two false statements total, and the box that **c