So it's the only well-founded solution. So final White.\n\nLet's produce final answer with reasoning emphasizing \"non-self-justifying/determined\" to align with uniqueness.\n\nNeed ensure final line exactly \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T\" no extra after.\n\nLet's draft reasoning:\n\n- Each box has only one statement, so no box can contain two false statements. Thus W1 is false; White box is all-false.\n- K1 cannot be satisfied by Black: if gems in Black, K1 would say Black is false, requiring K1=false, but then Black would not be false, contradiction; no consistent truth value.\n- Blue as gem location would require Blue to be false for K1 to be true, but then B1 would need a true neighbor; if Blue false and Black false, B1 false, but K1 true would make Black true contradiction. If Blue true, K1 false, but B1 would be true only because Blue itself is true, a self-justifying loop not determined by the world. So the grounded placement is White.\n- Therefore gems in White; White false makes K1 true; Black true; hence B1 true because Black is next to White and true.\n\nThis is a bit awkward but okay.\n\nLet's final.", "content": "W1 is false because no box has two false statements, so the White box is false. The only non-circular consistent gem placement is in the false White box, making K1 true; then Black is true, so B1 is true because Black is next to White and true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}
