uld mean false, which is counterintuitive and not stated. So unlikely.\n\nWhat if K1 first clause \"blue box is white\" = \"blue box is [the same truth as] White box\" = b=w always true; then K1=G≠W, solutions G=W,T,T,F and G=K,F,F,T. Which one would be eliminated by \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" if \"box is true\" = contains gems? Already nonunique.\n\nThus final G=WHITE,T,T,F is best.\n\nLet's prepare final answer with a note about interpretation? The final answer must be exact one-line at very end. The reasoning can include \"I read K1's 'the blue box is white' as the game's way of saying the Blue box is all-true\" to justify. But if I mention that, and user didn't intend, might seem odd. Maybe better to phrase \"K1 is false in the unique consistent solution; its first clause would require Blue to be true, but its second clause fails because the gems are in White, not in a box next to White.\" That implicitly treats first clause as Blue true.\n\nLet's write step-by-step:\n1. Let b,w,k.\n2. W1 says \"Blue true and B1 true\" => w = b ∧ b = b.\n3. Thus b=w. Since B1 and W1 are both the same truth value, and at least one statement true and at least one false, k must be ¬b.\n4. K1 = \"Blue box is true AND gems are next to White\". If b=false then k would require Blue true to be true, contradiction; so b=true.\n5. Hence w=true, k=false. Since k=false despite Blue true, the second conjunct must be false: the gems are not next to White. The only non-adjacent box to White is White itself (middle box). So gem=White.\n6. Verify B1 true because White box next to Black is true; W1 true; K1 false.\n\nNeed be careful in step 4: If b=false, K1 first clause false => k=false, but k=¬b=true, contradiction. So b cannot false; b=true. Then k=false. Since b=true, K1's truth depends on second clause; k=false => second clause false => gems not next to White => gem=White. Good.\n\nBut step 4 assumes K1 first clause = b. I can state \"K1's first clause is 'the Blue box is true' (the game's white/true equivalence)\" or simply \"Let k = b ∧ (gems not in White)\". In reasoning, I'll write: \"The Black statement, interpreted as in the game, is: (Blue box is true) AND (gems are in a box next to the White box).\" This is transparent.\n\nWill final line exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nLet's ensure no extra text after final line. Final answer must be last line exactly.\n\nLet's produce final.", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says “Blue box is true AND \\(B1\\) is true.” Since Blue has only \\(B1\\), this is equivalent to \\(B1 \\land B1 = B1\\).  \n  So \\(W1 = B1\\).\n\n- Therefore Blue and White have the same truth value. Since at least one box must be all-true and at least one must be all-false, Black must have the opposite truth value from Blue/White.\n\n- \\(K1\\) effectively says “Blue box is true AND the gems are next to White.”  \n  If Blue/White were false, then \\(K1\\) would have to be true, but its first part “Blue box is true” would be false, so \\(K1\\) could not be true. Contradiction.  \n  Thus Blue and White are true, and Black is false.\n\n- Since \\(K1\\) is false but its first part “Blue box is true” is true, its second part must be false: the gems are **not** in a box next to White. The only box not next to White is White itself.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
