cal arrangement? The boxes are left/middle/right horizontally, but statements listed vertically: BLUE box B1, WHITE box W1, BLACK box K1. The statement above W1 in list is B1. So W1 = B1 true. Yes.\n\nBut could \"above this one\" refer to \"the box above\" if boxes stacked vertically? It says BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right, so not above. Thus B1.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise, then final line exactly. Need include truth values every statement only B1 W1 K1. Need decide final: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nLet's ensure no other possible interpretations yield different unique solution? Need be robust. Let's explore because user asks think step by step. We need solve logically with rules. Need maybe mention K1 fixed point. Need be careful with \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\" Could mean \"the two boxes other than the white box are empty\" i.e., Blue and Black empty. If gem is Blue, Blue not empty => K1 false. If gem Black, Black not empty => K1 false. If gem White, both Blue and Black empty => first part true; second part \"this box has at least one false statement\" = black box has at least one false statement, i.e., K1 false. So K1 would be true ∧ K1 false = contradiction, so gem not White. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix\" in final reasoning? Since each box has one statement, no mix.\n\nLet's formulate step-by-step:\n\n1. Let b,w,k be truth values of B1,W1,K1.\n2. K1 says: (Blue empty ∧ Black empty) ∧ K1 is false. Since exactly one gem, Blue empty ∧ Black empty iff gem is White. Thus K1's truth condition is k = (g=W) ∧ ¬k. This has no solution if g=W; if g≠W, then k=false. So gem is not White and K1 is false.\n3. W1 says B1 is true, so w = b.\n4. At least one box has all statements true. Since K1 false, at least one of B1/W1 must be true; because they share truth value, B1 and W1 must both be true. Thus b=w=true, k=false.\n5. B1 true means the box containing gems has no false statements (all its statements true). All-true boxes are Blue (B1 true) and White (W1 true). Gem cannot be White (from K1), so it must be Blue. This also matches K1 false: Blue not empty, so first conjunct false.\n6. Final.\n\nNeed ensure step 5: \"all-true boxes are Blue and White\" because b=true,w=true,k=false. Gem cannot White, so Blue. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If B1 true interpreted existential, step 5 okay. But if someone argues B1 singular \"the box\" unique, our reasoning might conflict. Need maybe avoid saying \"All-true boxes are Blue and White; gem cannot White; therefore Blue\" because if B1 asserts \"the box with no false statements has gems\" and there are two all-true boxes, statement could be considered false under uniqueness. But we already established B1 must be true. Therefore intended reading must be \"the gem is in a box with no false statements\" (existential). In final reasoning, we can phrase: \"Since B1 is true, the gem is placed in one of the boxes whose statements are all true.\" This avoids definite uniqueness.\n\nNeed perhaps mention \"Because exactly one gem, 'the box with no false statements has the gems' means the gem-containing box is one of the all-true boxes.\" If multiple all-true boxes, the gem is in one of them; the definite article convention in puzzle: statement true iff the gem is in an all-true box. We can state that.\n\nLet's double-check with formal fixed point if B1 = gem box all-true:\n- k=false, g≠W.\n- b=w.\n- If g=B, b can be? B1 = (g=B⇒b) = b, tautology; w=b; k=false. At least one true => b=true. So g=B,b=T,w=T,k=F.\n- If g=K, B1 = (g=K⇒k) = (true⇒false)=false; w=false; k=false; all false violates at least one true. So g=K invalid.\n- If g=W, K1 no solution because k = true∧¬k impossible; also g=W invalid. So unique g=B.\nWorks.\n\nNeed final exactly one line: \"FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\" nothing after. User requested step by step then final line. We can include reasoning paragraphs and final line. The final line must be exact format. Need ensure no extra after final line. They said \"on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\" So final response should have reasoning then last line final. Need ensure final line is indeed last, no punctuation after. Example format only. We'll comply.\n\nLet's craft concise but deductive step-by-step.\n\nPotential nuance: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" and \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\" Since each box has exactly one statement, all true = statement true, all false = statement false. At least one true and one false => among b,w,k at least one T and one F. We used after k=false: b=w cannot be false because then all false; so b=w=true.\n\nNeed perhaps address W1 = \"statement above this one is true\" = B1. If \"above\" could be interpreted differently? The boxes are left/middle/right; text lists B1 then W1 then K1; statement above W1 is B1. Good.\n\nLet's include K1 fixed point explanation:\nK1 statement: \"The other two boxes relative to the white box are empty AND this box has at least one false statement.\" Since Black contains only K1, \"this box has at least one false statement\" means \"K1 is false.\" Also, Blue and Black both empty iff gems in White. So K1 = (gems in White) AND (K1 is false). If gems in White, that would require K1 true iff K1 false, impossible; therefore gems not in White. With gems not in White, the first conjunct is false, so K1 is false.\n\nWait: \"Blue and Black both empty iff gems in White\" yes because exactly one gem: if gem White, Blue empty and Black empty true; if gem not White, one of Blue/Black has gem so not both empty => false. Good.\n\nThen W1: w=b. Since k=false, to satisfy at least one true, b must be true (if b false then w false too). So b=w=true.\nThen B1 true: gem in all-true box. All-true boxes: Blue (B1 true) and White (W1 true); Black not all-true (K1 false). Gem cannot be White from K1, so