en w=false, all false, violates all-true. No solution. If uniqueness: b = (exactly one all-true ∧ that has gem). All-true count = b + w + k = b + b + 0 = 2b. Exactly one => 2b=1 impossible. No solution. Thus only existential.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"at least one all-true\" eliminates b=false.\n\nLet's consider if there is any way to satisfy at least one all-true with b=false via Black all-true? But K1 cannot be true due self-ref, so no. Thus b=true.\n\nLet's decide final reasoning structure:\n\n1. Analyze Black statement K1:\n   - Let E = \"Blue and Black are empty\" = White has gems.\n   - Let F = \"Black has at least one false statement\" = K1 is false (since K1 is Black's only statement).\n   - Thus K1 = E ∧ ¬K1.\n   - If K1 true, then true = E ∧ false = false, contradiction.\n   - Hence K1 false, and E must be false. Therefore White is empty (gems are in Blue or Black).\n\nActually E false means not(Blue empty and Black empty) = at least one of Blue/Black has gems. Since exactly one gem, White empty. Yes.\n\n2. Analyze W1:\n   - W1 says B1 is true, so W1 = B1.\n\n3. Determine B1:\n   - Suppose B1 false. Then W1 false, so Blue and White each have a false statement (their only statement false), and Black already has false K1. That would give no all-true box, contradicting rule at least one all-true.\n   - Therefore B1 true, W1 true.\n   - So Blue and White are all-true boxes; Black is all-false.\n\n4. Determine gem:\n   - B1 true means an all-true/no-false box has the gems.\n   - The all-true boxes are Blue and White; White is empty (from K1), so gems must be in Blue.\n   - Check: Blue has gems, B1 true; White empty but W1 true; Black K1 false; exactly one gem; at least one all-true (Blue, White); at least one all-false (Black). Consistent.\n\nNeed be careful in step 4 \"White is empty\" from K1. Yes.\n\nPotential issue: If White is all-true and empty, is it okay that an all-true box does not contain gems while B1 says \"The box with no false statements has the gems\"? If B1 is existential, yes because Blue all-true has gems. If final answer includes \"the all-true boxes are Blue and White; White is empty, so gems must be in Blue\" because B1 true requires \"an all-true box\" has gems. This implicitly says White being all-true empty is fine as long as at least one all-true box has gems. Good.\n\nLet's ensure final line exactly with semicolons and spaces:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNo extra text after. The final answer line should be very last. We can include reasoning before.\n\nLet's also consider if statement labels: BLUE=1 B1, WHITE=1 W1, BLACK=1 K1. Yes.\n\nLet's produce final answer with concise step by step.\n\nNeed maybe avoid saying \"the all-true boxes are Blue and White\" and \"B1 says an all-true box has gems\" because someone might object \"which one?\" We can phrase:\n\"Because B1 is true, the box containing the gems must be one of the boxes with no false statements. The boxes with no false statements are Blue and White; White is empty; hence it must be Blue.\"\nThis is clear.\n\nLet's maybe include a verification line:\n\"With gem=BLUE: B1 is true (Blue has no false statements and has gems), W1 true (B1 true), K1 false (Black has a false statement, but Blue is not empty, so the conjunction is false).\"\nActually K1 false because Blue is not empty, so first conjunct false; second conjunct true; conjunction false. Good.\n\nLet's final.", "content": "Let \\(K1\\) be Black’s only statement.  \n“This box has at least one false statement” means “\\(K1\\) is false.”  \nSo \\(K1\\) says: *Blue and Black are empty, and \\(K1\\) is false.*  \nThat forces \\(K1 = F\\), and therefore Blue and Black cannot both be empty. Hence White is empty.\n\nNow \\(W1\\) says “\\(B1\\) is true,” so \\(W1 = B1\\).  \nIf \\(B1\\) were false, then \\(W1\\) would also be false, meaning Blue and White would be all-false; with Black all-false, there would be no all-true box, violating the rules.  \nSo \\(B1 = T\\), hence \\(W1 = T\\).\n\nThus Blue and White are all-true boxes.  \nSince \\(B1\\) says an all-true/no-false box has the gems, and White is empty, the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
