EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\": If \"boxes next to white contain gems\" plural could mean \"the boxes next to white contain gems [together]\" i.e., gem among them. In logic puzzles, \"boxes ... contain gems\" with exactly one gem often means \"one of those boxes contains the gems\" because gems is plural but location singular? Actually \"contain gems\" could mean \"contain the gems\" = the gems are inside those boxes; since only one gem total, it means the gem is inside one of them. So yes.\n\nLet's answer.", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) mean “a box next to BLACK is true.” Since BLACK’s only neighbor is WHITE, \\(B1\\) is true exactly when WHITE’s statement \\(W1\\) is true.\n\n- \\(W1\\): “THIS BOX IS EMPTY” → true iff the gems are not in WHITE.\n- \\(K1\\): The two boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK; “the other two boxes” are also BLUE and BLACK. So \\(K1\\) covers both possible gem placements relative to WHITE and is always true.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then WHITE would be empty, making \\(W1\\) true, \\(B1\\) true, and \\(K1\\) true. Then all three boxes would be all-true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE. Then:\n- WHITE is not empty → \\(W1 = F\\)\n- WHITE is not true → \\(B1 = F\\)\n- BLUE and BLACK are empty → \\(K1 = T\\)\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
