TE})\\).\n- \\(K1\\): Boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Since exactly one box has gems, “boxes next to White contain gems” means the gem is in Blue or Black, i.e. \\(G \\ne \\text{WHITE}\\). “The other two boxes are empty” means Blue and Black are empty, i.e. \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\). So \\(K1\\) is always true.\n- If the gem were in BLUE or BLACK, then \\(W1\\) and \\(B1\\) would also be true, making all three boxes true, violating the requirement that at least one box is all false.\n- Therefore the gem must be in WHITE. Then \\(W1\\) is false, \\(B1\\) is false, and \\(K1\\) is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
