{"id": "direct_ft_train_00109", "logic_attempt": 2, "timestamp": "2026-08-23T21:16:38", "verdict": {"parse_ok": true, "gem": "WHITE", "gem_correct": true, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": true, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": {"BLUE": [false], "WHITE": [true], "BLACK": [true]}}, "reasoning": "We need answer logic puzzle. Need solve. Let's restate puzzle.\n\nThree boxes BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. Each box displays one or more statements. Here each box has one statement: B1, W1, K1.\n\nRules:\n- Exactly one box contains gems; other two empty.\n- At least one box has all statements true.\n- At least one box has all statements false.\n- Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix.\n- Statement truth value determined by actual state (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self/cross refs).\n\nNeed determine gem location and truth values.\n\nStatements:\nB1: \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\"\nNeed parse: Black box is true and the top statement of each box is true. Each box has one statement top statement = B1, W1, K1. So B1 asserts: K1 is true AND B1 is true AND W1 is true AND K1 is true (top statements of each box: B1, W1, K1). So B1 = (K1 ∧ B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1) = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1. Actually \"Black box is true\" means K1 true. \"Top statement of each box is true\" means B1 true, W1 true, K1 true. So B1 truth value is equivalent to B1∧W1∧K1. This creates self-reference.\n\nW1: \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\" => Blue and Black empty. Since exactly one gem, W1 true iff gem is in WHITE. False if gem not in WHITE (i.e., in BLUE or BLACK). It does not depend on other statements.\n\nK1: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" => K1 true iff W1 true. Because statement above K1 is W1. So K1 = W1.\n\nNeed solve with global constraints.\n\nLet's denote G = BLUE/WHITE/BLACK gem. W1 = (G=WHITE). K1 = W1. B1 = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1 = B1 ∧ W1 (since K1=W1). Actually B1 = B1 ∧ W1. This equation: If B1 true then requires W1 true and K1 true. If B1 false, statement B1 content false? Need careful: Statement's truth value = truth of proposition. B1 proposition is \"K1 true and B1 true and W1 true and K1 true.\" So B1 ↔ (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1). With K1=W1, B1 ↔ (B1 ∧ W1). Let's solve: If W1=false then RHS false, so B1 must be false. If W1=true then RHS = B1, equation holds for B1 either true or false? Wait B1 ↔ B1 is tautology if W1=true. So W1 true permits B1 true or false. But there are constraints at least one all-true box, one all-false box.\n\nBoxes:\nBLUE box has B1 only: all true iff B1=T; all false iff B1=F (single statement box is either all true or all false because one statement; cannot be mix).\nWHITE box has W1 only: all true iff W1=T; all false iff W1=F.\nBLACK box has K1 only: all true iff K1=T; all false iff K1=F.\n\nSo each box is all-true or all-false depending on its statement. Since at least one all-true and at least one all-false, and there are 3 boxes, possible patterns: two true boxes one false, or one true two false, depending statements.\n\nBut W1=K1, so WHITE and BLACK boxes have same truth value. Thus they are both all-true or both all-false. Then to have at least one true and at least one false, BLUE must be opposite. So B1 = ¬W1? Let's see: If W1=K1=T, then White/Black all true; need at least one all-false -> Blue must be all false -> B1=F. If W1=K1=F, then White/Black all false; need at least one all-true -> Blue all true -> B1=T. So B1 = ¬W1. Additionally B1 equation: B1 ↔ B1 ∧ W1. Let's test:\nIf W1=T, equation B1↔B1 tautology, but global requires B1=F. OK.\nIf W1=F, equation B1↔F => B1=F, but global requires B1=T. Contradiction! Wait check: If W1=F, K1=F, White/Black false, need Blue all true => B1=T. But B1 proposition is \"Black box true and top statements each true\": K1=false, B1 false? Actually if B1=T, statement B1 says K1 true and B1 true and W1 true and K1 true. But W1=false, so statement content false, so B1 cannot be true. Thus B1 necessarily false when W1=false. Then Blue all false, White false, Black false => no all-true box, violates at least one all-true. Thus W1 cannot be false? Let's test W1=T.\n\nIf W1=T => gem WHITE, K1=T. White and Black all true. Need at least one all-false: Blue must be B1=F. Equation B1↔B1∧T => B1=F works? If B1=F, statement content \"K1 true and B1 true and W1 true and K1 true\" is false because B1 true part false. So B1 false is consistent. Then boxes: Blue false, White true, Black true. Exactly one all-true? Actually White and Black both all-true, Blue all-false. At least one true yes two, at least one false yes. Remaining? \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" With 3 boxes: two true boxes (White, Black) and one false (Blue); no remaining? The rule means among three boxes, at least one all-true, at least one all-false, the third may be any. Here exactly one all-false and two all-true, okay. So solution gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T.\n\nNeed verify no other possibilities? Let's consider if W1=T and B1=T? Then all three true, no all-false, violates. So B1 must F.\n\nCould W1=F but B1=T somehow? B1 says black box true and top statements all true: K1 false so black box not true; B1 false; W1 false; so content false => B1 cannot true. So no.\n\nThus final: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning, final line exactly format.\n\nLet's craft reasoning concise but deductive. Need mention each statement id.\n\nPotential nuance: \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" could mean \"the black box (as a whole) is true\" i.e., all statements in black box true = K1 true. \"AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\" = B1, W1, K1 true. So B1 = K1 ∧ B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1 = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1. Since K1=W1, B1 = B1 ∧ W1. Good.\n\nLet's ensure no hidden issue \"top statement of each box\" maybe each box displays one statement, so top statement = that statement. Good.\n\nNeed answer on very last line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T\n\nNeed not include extra after. final answer with reasoning before final line.", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “The black box is true and the top statement of each box is true.”\n  So B1 = (K1 is true) AND (B1 is true) AND (W1 is true) AND (K1 is true).\n  Simplified: B1 = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1.\n\n- W1 = “The other two boxes are empty.”\n  So W1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- K1 = “The statement above this one is true.”\n  So K1 = W1.\n\nThus W1 and K1 always have the same truth value.  \nSo WHITE and BLACK boxes are either both all-true or both all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true and at least one box must be all-false, BLUE must have the opposite truth value from WHITE/BLACK.\n\nCase W1 = F:\nThen K1 = F, so WHITE and BLACK are all-false.  \nFor BLUE to be all-true, B1 = T.  \nBut B1 requires W1 to be true, contradiction.  \nSo W1 cannot be F.\n\nCase W1 = T:\nThen K1 = T, so WHITE and BLACK are all-true.  \nFor at least one all-false box, BLUE must be all-false, so B1 = F.  \nThis is consistent: B1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true, but B1 itself is false, so B1 is false.\n\nTherefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
