ightarrow (\\text{gem = WHITE})\\).\n- \\(K1\\) says \"the statement above this one is true,\" meaning \\(K1 \\leftrightarrow W1\\).\n- \\(B1\\) says the Black box is true and all top statements are true, so \\(B1 \\leftrightarrow (K1 \\land B1 \\land W1 \\land K1)\\), which simplifies to \\(B1 \\leftrightarrow (B1 \\land W1 \\land K1)\\).\n\nIf the gems are in BLUE or BLACK, then \\(W1 = F\\), so \\(K1 = F\\). Then \\(B1\\) would also be false, making all three statements false — violating the rule that at least one box is all true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE:\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n- \\(K1 = T\\)\n\nNow \\(B1\\) cannot be true, because then all three statements would be true, violating the rule that at least one box is all false. So \\(B1 = F\\).\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
