he user didn't explicitly state that \"with two false statements\" means neighboring. Hmm.\n\nAlternative reasoning to get BLUE without spatial: Use rule \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE\" and \"remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix\" maybe plus something about \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" false because none, then two solutions; cannot deduce unique. So must invoke spatial or alternative semantics. Since prompt includes left/middle/right, I think spatial is intended.\n\nLet's see if there is a way to derive unique BLUE using standard semantics plus \"the box with two false statements\" refers to \"the box that has exactly two false statements total in the entire set\"? Actually if gems in WHITE, there are exactly two false statements (B1 and K1), but they are not in one box; however the middle WHITE box is \"with two false statements\" because it is adjacent to them. So spatial.\n\nWhat if \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\" means \"the box [that contains] two false statements\" but since each box has one, no such box, B1 false. Then puzzle not unique. The only way unique is if positions matter. The prompt's \"BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right)\" is likely deliberately there to allow interpretation: the middle box has two adjacent statements; if both are false, it is \"the box with two false statements.\" In the final reasoning, I should explicitly state this interpretation: \"Because the boxes are arranged left-middle-right, the only box that can be 'with two false statements' is the middle WHITE box, namely when the statements on both neighboring boxes are false.\" This handles B1 semantics.\n\nBut wait: If B1 is on BLUE (left), it says \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS.\" Under spatial, the box with two false statements could be WHITE if both B1 (left neighbor) and K1 (right neighbor) are false. Then B1 is one of the false statements; self-reference. We used contradiction to rule out gem=WHITE. Let's