there is any possibility gem=BLACK under spatial with B1 maybe true? We ruled out by K1 self-ref. Let's double-check: If gem=BLACK, K1 = \"gems in a false box\" = BLACK is false = K1 false. So K1 = ¬K1, impossible. So gem≠BLACK.\n\nWhat if gem=BLUE: K1 true because BLUE false. Good. W1 true. B1 false because no box with two false statements (side statements: B1 false, K1 true -> not both false; W1 true; so no middle with two false). Good.\n\nThus final answer likely BLUE.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise but deductive. The user requested \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format\". We can include reasoning paragraphs then final line. Ensure final line exactly \"FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T\" no extra after.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n\n- Let B1, W1, K1 be truth values.\n- Since at least one box is all false, at least one statement is false, so not all three statements true. Hence W1 \"At most two statements are true\" is true. So W1=T.\n- Gems cannot be BLACK: if gem in BLACK, K1 would assert BLACK is false; that makes K1 true iff K1 false, contradiction.\n- Gems cannot be WHITE: Assume gem in WHITE. Then WHITE is true (W1=T), so K1 is false (gems not in a false box). Now B1 is the only unresolved side statement. If B1 were true, its claim would require the box with two false statements (the middle WHITE box, since only it can be flanked by two side statements) to contain gems, but WHITE would only be the box with two false statements if B1 and K1 were both false; B1 true contradicts that. If B1 were false, then both side statements B1 and K1 are false, so WHITE is indeed the box with two false statements; since gems are in WHITE, B1's claim would be true, contradicting B1 false. Thus no consistent assignment with gem=WHITE.\n- Therefore gem=BLUE.\n- Then BLUE is false (B1=F) because no box has two false statements (only B1 false; K1 true, W1 true). K1=T because gems are in BLUE, a false box. W1=T because there are exactly two true statements (W1,K1), at most two. Check all rules satisfied.\n\nNeed be careful \"only it can be flanked by two side statements\" phrase: The middle WHITE box has two neighboring statements (B1 on left, K1 on right). The left/right boxes only have one neighboring statement. So \"box with two false statements\" = WHITE when both B1 and K1 false. We'll state that.\n\nPotential issue: In step \"If B1 were true, its claim would require the box with two false statements ... to contain gems, but WHITE would only be the box with two false statements if B1 and K1 were both false; B1 true contradicts that.\" Actually if B1 true, then B1 is not false, so not both side statements false; thus WHITE is not the box with two false statements; but B1 claims the box with two false statements has gems. Since WHITE has gems and there is no other box with two false statements, B1 would be false. So B1 cannot be true. Good.\nIf B1 false, then side statements B1 and K1 both false, so WHITE is box with two false statements; since gems in WHITE, B1's content true -> B1 should be true, contradiction. Good.\n\nThus gem=BLUE unique.\n\nLet's also consider if \"box with two false statements\" could refer to BLUE/BLACK having two false statements? No, only middle has two neighbors. We'll state assumption.\n\nNeed final line exactly.\n\nLet's prepare final answer with reasoning and final line.", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the statements.\n\n- Since at least one box is all-false, at least one statement is false, so not all three statements are true. Therefore W1, “At most two statements are true,” is **true**.\n- The gems cannot be in BLACK: if they were, K1 would say “the gems are in a false box,” but BLACK would be false only if K1 is false, making K1 true iff it is false — impossible.\n- The gems cannot be in WHITE: if they were, then K1 would be false, since WHITE is true. For B1 to be consistent, the “box with two