ted? Need be certain.\n\nLet's test the other plausible solution W1=F,K1=T: gem=White, B1=T,W1=F,K1=T. Would that satisfy rules? Statements:\nG=White.\nB1 = \"A box next to Blue contains gems\" = White contains gems = True.\nW1 = \"The statement above this one is true\" = if above is B1, then W1 = B1 = True, not F. So W1=F only if W1 does NOT refer to B1 (e.g., no above). But user explicitly labels B1 above W1. So W1=T. Thus K1=F solution.\n\nWhat if W1 refers to K1? \"Above\" no. So no.\n\nThus final gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n1. Let gem location be G.\n2. Blue has only neighbor White, so B1 is true iff G=White.\n3. W1 refers to B1 (statement above), so W1 is true iff B1 is true iff G=White.\n4. Black is rightmost; its only adjacent box is White, but statement uses plural \"BOXES...CONTAIN GEMS\". Since exactly one gem exists, it cannot be true that multiple boxes next to Black contain gems; indeed Black has only one neighbor, so K1 cannot be satisfied; K1=False.\nActually if K1 is false always, then to have at least one all-true box, B1 or W1 must be true, so G=White. Then B1 and W1 true; K1 false. All rules satisfied: Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false.\n\nBut wait: If K1=false always, then for G=White, Black box all-false (K1=F). At least one all-false yes. At least one all-true yes (Blue, White). The rule says \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" If we designate Black as all-false and Blue as all-true, remaining White is all-true, allowed. Good.\n\nLet's ensure no other solution: If G=Blue or Black, B1=false, W1=false, K1=false => all boxes all-false, no all-true, violates. Thus unique.\n\nPotential issue: If K1=false always, then White all-true, Blue all-true, Black all-false: there are two all-true boxes. Rule says \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Some might interpret \"remaining box\" as the third box after selecting one all-true and one all-false; if there are two all-true and one all-false, the \"remaining\" box (the one not selected as all-false and not selected as all-true?) is the second all-true, which may be all-true, so allowed. It doesn't say exactly one all-true. So okay.\n\nCould there be a requirement exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false? The wording says AT LEAST ONE each; remaining may be anything. So two all-true is allowed. If they wanted exactly one each, they'd say \"one box all true, one box all false, one mix\". They explicitly allow remaining all-true/all-false/mix, so at least one each.\n\nNeed final line exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed ensure uppercase/lowercase? Format says gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F> etc. Use WHITE. Good.\n\nLet's write final answer with step-by-step reasoning, concise.", "content": "Let the gem location be the key. Blue has only one neighboring box, White, so:\n\n- B1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- W1 refers to B1, so W1 is true exactly when B1 is true, i.e. when the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 says “BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS” using plural “boxes,” but Black has only one neighboring box and exactly one gem exists, so K1 cannot be true.\n\nThus, to have at least one all-true box, the gems must be in WHITE. Then B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is false. This gives Blue all-true, White all-true, and Black all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
