lse, so the first disjunct is false. Since K1 is true, the second disjunct \"the White box is true\" must be true. In the only consistent reading, this means the White box is the true/correct box, i.e., contains the gems.\n- Therefore gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T.\n\nChecks:\n- At least one all-true: Black K1 true.\n- At least one all-false: Blue B1 false, White W1 false.\n- Exactly one gems: White.\n- W1 false: no box has two false statements.\n- K1 true: second disjunct true.\n\nThis is concise and deductive.\n\nNeed be careful: \"B1 is always false\" reasoning: \"other two boxes empty\" means White and Black empty -> Blue has gems; \"a box next to Blue contains gems\" means White has gems (since only White next to Blue). Both cannot be true because exactly one gems. So B1 false. Good.\n\nPotential objection: If White contains gems, first clause \"other two boxes empty\" false; second clause \"a box next to Blue contains gems\" true; conjunction false. Good.\n\nNow final line exactly.\n\nLet's produce final answer with step-by-step and final line.", "content": "B1 is always false: “the other two boxes are empty” would require Blue to hold the gems, while “a box next to the Blue box contains the gems” would require White to hold the gems. These cannot both be true, so B1 = F.\n\nSince each box has only one statement, no box can have “two false statements.” Therefore W1 = F.\n\nWith B1 and W1 both false, the required all-true box must be K1, so K1 = T.\n\nIn K1, the first disjunct (“the top statement of each box is true”) is false because B1 and W1 are false. Thus the second disjunct, “the White box is true,” must be true — meaning the White box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
