e box is true\" = W1 = T. K1 = F∨T = T.\nW1 = \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" What are \"false statements\"? If we treat P and Q as statements, P is false (about White/Black), Q is true; E false (about all statements), Fwhite true; W1 itself? Self-reference. White box's displayed statement W1 is true, not false. Blue's displayed statement B1 is false (whole). Black's displayed statement K1 is true. There are no boxes with two false displayed statements (Blue has one, White/Black zero). If we treat atomic subclaims:\n- P is a compound of two atomic statements: P1=\"White empty\" false; P2=\"Black empty\" true.\n- Q is atomic? \"White or Black contains gems\" could be compound: Q1=\"White contains gems\" true; Q2=\"Black contains gems\" false. But Q as disjunction true.\n- E compound: E1=\"B1 true\" false; E2=\"W1 true\" true; E3=\"K1 true\" true.\n- Fwhite = W1 true = true.\nThen false atomic statements: P1 (about White), Q2 (about Black), E1 (about Blue's statement). So White has one false atomic (P1); Black has one false atomic (Q2); Blue has one false atomic (E1). No box has two false atomic statements. Thus W1 would be false, contradiction. So Solution B only works if E is counted as a single false statement about all boxes, which is dubious because E's falsity is due to B1 only; it doesn't falsely claim something about White or Black specifically. Actually E says \"the top statement of each box is true\"; it makes three separate claims, one about each statement. A compound \"and\" is false if any conjunct false; it is not accurate to say E makes a false claim about White if W1 is true. So E should not count as false about White. Thus Solution B invalid under atomic decomposition.\n\nWhat about Solution A with gem Black, B1=F, W1=T, K1=F under clause counting on boxes:\nBoxes' displayed texts:\nBlue B1 = P∧Q with P=(White empty ∧ Black empty), Q=(White gems ∨ Black gems). Since gem Black: P=F∧T=F? Wait White empty true, Black empty false => P=false. Q=T∨F=true. So B1 false, one false clause P.\nWhite W1 = \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" If W1 true, then there is a box with exactly two false statements (clauses on its own display?) and that box has gems. Black's displayed statement K1 = E∨Fwhite. If K1=F, then both E and Fwhite are false. E = \"top statement of each box true\" = B1∧W1∧K1 = F∧T∧F = F. Fwhite = \"White box is true\" = ? If \"White box is true\" = W1 true, then Fwhite=T, so K1=T, not F. Therefore to have K1=F with W1=T, Fwhite must be false, meaning \"White box is true\" must be false despite W1 true. Thus Fwhite cannot mean W1 true; it must mean something else false when gem Black, e.g., \"White box contains gems\" false. So Solution A requires \"White box is true\" = White contains gems, which is unnatural. If Fwhite = W1 true, K1=T and Black has one false clause E, not two; W1 false. So Solution A invalid under natural K1=W1.\n\nThus neither solution is fully satisfactory under natural semantics with one statement per box. The only self-consistent solutions rely on nonstandard interpretations:\n- A: K1's \"White box is true\" = White contains gems (unnatural), W1 counts subclauses on boxes (moderate).\n- B: K1=W1 natural, W1 counts compound false statements about boxes (unnatural).\nCould there be another solution with K1=W1 and W1 counting subclauses on boxes? We proved no.\nWhat if K1 is exclusive OR, not inclusive? K1 = E XOR Fwhite. If Fwhite=W1, K1 = E XOR W1. B1=F. E=B1∧W1∧K1 = F => E=false always. Thus K1 = F XOR W1 = W1. Same as inclusive because E false. So no change.\nWhat if \"White box is true\" means \"White box contains gems\" (G_W) and K1=G_W as Solution A. Then with gem Black, K1=F, W1=T, B1=F. This satisfies statement counts? W1 refers to box with two false statements: Black's K1 as a disjunction has two false components E and G_W, so Black has two false statements in its display. This is plausible if \"statements\" in W1 include the two components of 