aybe gem Blue? Let's attempt systematically with atomic statements about each box, treating each statement/clause as a whole about specific boxes.\n\nDefine atomic/compound claims:\nFrom B1:\nC1 = \"White empty\" (about White)\nC2 = \"Black empty\" (about Black)\nC3 = \"White contains gems\" (about White)\nC4 = \"Black contains gems\" (about Black)\n(Assuming \"a box next to Blue\" could be White or Black? If only White, C4 not in B1. Let's consider both possible.)\nFrom W1:\nW = \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" (about some box X)\nFrom K1:\nD1 = \"Top statement of each box is true\" = B1 true ∧ W1 true ∧ K1 true (about B1,W1,K1; can be decomposed into D1_Blue=\"B1 true\", D1_White=\"W1 true\", D1_Black=\"K1 true\")\nD2 = \"White box is true\" = W1 true (about White statement) OR G_White (about White box containing gems). Assume K1=W1 => D2=W1 true.\n\nWe need count false statements about each box. A statement about a box can be atomic claim like \"White empty\", \"White gems\", \"W1 true\", \"K1 true\", \"B1 true\". Let's compute for each gem location with unknown W1,K1 satisfying K1=W1 and W1= existence.\n\nCase gem Blue:\nB1 false (C1=T,C2=T,C3=F,C4=F if C4 included; if only C3, C3=F). False atomic about Blue: D1_Blue=\"B1 true\" false. About White: C3 \"White gems\" false. About Black: C4 \"Black gems\" false. Also D1_White=\"W1 true\" depends W1; D1_Black=\"K1 true\"=W1. If W1 true, D1_White true, D1_Black true, D2 true. Then false statements: about Blue: D1_Blue; about White: C3; about Black: C4. Each box has one false statement -> no box with two. W1 false -> contradiction W1 true.\nIf W1 false, K1 false, false statements include about White D1_White false, about Black D1_Black false, about Blue D1_Blue false, C3 false, C4 false -> White has C3 false + D1_White false = two false statements about White! Black has C4 false + D1_Black false = two false statements about Black. So two boxes with two false statements, gems Blue not in either -> W1