tements, so W1 cannot be true regardless of gems, unless \"box with two false statements\" refers to the box whose statement is W1 and something else? No. So w=F always. Thus solutions as above.\n\nCould the statement K1 \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\" if gems in WHITE and w=F, k=T; then there is a false box (WHITE) with gems, so K1 true. That's fine.\n\nNow, maybe the \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE\" and \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE\" plus \"remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" actually imposes something about \"the remaining box\" after removing all-true and all-false boxes: if there are two true boxes and one false box, there are two boxes all-true; we can choose one as the all-true, one all-false, and the remaining is the other all-true (allowed). So fine.\n\nCould there be a hidden rule \"EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems\" and \"all statements truth value determined ... including self- and cross-references\" implying no statement can be vacuously false due to nonexistent reference? No.\n\nLet's consider if W1 \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" might be interpreted as an implication: \"If there is a box with two false statements, then that box has the gems.\" In logic, a definite description \"the box with two false statements\" presupposes existence; if none, statement might be false or truth-valueless. But puzzle says every statement true/false. If treated as universal? \"The box with two false statements\" maybe means \"Any box that has two false statements has the gems\" i.e., \"A box with two false statements has the gems\" (existential? Actually \"the\" implies unique). If none exists, existential statement false; universal vacuously true? But \"the\" not \"any\". The prompt says statement: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" not \"A box\". So false if no such box.\n\nWhat if \"box with two false statements\" refers to a box whose statement (single) is false and also contains two false statements? No.\n\nLet's examine original Blue Prince maybe boxes can have multiple statements, but user simplified to one each? Wait final instructions: \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1).\" This might be user telling us each box has exactly one statement, yes. So W1 false.\n\nGiven ambiguity, perhaps the correct answer expected by puzzle is g=BLUE because they assume \"A box next to White is true\" means exactly one? But wording \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" in English \"A box\" often at least one, not exactly one. However in logic puzzles, they sometimes interpret \"A box next to...\" as \"Exactly one of the boxes next to...\"? No, \"a\" is existential. But maybe because \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" singular \"A box\" could mean \"one box\" but not \"at least one\"; in common language, \"A box next to the white box is true\" can be read as \"There is a box next to the white box, and it is true\" (existential). To say exactly one, they'd say \"Exactly one box next to the white box is true.\" So I lean existential.\n\nBut the user prompt says \"rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" — they might expect unique solution under correct interpretation. Which interpretation makes uniquely solvable? Existential B1 does not. Exactly-one B1 does. Did they mention \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" maybe \"A box\" in puzzle context could mean \"One box\" exactly? Need infer from \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\": Since White is middle, there are two boxes next to White: BLUE and BLACK. Statement \"A box next to the White box is true\" could mean \"One of the boxes next to the White box is true\" (at least one). If they wanted exactly one, they'd likely write \"Only one box next to the white box is true\" or \"Exactly one\". So existential.\n\nMaybe there is another constraint in rules: \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Since each box has one