_B=T: B=T, NOT G_W=T => K=T all true invalid.\nG_W=T: B=T, NOT G_W=F => K=F valid; B=F, NOT G_W=F => K=T (F==F true) -> Blue false, White false, Black true valid. Two solutions for gem=White.\nG_K=T: B=T, NOT G_W=T => K=T all true invalid; B=F, NOT G_W=T => K=F all false invalid. No solution. So gem=White with two truth assignments, not unique.\nK1 = \"Blue box true AND gem in Black\" = B AND G_K:\nG_B=T: B=T,K=F valid.\nG_W=T: B=T,K=F valid; B=F,K=F all false invalid -> B=T valid.\nG_K=T: B=T,K=T all true invalid; B=F,K=F all false invalid -> no solution. Thus solutions gem=Blue or White, not unique.\nK1 = \"Blue box true AND gem in Blue\" = B AND G_B:\nG_B=T: B=T,K=T all true invalid => no solution.\nG_W=T: B=T,K=F valid; B=F,K=F all false invalid -> B=T valid.\nG_K=T: B=T,K=F valid; B=F,K=F all false invalid -> B=T valid. Multiple.\nK1 = \"Blue box false AND gem in Black\" = NOT B AND G_K:\nG_B=T: B=T,K=F valid.\nG_W=T: B=T,K=F valid; B=F,K=F all false invalid -> B=T valid.\nG_K=T: B=T,K=F valid; B=F,K=T valid. Multiple.\nK1 = \"Blue box true AND gem in Black OR Blue false AND gem in Blue\"? Weird.\nUnique gem=White is most natural.\n\nLet's verify if gem=White solution satisfies all rules with K1 = B AND NOT G_W:\nGem=White, B1=T, W1=T, K1=F.\nBlue statement B1: \"Either a box next to Black is true or Blue contains gems.\" White is next to Black and W1 true => first true; Blue empty => second false; OR true => B1 true. Good.\nWhite statement W1: \"The Blue box is true AND the statement above this one is true.\" Blue box true (B1 true), statement above B1 true => true AND true => W1 true. Good.\nBlack statement K1: \"The Blue box is white AND boxes next to White contain gems.\" If white=true, Blue true => first true; boxes next to White (Blue/Black) contain gems? Gem in White => neither neighbor contains gems => second false; AND false => K1 false. Good.\nAt least one all-true: Blue true, White true. At least one all-false: Black false. Exactly one gem: White. Unique.\n\nThis is coherent if white=true. We'll output.\n\nNeed ensure final answer line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nLet's produce reasoning with equations but concise.", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) be the Blue statement, \\(W1\\) the White statement, and \\(K1\\) the Black statement.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says: “The Blue box is true and the statement above this one is true.”  \n  The statement above \\(W1\\) is \\(B1\\), so:\n  \\[\n  W1 = B1 \\land B1 = B1\n  \\]\n\n- \\(B1\\) says: “Either a box next to the Black box is true, or this box contains the gems.”  \n  The box next to Black is White, whose truth is \\(W1 = B1\\). So:\n  \\[\n  B1 = (W1 \\lor \\text{Blue has gems}) = (B1 \\lor \\text{Blue has gems})\n  \\]\n\n- If Blue had the gems, then \\(B1\\) is true, so \\(W1\\) is true, and the Black statement would also be true, making all three boxes true — impossible because at least one box must be all false.  \n  So Blue does **not** have the gems.\n\n- If Black had the gems, then either \\(B1\\) and \\(W1\\) are true, making all three boxes true, or \\(B1\\) and \\(W1\\) are false, making all three boxes false. Both violate the rules.  \n  So Black does **not** have the gems.\n\n- Therefore the gems must be in the **White** box.\n\nWith the gems in White, \\(B1\\) must be true to avoid all three boxes being false, so \\(W1\\) is also true. Then the Black statement is false because the boxes next to White do not contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
