t to White are Blue and Black; Blue contains gems, so this clause would be true. Then K1 = (Blue box white false) ∧ true = false. Still K1 false. So K1 false independent.\n\nWhat if \"THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\" could be true if \"Blue box is white\" means \"Blue box's statement is white/true\"? No, ignore.\n\nLet's ensure final line format exactly:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed include reasoning before, final line last. Oververbosity desired 5, so moderate step-by-step.\n\nPotential issue: The user said \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\" So final line should have those.\n\nLet's maybe not mention \"ungrounded\" too heavily? But to justify unique, we need. The prompt says \"A statement's truth value is determined by actual state ... including self/cross refs\"; I can say \"A non-Blue gem would leave B1 and W1 in a self-supporting loop with no determining fact, so it cannot be the determined solution.\" That should be acceptable.\n\nLet's test if there is another possible grounded solution with gem not Blue and B1=False,W1=False,K1=False? That violates at least one all-true. So no.\nWhat if gem White and B1=True,W1=True,K1=True? K1 cannot be true because Blue box white false. So no.\nWhat if gem Black and B1=True,W1=True,K1=? K1 false. Same loop.\nThus unique grounded solution gem Blue.\n\nLet's produce final answer with step-by-step:\n1. Let G_B = Blue contains gems; W_true = White box all true; B_true = Blue box all true.\n2. K1: Blue box is White = false; boxes next to White contain gems = false under exactly one gem (would require both Blue and Black to contain gems). So K1=False. Thus Black is all-false.\n3. At least one all-true must be Blue or White. W1 = B1 and B1 = B1; hence W1's truth same as B1, so Blue and White stand or fall together. Thus B1 must be true.\n4. If gem not in Blue, B1=true would require White box true (W1=true), which requires B1=true — a circular loop with no external determiner. Since truth values must be determined by actual state, reject; hence gem must be Blue.\n5. With gem Blue, B1 true directly; W1 true; K1 false.\nFinal.\n\nNeed ensure \"boxes next to White contain gems\" under exactly one gem: The boxes next to White are Blue and Black. If gem in Blue, only Blue contains gems, not both, so clause false. If gem in White, neither adjacent contains gems; false. If gem in Black, only Black contains gems, not both; false. So false always. Good.\n\nBut if \"boxes next to white box contain gems\" means \"at least one adjacent contains gems\", then with gem Blue, clause true; K1 still false due first clause false. So K1 false regardless.\n\nLet's craft final reasoning concise.", "content": "Let \\(G_B\\) mean the Blue box contains the gems.  \n- K1 says: “The Blue box is White and boxes next to the White box contain gems.”  \n  The Blue box is not White, so K1 is false. Thus the Black box is all-false.  \n- Since at least one box must be all-true, either Blue or White must be true.  \n- W1 says: “The Blue box is true and the statement above this one is true.”  \n  That is equivalent to “B1 is true and B1 is true,” so W1 has the same truth value as B1.  \n  Thus Blue and White are both true or both false; to satisfy the all-true requirement, B1 must be true.  \n- If the gems were not in Blue, B1 would be true only because the White box is true, which itself depends on B1 being true — a circular loop with no determining fact.  \n  Therefore the gems must be in Blue.  \n- With gems in Blue: B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
