ution.\n\nLet's see if there is any other solution under \"white=true\" and \"boxes next to white contain gems\" as plural-literal (both adjacent contain gems). K1 = B1 ∧ (G_B ∧ G_K). Since exactly one gem, G_B∧G_K=false => K1=false. B1=W1=x. At least one all-true => x=T. Then BLUE/WHITE true, BLACK false; gem could be anywhere because K1 false regardless; three solutions. So to get unique gem WHITE, must interpret second conjunct as \"the gems are in the boxes next to the white box\" i.e., gem is adjacent to white (BLUE or BLACK). The statement says \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" plural, but with exactly one gem, the only sensible non-contradictory reading is \"the gem is located in one of the boxes next to the white box\" (the collective adjacent boxes contain the gems). The puzzle likely uses \"boxes ... contain gems\" collectively? It might be a translation; in original it could be \"the boxes next to the white box contain the gems\" meaning the gem is in one of those boxes. We'll adopt.\n\nLet's solve formally under intended interpretation:\n\nLet T_B = B1 (blue all true), T_W = W1 (white all true), T_K = K1 (black all true). Since each box has one statement, box true iff its statement true.\nG_B, G_W, G_K exactly one true.\n\nStatements:\nB1 = T_W ∨ G_B.\nW1 = T_B ∧ B1 = B1 ∧ B1 = B1. So T_B = T_W = x.\nK1 = (blue box is white) ∧ (gems in a box next to white). \"Blue box is white\" = blue box all true = T_B = x. \"Boxes next to white\" = BLUE or BLACK contain gems = G_B ∨ G_K. So K1 = x ∧ (G_B ∨ G_K) = x ∧ ¬G_W.\n\nNow apply rules:\nAt least one all-true: If x=F, then BLUE and WHITE all false; BLACK would have to be true for at least one all-true => K1=T. But K1 = F ∧ ¬G_W = F contradiction. Therefore x=T. So B1=T, W1=T.\nAt least one all-false: Since BLUE and WHITE all true, the all-false box must be BLACK => K1=F.\nK1 = T ∧ ¬G_W = ¬G_W. Set K1=F => ¬G_W=F => G_W=T. So gem is in WHITE.\nThus G_B=G_K=F, K1=F. Unique.\n\nCheck B1: T = W1 true OR G_B false = true. OK.\nW1: T = B1 true AND B1 true = true. OK.\nK1: F = (blue box white true) AND (boxes next to white contain gems: G_B∨G_K=false) = true AND false = false. OK.\n\nFinal: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed provide step-by-step reasoning concise but explicit. Also user asked \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 ... One T or F per id. Example...\" So final line exactly.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"the statement above W1 is B1\" in reasoning. Also \"black statement's second part false because gem not in blue/black\" etc.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n1. Because exactly one box contains gems, \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" must mean the gem is in one of the adjacent boxes (BLUE or BLACK) — otherwise plural would be impossible; under the puzzle's intended reading, it refers to the gem being among the adjacent boxes. Actually maybe avoid overexplaining ambiguity. State: \"The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK, so this part is true iff the gem is in BLUE or BLACK; i.e., false iff gem is in WHITE.\"\n2. \"The blue box is white\" means the BLUE box's statements are all true, i.e., B1 is true (using game terminology white=all-true). Thus K1 = B1 ∧ (G_B ∨ G_K).\n3. W1 = \"BLUE box is true\" (B1) AND \"statement above this one\" (B1) true => W1 = B1 ∧ B1 = B1. So BLUE and WHITE have same truth value; call x.\n4. B1 = W1 ∨ G_B = x ∨ G_B. Since x=W1. If x=false, then B1=false requires G_B=false, so blue/white false; but K1 = false ∧ ... = false, so no all-true box, violating rule. Thus x=true. So B1=true, W1=true.\n5. With BLUE and WHITE all true, the required all-false box must be BLACK, so K1=false.\n6. K1 = true ∧ (G_B∨G_K) = G_B∨G_K. For K1=false, must have G_B=false and G_K=false. Exactly one gem => G_W=true. Gem in WHITE.\n7. Verify: B1 true because W1 true; W1 true because B1 true; K1 false because blue box is white (true) but adjacent boxes don't contain gems (false).\n\nNeed be cautious: In step 3, \"BLUE box is true\" = B1