onse should include reasoning paragraphs then last line FINAL. Need ensure final line exactly as required, no trailing punctuation. Good.\n\nLet's craft reasoning with self-reference:\n- \"K1 cannot be true: if gems were in White, Blue/Black empty, so first clause true; and K1 true would mean Black has no false statement, making second clause false, contradiction. More formally k = A ∧ ¬k; only fixed point is A=false, k=false.\"\nActually if K1 true, Black has no false statements, so \"this box has at least one false statement\" is false, so K1 false; contradiction. If K1 false, \"this box has at least one false statement\" true, so K1 = A ∧ true = A; for consistency A must be false? Wait if K1 false and A true, then K1 = A ∧ true = true contradiction. So A false. This is intuitive.\nNeed be precise: K1 = A ∧ ¬k. If A true, k = ¬k no fixed point; so A false, k=false. Thus gems not White and K false.\nActually if A false, K1 = false ∧ ¬k = false, so k=false consistent. Good.\n\nThen W1 = B1.\nAt least one all-true: since k=false, need b or w true; but w=b, so b=w=true.\nAt least one all-false: k=false satisfies.\nB1 true: \"the box with no false statements has gems.\" Since b=w=true, all-true boxes are Blue and White. Gem not White -> Blue. Check B1 true: Blue is all-true and has gems. Fine.\nActually need check if B1 true could also be satisfied if gem=Black? No because Black all-false; B1 true would require gem in an all-true box; Black not all-true. So gem must be Blue or White; White excluded -> Blue.\n\nLet's also consider if \"at least one box all-false\" with k=false and b=w=true gives Black all-false yes. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If b=w=true, there are two all-true boxes. The phrase \"THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" singular might be problematic. To avoid ambiguity, maybe state \"Interpreting B1 as 'some/all-true box contains the gems' (the only reading giving a consistent solution)\"? But final answer doesn't need. However if evaluator expec