, so B1 is true iff White box is all-true. Since White has only W1, B1 = W1.\n3. Black's statement K1 = \"Either boxes next to White contain gems or the other two boxes are empty.\" The boxes next to White are Blue and Black; under exactly one gem, \"boxes next to White contain gems\" means one of those contains gem (gem in Blue or Black). The \"other two boxes empty\" means Blue and Black empty, i.e., gem in White. Thus K1 covers both possibilities and is always true.\n4. Therefore Black box is all-true, satisfying at least one all-true.\n5. Need at least one all-false box. Since K1 true, Black is not false. Blue and White each have one statement; they can be all-false only if B1=F and W1=F. But B1=W1, so both false iff W1=false. W1 false means \"White is empty\" is false => gem in White.\n6. Then W1=F, B1=F, K1=T.\n\nNeed be mindful: Step 3 \"boxes next to White contain gems means one of those contains gem (gem in Blue or Black)\" maybe if exactly one gem, \"boxes\" plural but means \"the boxes adjacent to White\" collectively? To avoid ambiguity, phrase: \"Interpreting the first alternative in the only sense compatible with exactly one gem: a gem is in a box next to White (Blue or Black).\" This covers all non-White locations; second alternative covers White. So K1 always true.\n\nPotential issue: If first alternative literally \"both boxes next to White contain gems\" then impossible; but unique solvability requires intended reading. Could mention \"The statement is worded as an either/or covering the gem's location: if it is in Blue/Black, the first alternative holds; if it is in White, the second alternative holds.\" Good.\n\nLet's final with reasoning and final line.", "content": "Let the gem location determine the statements.\n\n- W1 says “White is empty,” so W1 is true iff the gem is **not** in White.\n- B1 says “a box next to Black is true.” The only box next to Black is White, so B1 is true exactly when White’s statement W1 is true. Thus B1 = W1.\n- K1 says either a box next to White contains the gem, or the other two boxes are empty. These two cases cover all possible gem locations, so K1 is always true.\n\nSince K1 is always true, Black is an all-true box. We still need at least one all-false box. Blue and White can only be all-false if B1 and W1 are both false, which requires W1 = false, meaning White is **not** empty. Therefore the gem must be in White.\n\nThen W1 = F, B1 = F, and K1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
