" White box statement refers to statement above it, i.e., B1 on Blue box. So W1 = (B1 is true). Thus W1 truth equals B1 truth.\n\nNow apply constraints.\n\nLet gem = G ∈ {BLUE, WHITE, BLACK}. Let b = B1 truth = (G == WHITE). Let k = K1 = False always. Let w = W1 = b.\n\nSo truth values:\nIf gem=BLUE: b=False, w=False, k=False. All three statements false. Exactly one gem (Blue), other empty. At least one all-true? none. At least one all-false? all boxes each have one statement false, so yes all boxes all-false? Blue all-false, White all-false, Black all-false. But rule requires AT LEAST ONE box all TRUE and AT LEAST ONE box all FALSE. No all-true, invalid. Unless \"remaining box may be mix\" etc but at least one all-true required. So gem=BLUE invalid.\n\nIf gem=WHITE: b=True, w=True, k=False. Blue all-true; White all-true; Black all-false. At least one all-true (Blue, White), at least one all-false (Black). Remaining? Actually both Blue and White all-true, Black all-false, no mix. Valid.\n\nIf gem=BLACK: b=False, w=False, k=False. All statements false. No all-true, invalid.\n\nThus unique solution gem=WHITE; B1=T, W1=T, K1=F.\n\nNeed be careful: Could B1 \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" maybe includes both adjacent boxes? Blue has only one next box. White has both Blue and Black next to it; Black has only White next to it. \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX\" for Blue: box(es) next to Blue = White only. So fine. K1 \"THIS BOX IS BLUE\": Could mean \"this box contains gems\"? No, statement says \"This box is blue\" not \"This box contains gems\". It's a color/identity statement: Black box is not blue, false. It's not self-referential truth value? It is a statement about box color, independent. Good.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise and final exact line. Need include statement ids: B1, W1, K1. Final line exactly \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\". Ensure nothing after.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"at least one box has all statements true\": In solution Blue and White each have single statement true, so all-true; Black false all-false. Good.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning.\n\nPotential nuance: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Means among three boxes, each box's set of statements classified as all-true/all-false/mixed; need at least one all-true, at least one all-false. In our solution Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false; remaining? Actually no \"remaining box\" because two all-true and one all-false, allowed? Rule says at least one all-true and at least one all-false, remaining box may be ... It doesn't forbid additional all-true/all-false. So fine.\n\nNeed maybe mention W1 depends on B1: \"statement above this one\" = B1. So W1 = B1. K1 false. Then enumerate gem locations.\n\nLet's write final with step-by-step but not too long.\n\nNeed ensure final answer \"on VERY LAST line\" exactly one-line format nothing after it. We'll include reasoning before, then final line.", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “The box(es) next to Blue contain gems.” Since Blue is adjacent only to White, B1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1 = “The statement above this one is true.” The statement above White is B1, so W1 is true iff B1 is true.\n- K1 = “This box is blue.” The Black box is not blue, so K1 is false.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:\n   - B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.\n   - No box has all statements true. Invalid.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:\n   - B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = F.\n   - Blue: all true. White: all true. Black: all false.\n   - Satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false. Valid.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:\n   - B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.\n   - No box has all statements true. Invalid.\n\nTherefore, the gems are in WHITE, and all statements are determined.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
