n WHITE.  \n  So B1 = T iff gem = WHITE.\n\n- W1 says the statement above it (B1) is true, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.  \n  So W1 = T iff gem = WHITE.\n\n- If the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then B1 and W1 would both be false, and K1 would also be false because the box next to Blue would be empty. That would leave no all-true box, violating the rules.  \n\n- Therefore the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 and W1 true.  \n- To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, K1 must be false. The plural claim that “boxes next to the blue box contain gems” cannot be satisfied with only one gem, so K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
