ck box being blue. Since actual color of BLACK box is black, statement false. Unless \"blue\" refers to something else? In puzzle: BLACK box is right. The statement \"The black box is blue\" is false because black box is not blue. So K1 = F always.\n\nNeed apply global rules to see possible gem locations.\n\nLet variables: gem in B/W/K. B1 = (gem=W). W1 = B1. K1 = F.\n\nThen evaluate all-true/all-false boxes.\n\nCase gem=BLUE:\nB1 = false (gem not white)\nW1 = false\nK1 = false\nBox BLUE has one statement B1: all false => FALSE box.\nBox WHITE has W1: false => all false => FALSE box.\nBox BLACK has K1: false => all false => FALSE box.\nAll three all-false. At least one true? No. Violates \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE\". So gem=BLUE invalid.\n\nCase gem=WHITE:\nB1 = true\nW1 = true\nK1 = false\nBLUE box B1 true => all true.\nWHITE W1 true => all true.\nBLACK K1 false => all false.\nSatisfies at least one true (two), at least one false (one). Valid. Gem WHITE.\n\nCase gem=BLACK:\nB1 = false (white not gems)\nW1 = false\nK1 = false\nBLUE false, WHITE false, BLACK false. All false, no true. Violates at least one all-true. Invalid.\n\nThus unique solution gem=WHITE, B1=T, W1=T, K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure no alternative interpretation \"box next to black box\" might include black box itself? \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX\" generally means adjacent to black, not black itself. If includes black box itself? Usually \"next to\" means adjacent, not itself. So fine.\n\nNeed craft step-by-step concise. Final line exact format. Need include only final answer on very last line, nothing after. We can have reasoning before. Ensure final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed maybe address self-reference? W1 refers to B1, no paradox. K1 false.\n\nLet's produce reasoning with labels. Desired oververbosity 5. Step by step.", "content": "Let the three statements be evaluated from the actual gem location and statement truth values.\n\n- K1: “The black box is blue” is false