s? Usually boxes colored blue, white, black. Statement \"THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\" false. So K1 = F.\n\nThen evaluate boxes:\n- Blue box has only B1. Its all-true if B1 true; all-false if B1 false.\n- White box has only W1. Its all-true if W1 true; all-false if W1 false.\n- Black box has only K1 = false => all-false (since only statement false). At least one all-false satisfied by Black.\n- Need at least one all-true. So at least one of Blue or White must be all-true. Blue all-true iff B1 true iff gem=WHITE. White all-true iff W1 true iff B1 true iff gem=WHITE. So both B1 and W1 true if gem=WHITE, then Blue all-true and White all-true. If gem not WHITE (BLUE or BLACK), B1 false, W1 false, Blue all-false, White all-false, Black all-false => no all-true box, violates at least one all-true. So gem must be WHITE. Then B1=T, W1=T, K1=F. Check exactly one gems yes. At least one all-true: Blue true, White true; at least one all-false: Black false. Remaining? Actually remaining box? We have Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false. \"Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix\" If at least one true and at least one false, remaining box (the third?) In this case two boxes all-true, one all-false. That's allowed? Wording: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" This implies exactly? It says at least one all-true and at least one all-false, remaining box may be anything. So yes two all-true okay. Final gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure no other interpretations? Let's scrutinize.\n\nPotential issue: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\" Could \"a box next to the black box\" mean boxes adjacent to black, i.e., white box. Yes white is the only box next to black. But \"A box next to the black box contains the gems\" could also mean \"there exists a box adjacent to black that contains gems\"; since only white adjacent, equivalent gem=WHITE. Good.\n\nPotential issue: W1