se (white empty), W1 false, K1 false. All false. Invalid. Only WHITE.\n\nNeed final: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed include reasoning before final; final line exactly. Let's craft.\n\nPotential nuance: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Does \"remaining box\" imply there is exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false? It says at least one each. Could there be two all-true? The phrase \"remaining box\" suggests among three boxes, one all-true, one all-false, one remaining. But \"at least one\" explicitly allows more. In our solution two all-true one all-false, so there is no \"remaining box\" besides? Actually there are two all-true, one all-false; the \"remaining box\" after choosing one all-true and one all-false could be the other all-true, which is allowed to be all-true. So valid. If they intended exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false, our solution still has at least one all-true and at least one all-false, but has two all-true not a mix. Wording: \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" This means if there is a remaining box (after identifying at least one true and at least one false), it can be anything; so multiple all-true okay. Good.\n\nNeed maybe consider if W1 \"statement above this one is true\" when B1 true, W1 true; when B1 false, W1 false. No paradox.\n\nLet's final.", "content": "The black box statement K1 is simply false because the black box is not blue.  \nB1 is true only if the white box contains the gems, since the white box is the only box next to the black box.  \nW1 is true exactly when B1 is true.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=true, W1=true, K1=false → at least one all-true and one all-false.\n\nSo the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
