 a self-referential equation. Let's solve: If K1=T, then RHS = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ T = B1∧W1, so need B1∧W1=T => B1=T, W1=T. If K1=F, then RHS = B1∧W1∧F = F, so equation F ↔ F holds regardless B1,W1. Thus K1=T requires B1=T,W1=T; K1=F possible with any B1,W1? Actually if K1=F, equation F = F always true, so B1,W1 arbitrary. So K1 can be F always; K1 can be T only if B1=W1=T.\n\nNeed also W1 = B1 (if above refers to B1). Then K1=T requires B1=T and W1=T => B1=T,W1=T. K1=F possible arbitrary, but W1=B1.\n\nNeed gem exactly one: B1 = (gem=WHITE). So if B1=T => gem=WHITE. If B1=F => gem≠WHITE (Blue or Black).\n\nRules at box level: At least one box all-true, at least one all-false, remaining mix allowed. Since each box has one statement, \"all true\" = statement true; \"all false\" = statement false. Thus among the three boxes, at least one true statement (box all true) and at least one false statement (box all false). The remaining box can be true or false. So essentially at least one T and at least one F among B1,W1,K1.\n\nWe need find unique solution? Let's enumerate possible assignments of B1,W1,K1,g hidden with equations.\n\nVariables: g ∈ {BLUE, WHITE, BLACK}; b = (g=WHITE); w = b; k = (b ∧ w ∧ k) OR black_has_at_least_two_false. Black_has_at_least_two_false = (k=F) ? Since only one statement in Black, number of false statements in Black is 1 if k=F else 0, never >=2. So false. k = b ∧ w ∧ k = b ∧ b ∧ k = b ∧ k. Thus equation k = b ∧ k. Solve: If b=T: k = k => k can be T or F (tautology? Actually k = T ∧ k => k = k, no constraint). If b=F: k = F ∧ k = F => k=F. So b=F forces k=F; b=T allows k=T or F. Wait earlier I used B1∧W1 = b∧b=b, so K1 = b ∧ K1. If b=T, K1=K1 no constraint. If b=F, K1=F.\n\nThus:\n- If gem≠WHITE (b=F), then w=F, k=F. All statements false. This satisfies at least one all-false but no all-true. Rule requires at least one all-true, so invalid.\n- If gem=WHITE (b=T), w=T, k can be T or F.\n  * If k=T: B1=T,W1=T,K1=T => all boxes all-true. This satisfies at