by-step concise but deductive. Final line exactly format. They want \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it): FINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\" Need ensure final line exactly. We can provide reasoning before, then final line.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\nLet g=WHITE denote gems in White. B1 iff gems in box next to Blue = White, so B1 = (g=WHITE).\nW1 = \"statement above W1 is true\" = B1.\nK1 = (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1) OR (Black has at least two false statements). Since Black has only one statement K1, the second disjunct is false. So K1 ↔ (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1) = (B1 ∧ K1) because W1=B1.\nIf gems not White, B1=F, W1=F, then K1 ↔ F => K1=F; all three false, violates at least one all-true.\nThus gems must be White, B1=T, W1=T.\nWith B1=T,W1=T, K1 equation becomes K1 ↔ K1, so K1 may be T or F. If K1=T, all three statements true, violating at least one all-false. Therefore K1=F.\nCheck: Blue true, White true, Black false => at least one all-true (Blue/White), at least one all-false (Black). K1 false because not all top statements true (K1 false) and Black cannot have at least two false statements.\n\nFinal line.\n\nNeed be mindful \"black box has at least two false statements\" could refer to statements in black box? Only one statement so false. Yes.\n\nLet's produce final with reasoning and final line exactly.", "content": "Let the gem location determine B1:\n\n- B1: “A box next to the blue box contains the gems.” The only box next to Blue is White, so B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- W1: “The statement above this one is true.” The statement above W1 is B1, so W1 = B1.\n- K1: “Either the top statement of each box is true, or the black box has at least two false statements.” Since Black has only one statement, “at least two false statements” is impossible. So K1 is true only if B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n\nIf the gems were not in White, then B1 = False, W1 = False, and K1 would be forced False. That would make all three boxes all-false, violating the requirement of at least one all-true box.\n\nSo the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 = True and W1 = True.\n\nWith B1 and W1 true, K1 could be true or false by its self-reference, but if K1 were true, all three boxes would be all-true, violating the requirement of at least one all-false box. Therefore K1 = False.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
