ue, W1=true, K1=false.\n5. Since B1 true means a box next to Blue (White) contains gems, gem=WHITE.\n\nCheck rules: Blue box all true (B1=T), White all true (W1=T), Black all false (K1=F). At least one all-true and one all-false satisfied.\n\nNeed final line: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed be careful: W1 refers to statement above it = B1. But if user expected W1=false? The final answer would differ. Let's further investigate possibility from actual game to be certain.\n\nSearch memory deeper: Blue Prince is a roguelike puzzle game. There is a known logic puzzle with three boxes \"Blue, White, Black\" and statements:\n- Blue: \"A box next to the blue box contains the gems\"\n- White: \"The statement above this one is true\"\n- Black: \"Either the top statement of each box is true or the black box has at least two false statements\"\nI think I saw solution on a wiki: \"The gems are in the White box. Blue: True, White: True, Black: False.\" Yes, because White statement references Blue statement. The boxes in game might actually be arranged vertically? Wait \"above this one\" implies Blue is above White in the room? But user says left/middle/right. Could be there is a row of boxes, but the statements are displayed in a vertical list on each box? No. The game might have the boxes on a shelf with Blue on left, White middle, Black right; \"above\" could refer to a statement above on the same box? But White has one statement. Hmm.\n\nActually maybe the boxes have multiple statements but only top statement labeled? User: \"statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1\" clearly each one statement. So W1 cannot refer to another statement on same box. The only \"above\" is B1 in the printed order. Therefore W1=T.\n\nLet's also consider if \"above this one\" could refer to the statement physically above the white box if there is an unlabeled box? No.\n\nLet's solve under W1=B1 rigorously and final.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"assuming 'above' refers to the statement listed above it (B1), as no other statement exists above W1 on its own box.\" But final answer should not include ambiguity? The user wants final answer. We can state assumption? Might be safer to include in reasoning: \"The White box's only statement refers to the statement above it in the puzzle list, B1.\" Since they gave list order, it's fair.\n\nLet's produce final with reasoning then final line exactly.", "content": "- The Blue box is leftmost, so its only neighboring box is the White box.  \n  Thus **B1 is true iff the gems are in the White box**.\n\n- W1 refers to the statement above it, which is B1, so **W1 = B1**.\n\n- The Black box has only one statement, so “the Black box has at least two false statements” is false.\n\n- Therefore K1 reduces to: “B1, W1, and K1 are all true.”  \n  If K1 were true, then B1 and W1 would also be true, making all three boxes all-true — violating the “at least one all-false” rule.  \n  If B1 were false, then all three statements would be false — violating the “at least one all-true” rule.\n\n- So B1 and W1 must be true, and K1 must be false.  \n  Since B1 is true, the gems are in the White box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
