lue box. Since B1 is true, the gems must be in the Blue box.\n- Check consistency: gems in Blue => B1 true; W1 \"Blue box is true\" true; K1 \"at least three statements are false\" false because only K1 is false. At least one all-true: Blue and White; at least one all-false: Black. Unique.\n\nNeed be careful: If B1=true because Blue has phrase and gems, then W1=true because B1 true. K1=false because not all three false (only K1 false). Good.\n\nPotential objection: \"box with two false statements\" could mean actual false statements, not textual phrase; but step-by-step should mention interpretation.\n\nNeed final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed ensure no extra after final line. Use final answer with reasoning before final line. Desired oververbosity 5, so moderate.\n\nLet's craft step-by-step reasoning:\n1. There are exactly three statements total.\n2. K1 true would require all three statements false, including K1 itself, impossible. Thus K1=F.\n3. Since K1=F and at least one statement must be true, B1 or W1 true.\n4. W1 truth equals B1 truth because Blue box contains only B1, so \"Blue box is true\" is equivalent to B1=T. Therefore B1 and W1 have same value; since at least one true, both B1=W1=T.\n5. Thus false statements: only K1 (one false).\n6. B1 is true, so its referent \"the box with two false statements\" must contain gems. Since each physical box has only one displayed statement, no box actually has two false statements; the only box \"with two false statements\" as text is the Blue box. Hence gems in Blue.\n7. Consistency check.\n\nNeed maybe phrase \"the phrase 'two false statements' appears on the Blue box's card\" to justify.\n\nLet's final.", "content": "Since there are exactly three statements total, K1 (“at least three statements are false”) would be true only if all three statements, including K1 itself, were false—impossible. So K1 is false.\n\nWith K1 false, at least one of B1/W1 must be true. W1 says “the Blue box is true,” which is equivalent to saying B1 is true, so B1 and W1 share the same truth value. Therefore B1 and W1 must both be true.\n\nB1 is true, and the Blue box is the box whose statement contains the words “two false statements,” so the gems are in the Blue box. Then W1 is true, and K1 is the only false statement.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
