ly if W1 false and K1 false and B1 false, false count=3, making K1 true, contradiction. So K1 false forces W1 true? Let's derive carefully:\nK1 true => all three false => K1 false contradiction, so K1 false.\nWith K1 false, the statement \"at least three statements are false\" is false, meaning fewer than 3 false statements. Since B1 is false already, W1 must be true (otherwise B1,W1,K1 all false would be 3 false, making K1 true). Thus W1=true.\nThen Blue box's statement false, so Blue not all-true; White all-true; Black all-false. At least one all-true satisfied (White).\nW1's content \"The Blue box is true\" is true; since Blue's statements are not true, this must mean Blue is the true/genuine box containing the gems -> gem=Blue.\nCheck final: B1 false, W1 true, K1 false; false statements=2 (B1,K1), so K1 false consistent; rules satisfied.\n\nThis is strong.\n\nLet's write final answer with final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed ensure no extra after final line.", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. There is exactly one statement per box, so no box can contain two false statements. Therefore B1, “The box with two false statements has the gems,” must be false. So B1 = F.\n2. K1 cannot be true: if K1 were true, all three statements would have to be false, but K1 itself being true contradicts that. So K1 = F.\n3. Since B1 and K1 are false, the only way to satisfy “at least one box has all statements true” is for White’s statement W1 to be true. So W1 = T.\n4. W1 says “The Blue box is true.” Since Blue’s statement B1 is false, “true” here must mean the Blue box is the genuine box containing the gems. Thus the gems are in Blue.\n5. Final check: B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = F gives exactly two false statements, so K1 is correctly false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}
