er statements in the world\"; it says the boxes display one statement each. Content is not displayed, so not a statement. Therefore K1's reference to two false statements cannot refer to displayed statements; it's vacuous. So underdetermined.\n\nI will final with underdetermined. To partially comply with format, I'll use \"UNKNOWN\" for gem and B1/W1, but K1=T. Maybe acceptable as \"cannot determine\".\n\nLet's ensure final line exactly one line, nothing after. Use:\nFINAL: gem=UNKNOWN; B1=UNKNOWN; W1=UNKNOWN; K1=T\nBut the allowed gem values don't include UNKNOWN; however it's the truthful answer. Alternatively, I could output \"FINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T\" and in reasoning note underdetermined; but final line contradicts reasoning's \"not unique\". I think UNKNOWN is better.\n\nWait, maybe we can output all possible solutions in final line? \"FINAL: gem=BLUE/WHITE/BLACK; B1=F/T; W1=T/F; K1=T\" but not exact. No.\n\nLet's final with UNKNOWN.", "content": "The puzzle is underdetermined as stated. Since each box has only one displayed statement, no box can have two false displayed statements, so K1 is vacuously true. That leaves three valid gem placements, all satisfying the “at least one true / at least one false” rule.\n\nFINAL: gem=UNKNOWN; B1=UNKNOWN; W1=UNKNOWN; K1=T"}
