 value is determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\" This allows self-reference; we should use fixed point.\n\nNeed consider \"gems are in a box that is false\" means the box containing gems has all its statements false. Since each box one statement, means the statement on the gem box is false. So F(g) = truth value of statement displayed on gem box is false. Let S(B)=b, S(W)=w, S(K)=k. Then F(g) = ¬ S(g). So K1 = k ∧ ¬ S(g). But S(g) depends on g:\nIf g=BLUE, F(g)=¬b.\nIf g=WHITE, F(g)=¬w.\nIf g=BLACK, F(g)=¬k.\nThen K1 statement content = k ∧ F(g), and k = (k ∧ F(g)). Equivalent k ⇒ F(g).\n\nAtomic equations:\nb = FALSE (B1 always false) under no-box-with-two-false interpretation. But let's hold b as variable maybe.\nw = b ∨ k (exists adjacent true).\nk = k ∧ F(g) (self-ref).\n\nAnd global constraints: at least one true statement among b,w,k; at least one false statement among b,w,k; exactly one gem.\n\nLet's solve generally for b,w,k,g with b maybe variable, then apply b=false? Actually B1 content may be \"box with two false statements has gems\" could depend on number of false statements among b,w,k and gem location. Let's define \"box with two false statements\": Could be interpreted as \"the box that has two false statements\" meaning there is a box with exactly two false statements among its displayed statements, impossible because one statement each, so false always. But to be thorough, maybe they mean \"the box [statement?] with two false statements\" could mean \"the statement that has two false statements\"? No.\n\nAlternative interpretation: Because there are three statements total, \"box with two false statements\" might refer to the box associated with the statement that has two false statements? No.\n\nLet's search memory: In Blue Prince, there is a puzzle with three boxes BLUE, WHITE, BLACK each with statements, and rules exactly one gem, at least one box all true, at least one all false, remaining mix. Statements like \"The box with two false statements has the gems\", \"A box next to this box is true\", \"The black box is true and the gems are in a box that is false\". Actually each box may display multiple statements in original? Wait perhaps original had boxes with multiple statements: BLUE box might have multiple statements including B1, B2, etc. But user says statement counts BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1, so they simplified? Hmm \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" makes sense if the BLUE box has maybe multiple statements? But user explicitly: \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1).\" So yes each box one statement. The phrase \"box with two false statements\" then refers to a box that has two false statements among the entire set? Wait each box has one statement, so a \"box with two false statements\" cannot exist. Could be a mistranscription: maybe \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\" means \"the box with [the statement that has] two false statements\"?? No.\n\nPerhaps \"box with two false statements\" refers to a box among the three that has exactly two statements that are false *in the entire puzzle*? Actually if each box has one statement, a box \"with two false statements\" might mean \"the box that is associated with two false statements\" because each statement is displayed on a box; but no.\n\nCould there be a hidden twist: The boxes themselves have colors BLUE, WHITE, BLACK; \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\" might refer to a box that is described by two false statements? Like there are statements about boxes? No, only B1,W1,K1.\n\nLet's examine W1: \"A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\" — \"A box next to this box is true\" means at least one adjacent box has all statements true. With one statement each, adjacent box's statement true.\n\nK1: \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\" — \"The black box is true\" means all statements in black box are true, i.e., k=True. \"The gems are in a box that is false\" means the box containing gems is all-false, i.e., its statement(s) false; with one statement, statement false.\n\nGiven B1 impossible, b=False always. Then W1 = b ∨ k = k. So w = k. Then K1 = k ∧ F(g). Since k = w. Cases:\n- If k = True, then K1 true => F(g)=True => statement of gem box false.\nSince b=False, w=True, k=True. Gem cannot be black because black statement true, F(black)=False. Gem cannot be white because white statement true, F(white)=False. Gem must be blue, F(blue)=True. So g=BLUE works: b=False, w=True, k=True, gem blue. At least one true (W,K), at least one false (B). Good.\n- If k = False, then w=False, b=False. All statements false. Violates at least one true. So invalid.\nThus unique solution: gem=BLUE, B1=F, W1=T, K1=T.\n\nNeed ensure K1 true with k=True and F(g)=True: K1 statement content = \"black box is true\" true AND \"gems are in a box that is false\" true => true. Consistent.\n\nThus final answer likely gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T.\n\nNeed provide step-by-step reasoning concise. But must be careful with \"box with two false statements\" — B1 false because no box has two false statements since each box displays exactly one statement. However, is there a possibility that \"box with two false statements\" refers to a box that contains two false statements among the three total statements? No, let's articulate: Since BLUE, WHITE, BLACK each have exactly one statement, none can have two false statements, so B1's claim is false regardless of gem location.\n\nThen W1: middle box adjacent to both blue and black; since B1 is false, W1 is true iff K1 is true.\n\nThen K1 self-referential: K1 true requires black's statement true and gem in a false box. Since K1 true itself makes black true, gem cannot be black (because black would be true, not false). Also if W1 were true (since K1 true => W1 true), gem cannot be white because white would be true. So gem must be blue. Blue's statement is false, satisfying \"gems in a box that is false.\" Thus K1 true, W1 true, B1 false. Check at least one true/false: satisfied.\n\nNeed maybe also rule out K1 false: If K1 false, then W1 false (since W1=K1), and B1 false => all three statements false, violating at least one true. Thus K1 must be true. Good.\n\nLet's craft final reasoning step by step. Need final line exactly format. They asked \"Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it).\" So final answer should include reasoning then final line. Need ensure final line exactly: FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T\n\nNeed include step-by-step reasoning before final line. The final line must be very last, nothing after. We'll produce reasoning and then final line. Ensure no extra after final.", "content": "Since each box has exactly one statement, no box can have two false statements. Therefore B1 is always false.\n\nSo W1, meaning “a box next to this box is true,” is true only if K1 is true, because B1 is false and the middle box is next to both BLUE and BLACK.\n\nNow consider K1. If K1 were false, then W1 would also be false, and B1 is already false. That would make all three statements false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true. Therefore K1 must be true.\n\nSince K1 is true, the BLACK box is true and the gems must be in a box that is false. The gems cannot be in BLACK, because BLACK is true. They also cannot be in WHITE, because K1 true makes W1 true. Therefore the gems must be in BLUE, whose statement B1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
