ions maybe allow multiple solutions? Let's model B1 as \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" Suppose due to self-reference, if B1 is true, then B1 is not false; if B1 false, it is one false statement. For a box to have two false statements, it needs two statements; but each box has one. So impossible independent of self-ref.\n\nCould \"BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\" mean \"the box that has the second false statement\"? No.\n\nMaybe the labels B1, W1, K1 are not the only statements; the *box names* \"BLUE\", \"WHITE\", \"BLACK\" are statements? The colors? No.\n\nCould the gems be inside a box that also contains two false statements if we count the gem's \"presence\" as a statement? Like \"This box has gems\" is a statement written nowhere? If each box has an implicit statement \"This box has the gems\" (one true, two false), then each box has one displayed statement + one implicit gem statement = two statements. Ah! This is interesting! The rules: \"EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\" Could each box be considered to \"have\" a statement about containing gems? No, but the actual state of the world includes gem location; maybe each box has an implicit statement \"I have the gems\" displayed? Not listed. However, if each box has an implicit statement about containing gems, then each box has two statements: displayed statement plus implicit gem statement. Then \"box with two false statements\" could exist: a box with its displayed statement false and its implicit gem statement false (empty) = two false statements. This could make B1 meaningful! Let's explore.\n\nThe prompt: \"Each box displays one or more statements.\" Then lists displayed statements. It does not mention implicit statements. But rules include \"EXACTLY ONE box contains the gems; other two empty.\" In the world, for each box, the proposition \"This box contains the gems\" is either true or false, but it's not a displayed statement. Does a box \"have