nique gem, so no.\n\nWhat if W1 counts only displayed statements but B1's \"box with two false statements\" could refer to the two false statements B1 and K1 if B1 false and K1 false, and \"the box\" is the one containing the gems? No.\n\nLet's consider if \"the box with two false statements\" could mean \"the box [whose statement is one of] two false statements\"; i.e., there are two false statements, and the box referenced is the one that contains the gems? No, the statement would be ambiguous if two boxes contain false statements. But if there are exactly two false statements and one true statement, maybe \"the box with two false statements\" could refer to the box **not containing** a false statement? Actually if there are two false statements, they are in two boxes; the remaining box has the true statement and the gems? The phrase \"the box with two false statements\" might be interpreted as \"the box [that is left when there are] two false statements\" = the box with the true statement? No.\n\nLet's test if B1 = \"The [only] box that has two false statements [associated with it in the sense it is not one of the false-statement boxes?]\" No.\n\nMaybe \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" could mean \"The box [that contains the statement] 'two false statements has the gems'\"? No.\n\nGiven uncertainty, I think the most defensible logical answer is \"no solution\" because under standard interpretation the premises are inconsistent. But the user's final format forces a solution; perhaps they want the puzzle's intended solution White. We need infer from \"Blue Prince game\" context: In game, puzzles are designed to be solvable; maybe the correct solution is indeed White with B1=T,W1=F,K1=F and the \"two false statements\" are W1 and K1, both about the White box (one inside, one about). Let's see if there is a way to state this without logical contradiction: We can say \"K1 is false, so the White box does **not** contain two false statements; however, the White box is the subject of two false statements: its own W1 is false and K1—which is about it—is false. Therefore, if we interpret B1's 'box with two false statements' as the box associated with two false statements (the only interpretation that gives a solution), the White box is that box.\" But the puzzle didn't specify \"associated\"; the rules say statements' truth values determined by world, not interpretation. A logic puzzle shouldn't rely on ambiguous \"associated\".\n\nCould there be a unique solution if we treat **all statements as referring to the entire set of statements**, and \"the box with two false statements\" = \"the box that is in the position of having two false statements in the truthful evaluation\", maybe the White box because K1 (false) says White has two false statements, and W1 (false) is in White, so White \"has\" two false statements in the sense K1 falsely claims it has two? This is too meta.\n\nLet's search memory one last time: I think the answer to this exact puzzle in Blue Prince is **BLUE box** with B1=False, W1=True, K1=False? Wait how could W1 true if only W1 true? Unless there are two true statements: W1 and K1? K1 false no. No.\nMaybe K1 true if White box has exactly two false statements because W1 is false and **B1 is false?** B1 is on Blue, not White. So no.\nCould White box have exactly two false statements if W1 false and the statement K1 (on Black) is considered part of White box because it says \"White box...\"? Then K1 true if both false, contradiction; but if K1 true, then White has one false statement (W1) plus K1 true => not two; so K1 cannot true. Thus K1 false. Then White has two false statements associated (W1,K1), making B1 true if gems in White. This is exactly White solution and K1 false despite White having two false associated because K1's own content refers to statements **inside** White, not associated. This is the only coherent way to get a solution: K1's predicate \"White box has exactly two false statem