false. However K1's first disjunct \"boxes next to white contain gems\" for G=WHITE is false (both adjacent empty), second \"other two boxes are empty\" is true (Blue and Black empty), so K1 true. For G=BLUE, first disjunct \"boxes next to white contain gems\" could be true because Blue (next to White) contains gem; second false; K1 true; then B1=true (White box true because W1 true), W1=true, all true -> no false box, violates at least one all-false. For G=BLACK similarly all true. So only G=WHITE gives false boxes. This seems plausible and unique. The wording \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" with exactly one gem could be interpreted as \"the boxes next to the white box [i.e., at least one of them] contain gems\" because plural \"boxes\" but \"contain gems\" maybe \"among the boxes next to White, there is a gem\" -> at least one adjacent box contains a gem. Then K1 becomes \"either gem is next to White or the other two boxes are empty\", which is a tautology given exactly one gem (if gem next to White = Blue/Black; if not, gem in White => other two empty). Yes K1 always true. That makes unique by all-false requirement.\n\nCould be intended: Black statement is intentionally tautological? \"Either boxes next to the white box contain gems or the other two boxes are empty\" — Actually if gem in Blue or Black, then boxes next to White (Blue/Black) contain the gem (at least one). If gem in White, the other two boxes (Blue and Black) are empty. So yes K1 is always true. That seems a natural way to parse \"Either boxes next to white contain gems OR the other two boxes are empty\" as covering all gem locations: either gem is in a box adjacent to White (Blue/Black) or the non-White boxes are empty (gem in White). Since \"boxes next to white contain gems\" could mean \"the gem(s) are among boxes next to white\" rather than both contain gems. In a game with one gem, \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" might be intended as \"a box next to the white box contains gems\" or \"the boxes next to the white box contain the gems\" (collectively). So K1 = T always. Then unique G=WHITE because otherwise all statements true violating at least one false. Also B1: \"A box next to black box is true\" = White box true? If G=WHITE, W1 false so White box false, so B1 false. Good.\n\nThis likely final answer: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T.\n\nNeed ensure B1 false if G=WHITE: B1 says \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\": Black's only neighbor is White; White's W1 is false, so White box is not all-true, so B1 false. Correct.\n\nNeed ensure at least one all-true: BLACK has K1 true, so all-true. At least one all-false: BLUE and WHITE both false. Remaining? There are two false boxes, one true; \"remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix\" okay. Exactly one gem in WHITE; other two empty.\n\nNeed provide step-by-step reasoning concise but deductive. Final line exact format. Need maybe mention interpretation: K1 is always true because if gem is in Blue/Black then a box next to White contains gem; if gem in White then other two boxes empty. Then W1 false (White not empty), B1 false (White box not true). Check consistency: If gem White, Blue and Black empty; K1 true; W1 false; B1 false. At least one true (Black), at least one false (Blue, White). Could there be other solutions? Need show uniqueness: If gem Blue or Black, W1 true, so White box true => B1 true; K1 true as tautology; then all three statements true => no all-false box, violates rule. Therefore gem cannot be Blue/Black. So gem White only. Good.\n\nNeed be cautious: B1 \"A box next to black box is true\" if gem White, White box false due W1 false, so B1 false. Blue box all-false because its only statement B1 false. White false. Black true. Good.\n\nLet's craft final answer with steps and final line exactly. The user requested \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer...\" So we can include reasoning then final line. The final line exactly one-line format nothing after. Need label every statement id only B1,W1,K1. Use FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T\n\nNeed ensure no extra after final line.", "content": "Let the gem location be considered case by case.\n\n- K1 says: either a box next to WHITE contains the gem, or the other two boxes (BLUE and BLACK) are empty.  \n  - If the gem is in BLUE or BLACK, then a box next to WHITE contains the gem, so K1 is true.  \n  - If the gem is in WHITE, then BLUE and BLACK are empty, so K1 is true.  \n  Thus K1 is always true.\n\n- If the gem were in BLUE or BLACK, then WHITE would be empty, so W1 would be true.  \n  Then the WHITE box would be true, making B1 (“a box next to BLACK is true”) true.  \n  So all three statements would be true, violating the rule that at least one box must be all-false.\n\n- Therefore the gem must be in WHITE.  \n  Then W1 is false, so the WHITE box is not true, making B1 false.  \n  K1 remains true because the other two boxes are empty.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
