e OR the other two boxes are empty\" = gem in blue/black OR gem in white = tautology (since exactly one gem in one of three; if not blue/black then white). So K1 always true. Then statuses:\n- G=W: W1=F,B1=F,K1=T => BLUE F, WHITE F, BLACK T valid.\n- G=B: W1=T,B1=T,K1=T => BLUE T, WHITE T, BLACK T all true, no false invalid.\n- G=K: W1=T,B1=T,K1=T => all true invalid.\nThus unique G=W, B1=F,W1=F,K1=T. This yields uniqueness! Could this be intended? Let's examine K1 wording: \"EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\" If first clause means \"either [one of the] boxes next to the white box contain gems\" (at least one adjacent to white has gem), and second clause \"the other two boxes are empty\" = if gem not next to white, then the other two boxes (the non-adjacent? There are no non-adjacent except white itself? Wait boxes next to white = blue and black, so if one of those contains gem, first true; if not, gem is in white, then the other two boxes (blue and black) are empty, second true. So K1 is indeed tautology under exactly one gem: gem either adjacent to white (blue/black) or in white (then other two empty). So K1 always true. That would make puzzle uniquely G=W because if gem in blue/black, all boxes true? Let's check BLUE true if B1 true; B1 = WHITE true = W1 true = gem not white, yes true. WHITE true W1 true. BLACK true K1 true. All three all-true violates at least one false. So gem must be WHITE; then W1 false, B1 false, K1 true => BLUE all-false, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-true. Valid. Unique. This seems plausible and elegant. The K1 statement being a tautology in context ensures black always true, forcing white gem to create false boxes.\n\nBut is \"EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" grammatically \"Either boxes next to the white box contain gems\" = \"Either [one of the] boxes next to the white box contain gems\" indeed at least one. The phrase \"Either boxes ... contain gems\" is ungrammatical; likel