intended K1 tautology \"either of the boxes next to white contains gems\" OR \"the other two boxes are empty\" (gem in white). This yields unique.\n\nNeed be careful: If K1 always true, BLACK box all-true regardless gem? K1 true for all gem locations yes. Then black box all-true. At least one false requires BLUE or WHITE false. BLUE has B1 = WHITE true = W1 = gem not white. If gem not white, B1 true and W1 true => BLUE and WHITE both true; all three true -> invalid. Therefore gem must be white. Then W1 false, B1 false -> BLUE all-false, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-true. Valid. Unique.\n\nLet's verify B1: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" = \"There is a box next to black that is true.\" Black's only neighbor is WHITE. If gem=WHITE, WHITE statement W1 \"This box is empty\" is false, so WHITE box is all-false (false box), not true. Therefore B1 false. If gem=BLUE, WHITE empty -> W1 true -> WHITE true -> B1 true. If gem=BLACK, WHITE empty -> W1 true -> WHITE true -> B1 true. Good.\n\nK1 under at-least-one-adjacent interpretation: If gem=WHITE, boxes next to white are BLUE and BLACK: neither contains gem? Actually boxes next to white = blue and black; they are empty, so first clause \"either box next to white contains gems\" false; second clause \"the other two boxes are empty\" = the other two boxes besides white? Blue and black empty true. So K1 true. If gem=BLUE, first clause true (blue adjacent to white contains gem); second false (other two boxes blue/black not empty because blue has gem). K1 true. If gem=BLACK, first true; second false; K1 true. So K1 necessarily true.\n\nThus final: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise. Need final line exact format. We must ensure \"on VERY LAST line output final answer exactly one-line format (nothing after it)\". So final answer should include reasoning then last line FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T. Need no extra after. Ensure statement labels only B1,W1,K1.\n\nLet's craft reasoning.\n\nBut before final, let's consider if there is any hidden issue: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" Could \"next to black\" include blue? In a row left-middle-right, blue is not next to black because white between. Yes only white. If arrangement circular? No, boxes left/middle/right; next to means adjacent horizontally. So only white adjacent to black.\n\n\"EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\": Need explain interpretation. We should mention interpreting \"either boxes next to white contain gems\" as \"at least one box next to white contains the gem\" (the natural \"either of the boxes\"). Then \"other two boxes\" are the two boxes other than WHITE (BLUE and BLACK). Actually if gem in WHITE, other two boxes empty true; if gem in BLUE/BLACK, one of boxes next to white contains gem true. So K1 always true. We need be explicit because user may expect this. But what if they intended \"both boxes next to white contain gems\"? Then final might differ? But puzzle \"uniquely solvable\" and our reasoning yields unique; if both interpretation non-unique, so intended must be at-least-one. Need maybe justify K1 always true: Since exactly one gem, if gem is in BLUE or BLACK then a box next to WHITE contains gems; if gem is in WHITE then the other two boxes (BLUE and BLACK) are empty. Therefore K1 true in all cases. This is elegant and ensures unique.\n\nLet's double-check if at-least-one all-false satisfied with gem=WHITE: BLUE has B1 false => all statements false (only one) so BLUE all-false; WHITE has W1 false => all-false; BLACK has K1 true => all-true. At least one true yes; at least one false yes; remaining (if choose one true black and one false blue, remaining white false) allowed. Good.\n\nAlternative if gem=BLUE: B1 true, W1 true, K1 true => all three true; violates at least one false. If gem=BLACK same. Thus unique.\n\nLet's produce final answer with step-by-step. Need maybe not overdo. Final line exact.\n\nNeed ensure \"on VERY LAST line output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\" So after FINAL line no newline? Usually newline after okay but \"nothing after it\" means no additional text. We can include reasoning before final line, then final line last. Good.\n\nLet's craft:\n\n1. Positions: BLUE-WHITE-BLACK. Black's only neighbor is WHITE, so B1 is true iff WHITE box is all-true.\n2. WHITE has only W1, so WHITE all-true iff W1 true, i.e. gem not in WHITE. Thus B1 = W1 = (gem≠WHITE).\n3. K1: If gem is BLUE or BLACK, at least one box next to WHITE contains gems; if gem is WHITE, the other two boxes (BLUE, BLACK) are empty. Therefore K1 is true regardless of gem location.\n4. Since K1 true, BLACK is all-true. To satisfy at least one all-false, at least one of BLUE/WHITE must be false. If gem were BLUE or BLACK, then W1 true and B1 true, so BLUE and WHITE both all-true and no false box. Thus gem cannot be BLUE/BLACK.\n5. Therefore gem=WHITE. Then W1 false, B1 false, K1 true.\n\nFinal line.\n\nNeed maybe mention B1 false because WHITE not true.\n\nLet's answer.", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- BLACK is on the right, so the only box next to BLACK is WHITE. Thus B1 means “WHITE is true.”\n- WHITE has only W1, so WHITE is true iff W1 is true, i.e. iff WHITE does NOT contain the gems.\n- Therefore B1 = W1 = “gem is not in WHITE.”\n- K1 says either a box next to WHITE contains the gems, or the other two boxes are empty. Since exactly one gem exists, this is always true: if the gem is BLUE or BLACK, a box next to WHITE contains it; if the gem is WHITE, the other two boxes are empty.\n- So BLACK is all-true. To satisfy the required all-false box, BLUE or WHITE must be false. That happens only if the gem is in WHITE, making W1 and B1 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}
