s box\" refers to the box displaying statement, which is BLACK box, so false. So K1=false.\n\nThen W1=B1, K1=false.\n\nNeed at least one true and at least one false. K1 false; need at least one true => B1 or W1 true. B1 true iff gem=WHITE; W1=B1. If gem=WHITE, B1 true, W1 true, K1 false. If gem=BLUE or BLACK, B1 false, W1 false, K1 false => no true statement, violates at least one true. So gem must be WHITE, B1=T, W1=T, K1=F. Need check exactly one gem, yes.\n\nBut wait K1 \"THIS BOX IS BLUE\" could be self-referential? Actually statement on Black box says \"This box is blue\"; the box is black, so false. Could there be ambiguity because \"blue\" might refer to truth value? No, box color is Blue/White/Black. So K1 false.\n\nNeed ensure rules about at least one all-true/all-false: With one statement each, all-true means statement true; all-false means statement false. We have B1 true, W1 true, K1 false. At least one true (B1/W1), at least one false (K1). Remaining? Since each one statement, no \"remaining box\" aside from true/false boxes; fine.\n\nNeed provide step by step reasoning concise, final line exact format.\n\nNeed be careful: The statement B1 \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\": In Blue left box, boxes next to it: White middle only? Black is two away, not next. So statement true iff White contains gem. Good.\n\nBut one nuance: \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" If there is exactly one box next to it, \"boxes next to this box contain gems\" could be interpreted as \"the boxes next to this box (plural) contain gems\" meaning all adjacent boxes have gems. For Blue, only White, so true iff White has gem. For Black, statement not relevant because not on Black. For White, boxes next to it are Blue and Black, but B1 is on Blue only. OK.\n\nCould there be \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" on White refers to statement above = B1? Yes, above White is Blue box statement B1. So W1 = truth value of B1.\n\nK1 \"THIS BOX IS BLUE\" Could \"blue\" refer to statement being true? No, box color. K1 false.\n\nThus final.\n\nNeed output exactly final line: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed include reasoning before final. Ensure final line nothing after.", "content": "Let the gem location determine B1 directly.\n\n- B1 says: “Boxes next to this box contain gems.” The blue box is only next to the white box, so B1 is true iff the white box contains the gems.\n- W1 says: “The statement above this one is true.” The statement above is B1, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n- K1 says: “This box is blue.” The black box is not blue, so K1 is false.\n\nAt least one statement must be true. Since K1 is false, either B1 or W1 must be true. Both depend on B1, so B1 must be true. Therefore the white box contains the gems.\n\nThen:\n- B1 = T\n- W1 = T\n- K1 = F\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
