blue\", both false because black box is black/not left. So K1 false. Then Black box all statements false (only K1). That's consistent with at least one all-false.\n\nNeed determine gem location and B1/W1. We have exactly one gem.\n\nLet's reason.\n\nLet G ∈ {Blue, White, Black}.\n\nB1 = (G = White) because only adjacent neighbor to Blue is White contains gems? Actually \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\": For Blue, adjacent boxes: White. So B1 true iff White contains gems. Since exactly one gem, yes G=White.\n\nW1 = B1 is true = B1.\n\nK1 = false always (Black box is not blue).\n\nConstraints:\n- Exactly one gem.\n- At least one box has all statements true.\n- At least one box has all statements false.\n- Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix.\nEach box has only one statement, so each box is all-true if its statement true; all-false if its statement false. \"Remaining may be mix\" irrelevant since single statement.\nThus we need at least one true statement and at least one false statement among B1,W1,K1. Since K1 false, at least one false satisfied. Need at least one true statement, so at least one of B1/W1 true. B1=W1, so need B1=true => G=White. Then B1=True, W1=True, K1=False. This satisfies Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false, at least one true and false. Also exactly one gem at White.\n\nIs that uniquely solvable? Let's check potential issue: if G=White then B1 true, W1 true, K1 false. If G=Blue then B1 false (White empty), W1 false, K1 false => all false, no true statement, violates at least one all-true. If G=Black then B1 false, W1 false, K1 false => all false, no true. So only White.\n\nThus final: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed present step-by-step concise. Ensure final line exact format.", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = \"Boxes next to this box contain gems\" — for the BLUE box, the only adjacent box is WHITE, so B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1 = \"The statement above this one is true\" — the statement above WHITE is B1, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n- K1 = \"This box is BLUE\" — stated by the BLACK box, so K1 is false.\n\nSince K1 is false, we already have an all-false box. We also need at least one all-true box. Therefore at least one of B1 or W1 must be true. Since B1 and W1 have the same truth value, B1 must be true, which means WHITE contains the gems.\n\nThen:\n- B1 = T\n- W1 = T\n- K1 = F\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
