e: In our solution, all three boxes are either all-true or all-false; no mixed. Fine.\n\nNeed consider if K1 \"gems are in a box that is true\" could mean \"the box containing gems is one of the true boxes\" where \"true box\" is a box whose statement is true. In final solution, White is true, Black is also true. Gem in White (true). Good.\n\nOne potential issue: K1 in gem=WHITE: \"the statement above this one is true\" could refer to B1? If \"above this one\" in the Black box list, above K1 is W1. If physically above means Blue left? No. But in puzzle layout displayed left-to-right, no above. So W1.\n\nLet's prepare final answer with moderate detail.", "content": "B1 says both boxes next to White contain gems. Since exactly one box contains the gems, B1 must be false in every possible case.\n\nW1 is true exactly when the gems are in the White box.\n\nK1 is true only if both: the box with the gems is a true box, and W1 is true.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, and K1=F because W1 is false. All statements false → invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, and K1=F because W1 is false. All statements false → invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=F, W1=T. The White box is true, and W1 is true, so K1=T. This gives at least one true box and at least one false box → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
