should yield unique. Thus B1 = both boxes next to white contain gems, always false under exactly one gem. Good.\n\nNeed ensure K1 self-reference? It says \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\". Could \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE\" refer to B1? Wait K1 is in BLACK box, above it in vertical list? Let's revisit layout: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). But statements displayed on boxes maybe physically: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. \"Above this one\" in Black box? There is no statement above within black box; maybe \"the statement above this one\" refers to the statement on the box above it in the screenshot arrangement? In Blue Prince, boxes might be arranged vertically? User says BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right) but lists statements top-to-bottom: BLUE, WHITE, BLACK. The phrase \"above this one\" likely refers to W1 because W1 is listed above K1 in the text. If \"above\" referred to spatial above in the room? Not know. Since user explicitly says BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right, there is no above/below spatially; maybe \"above\" means previous statement in the puzzle text. So W1. Good.\n\nCould K1's \"box that is true\" mean \"the gem is in a box whose label/box is true\" maybe \"a box that is true\" = a box that is all-true? Yes.\n\nNeed maybe consider statement truth value includes self-reference; K1 references W1 and \"box that is true\" which may depend on K1 if gem in BLACK? Let's formalize: K1 truth value = T iff [gem location L is a \"true box\"] AND [W1 is true]. A \"true box\" = all statements on that box are true. If L=BLACK, then \"BLACK is true\" depends on K1; so K1 = (K1 true) AND (W1 true). If W1 false (gem not WHITE), then K1 = (K1 true) AND false = false, consistent. If W1 true (gem=WHITE), L≠BLACK so no self-dependence for L=BLACK; BLACK's \"true box\" not relevant. If L=BLUE, BLACK true not relevant; K1 = (BLUE all-true) AND W1 false = false. If L=WHITE, K1 = (WHITE all-tru