But no solution otherwise, so must adopt that interpretation. Maybe in reasoning we can emphasize \"Given the puzzle is stated as uniquely solvable, 'the Blue box is true' must be read as 'the Blue box is the one with the gems' (the true/correct box), not merely that its inscription is true; otherwise no consistent assignment exists.\" But final answer should be decisive.\n\nLet's produce step-by-step:\n1. Let b,w,k be truth values of B1,W1,K1.\n2. K1 says at least three statements are false. Since there are exactly three statements, this would require all three statements false, including K1 itself. If K1 were true, then it would not be false, so it cannot be true. Hence K1=F.\n3. Therefore there are at most two false statements.\n4. B1 says \"the box with two false statements has the gems.\" But each box contains exactly one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Thus the descriptive condition in B1 is never satisfied; B1 is false. (Equivalently B1 cannot be true because that would require a box with two false statements, impossible here.)\n5. Since B1=F, there are already two false statements? Wait B1 false and K1 false => exactly two false statements so far if W1 true. Actually at step 4, we haven't determined W1. We know K1 false and B1 false -> two false statements. If W1 also false, then all three false, making K1 true, contradiction. Therefore W1 must be true.\nAlternatively: K1 false => not all three false, so at least one of B1,W1 true. B1 cannot be true (no box with two false statements), so W1 must be true.\n6. W1 says the Blue box is the true/correct box; since W1 true, gems are in BLUE.\n7. Verify: gem=BLUE makes W1 true; B1 false because no box with two false statements; K1 false because only B1 and K1 are false (2 false, not at least 3). At least one all-true box: White. At least one all-false: Blue and Black. Consistent.\n\nNeed ensure final line exactly: FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F\n\nLet's consider if there is any issue with \"A