th two false statements\" could refer to a box that contains two false statements **in total across all boxes**? No, we interpret standard. Fine.\n\nLet's also ensure final line exactly one-line format with no extra after. \"FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F\" Nothing after.", "content": "K1 cannot be true, because it would require all three statements, including itself, to be false. So K1 is false.  \nB1 cannot be true, because there is no box with two false statements when each box has only one statement. So B1 is false.  \nSince K1 is false, not all three statements can be false; therefore W1 must be true.  \nW1 says the Blue box is the true/correct box, so the gems are in Blue.  \nCheck: B1 false, W1 true, K1 false gives exactly two false statements, so K1’s claim is false. Consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}
