nIn gem=White, Black K1 true => mix; if K1 false, Black two-false empty => K1 should be true contradiction. So K1 true => all mix.\nIn gem=Black, White unique two-false; K1 true.\n\nWe can present candidate evaluation succinctly.\n\nFinal line exact: FINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T\n\nLet's ensure no extra text after final line.", "content": "Step by step:\n\n1. Exactly one box contains the gems.\n2. W1 says the Blue box has the gems, so W1 is true only if gem = BLUE.\n3. B1 says the boxes next to Blue contain the gems. Blue’s neighbors are White and Black, so B1 is true if the gems are in White or Black, and false if the gems are in Blue.\n4. Test gem = BLACK:\n   - B1 = True, since Black is next to Blue and contains the gems.\n   - W1 = False, since the gems are not in Blue.\n   - The box with two false statements is White: W1 is false and White is empty. Since White does not have the gems, K1 is True.\n   - Black then has K1 true and contains gems → all true. White has W1 false and is empty → all false. Blue is mixed. This satisfies the rules.\n5. Other gem locations fail to produce both an all-true box and an all-false box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}
