statements\" might refer to the box that has two false statements **among the three statements written on the boxes next to it plus itself?** We did S(X)=self+neighbors and got K1=true always, not K1=false. Let's re-evaluate S(X)=self+neighbors for G=Blue,k=F:\nb=F,w=T,k=F.\nS(Blue)={B1,W1}: B1 false, W1 true => C_B=1.\nS(White)={W1,B1,K1}: W1 true, B1 false, K1 false => C_W=2.\nS(Black)={K1,W1}: K1 false, W1 true => C_K=1.\nUnique X=White with count2. K1=¬(G=X)=¬(Blue=White)=true, so k=F contradicts. So G=Blue k=F invalid under S(X)=self+neighbors. G=White,k=F:\nb=T,w=F,k=F.\nS(Blue)={B1,W1}: B1 true,W1 false => C_B=1.\nS(White)={W1,B1,K1}: W1 false,B1 true,K1 false => C_W=2.\nS(Black)={K1,W1}: K1 false,W1 false => C_K=2.\nTwo boxes count2, no unique -> K1 true, contradict k=F. So k=F invalid. Thus S(X) yields K1=true always, non-unique three solutions.\n\nWhat if \"box with two false statements\" counts only statements **about** the box (excluding own displayed) but includes K1 about X? We did aboutness, K1=true always. What if counts only statements **displayed on neighboring boxes** (excluding own)? For Blue neighbors White only => C_B=¬w; White neighbors Blue+Black => C_W=¬b+¬k; Black neighbors White only => C_K=¬w. Then G=Blue,k=F: b=F,w=T => C_B=0,C_W=1+1=2,C_K=0 => unique X=White; K1=¬(Blue=White)=true contradict k=F. G=White,k=F: b=T,w=F => C_B=1,C_W=0+1=1,C_K=1 => no X -> K1 true contradict. G=Black,k=F: b=F,w=F => C_B=1,C_W=1+1=2,C_K=1 => unique X=White; K1=¬(Black=White)=true contradict. So k=F invalid; k=T always? G=Blue,k=T: C_B=0,C_W=1+0=1,C_K=0 no X; k=T consistent. G=White,k=T: C_B=1,C_W=0+0=0,C_K=1 no X; k=T. G=Black,k=T: C_B=1,C_W=1+0=1,C_K=1 no X; k=T. Three solutions.\n\nWhat if counts only statements displayed on **self** plus statements displayed on **neighbors** but K1's aboutness irrelevant; we did S(X) self+neighbors.\n\nWhat if \"box with two false statements\" = box that is the subject of exactly two false statements **among statements no