tement's truth value is determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references)\" meaning we must find a fixed point where K1's reference to \"the box with two false statements\" itself affects which statements are false, and there may be multiple fixed points; the puzzle says uniquely solvable, so maybe only one fixed point exists under intended K1 semantics. We need identify intended semantics that yields unique fixed point.\n\nLet's attempt to solve using iterative fixed point? Start with candidate G, assume K1? Actually K1 truth depends on box with two false statements, which depends on K1 truth. We can treat as equations. We want exactly one consistent assignment among all G and k.\n\nLet's define a plausible semantics where \"box with two false statements\" = the box that has exactly two false statements **among the three statements B1,W1,K1, where a statement is \"on\" a box if displayed there, and a statement is \"about\" a box if it explicitly names/refers to it; K1 is about whichever box has two false statements.** This is own+about, gave two solutions (G=Blue FTF and G=White TFF) plus three K1=true no-X? Wait under own+about, k=T no-X solutions also valid if nonexistence -> K1 true. But if we require the phrase \"the box with two false statements\" to have a referent for K1 to be true? Actually K1 true can be true because \"It is not true that the box with two false statements has gems\" is true if there is no such box. In natural language, a statement \"The present king of France is bald\" is false; \"It is not true that the present king of France is bald\" is true. So K1=true no-X valid. Thus own+about model gives five solutions including no-X. If we require existence of a box with two false statements for K1 to be meaningful (treat non-referring as invalid), then only G=Blue FTF and G=White TFF. Still two.\n\nWhat additional constraint could eliminate one? Maybe \"AT LEAST ONE box has