Yes.\nIf G=Blue, White empty, B1 false.\nThus symmetric.\n\nCould there be a rule that empty boxes contain no statements? No.\n\nMaybe \"the gems\" are a set of multiple gems? Exactly one box contains the gems, other two empty. B1 \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" could mean \"all boxes next to this box contain gems (each has some gems)\" but since only one box contains gems, for Blue with one neighbor, true iff White has gems. Same.\n\nLet's consider if Black's statement K1 \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" might be **about Black itself** if Black has two false statements? But Black has one statement. However, if K1 is false, then Black's statement is false; Black has one false statement. If K1 true, Black has one true statement. Never two.\n\nMaybe \"the box with two false statements\" refers to the box that has two false statements **among the three statements on all boxes, where one statement is on that box and another statement on an adjacent box?** We tried self+neighbors. Non-unique K1=true.\n\nLet's brute force all assignments under a generalized model where each box's \"false statements\" count includes some subset of {B1,W1,K1} depending on relationships (written on, about, adjacent). We can try see if any subset yields unique G. There are three boxes and three statements; each statement can be associated with one or more boxes. K1's association depends on X. Let's search for known solution online in memory: I think answer might be \"gems in BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F\". Why? Because many puzzle solvers would set K1=false if the box with two false statements has gems; the only way to have a box with two false statements is if K1 itself is false and refers to that box, and the box's own statement false plus K1 false = two false statements. Then either Blue or White could be that box. To decide, they might note Blue's statement B1 is \"boxes next to this box contain gems\"; if White had gems (G=White), then B1 true; then false statements would be W1 and K1, making White the box with two false statements. That's consistent. So why would Blue be preferred? Maybe because the box with two false statements must have **two false statements written on it**, not about it; but none does, so K1 cannot be false. Thus G=White TFF invalid because White has only W1 false on it, not two false statements on it. G=Blue FTF also invalid because Blue has only B1 false on it. So K1=false impossible under on-box-only. Thus K1=true, non-unique. So if puzzle is unique, K1 must be false and on-box-only must be false; so they must count about statements. Then two solutions.\n\nCould the statement W1 \"THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\" be considered **about the White box** because it is written on White? No, it's about Blue. B1 on Blue about White. So own+about counts each statement once for its writer and once for its subject. That yields two false statements for the box that is both writer of a false statement and subject of another false statement. In G=Blue, Blue writes B1 false and is subject of K1 false -> two. In G=White, White writes W1 false and is subject of K1 false -> two. Symmetric.\n\nWhat about B1 in G=White: Blue writes B1 true and is subject of W1 false -> one false (W1 about Blue). White writes W1 false and is subject of B1 true and K1 false -> two. Yes.\nIn G=Blue: Blue writes B1 false and is subject of W1 true and K1 false -> two. White writes W1 true and is subject of B1 false -> one.\nSymmetric.\n\nMaybe K1's statement \"It is not true that the box with two false statements has the gems\" when false means the box with two false statements **does have gems**, but if there are two boxes with two false statements, then \"the box\" fails uniqueness; in G=White,k=F under own+about, is White the unique box with two false statements? Let's compute counts including all statements on/about:\nG=White,b=T,w=F,k=F,X=White:\nFalse statements: W1 (on White), K1 (on Black about White). B1 true.\nCounts:\nBlue: displayed B1 true (0); about W1 false (1); about K1? X=White so K1 not about Blue =>0. Total 1.\nWhite: displayed W1 false (1); about B1 true (0); about K1 false (1). Total 2.\nBlack: displayed K1 false (1); about K1? X=White so K1 not about Black =>0. Total 1.\nUnique X=White. Good.\nG=Blue,b=F,w=T,k=F,X=Blue:\nFalse statements: B1 (on Blue), K1 (on Black about Blue). W1 true.\nCounts:\nBlue: displayed B1 false (1); about W1 true (0); about K1 false (1). Total 2.\nWhite: displayed W1 true (0); about B1 false (1); about K1? X=Blue so K1 not about White =>0. Total 1.\nBlack: displayed K1 false (1); about K1? X=Blue so K1 not about Black =>0. Total 1.\nUnique X=Blue. Both unique.\n\nSo no distinction.\n\nWhat if K1's reference to \"box with two false statements\" should be interpreted as the box whose **statement** is false and which is **subject** of exactly one other false statement, i.e., two false statements total including its own. In G=White, White's own W1 false and subject of K1 false -> two. In G=Blue, Blue's own B1 false and subject of K1 false -> two. Same.\n\nCould the location of Black (right) matter: Black's statement K1 is about the box with two false statements. If G=Blue, K1 about Blue (left); if G=White, K1 about White (middle). Both possible. Maybe the phrase \"the box with two false statements\" refers to a box that has **two false statements next to it**? Then unique X might be different. We tried neighbor counts.\n\nLet's systematically search for a semantic definition that yields a unique solution among the five possible truth assignments (G=Blue FTT, FTF; G=White TFT, TFF; G=Black FFT). We can list properties:\nA) G=Blue, k=T: B1=F,W1=T,K1=T. False statements count=1 (B1). No box has two false statements on/about? On-box: none; about: B1 about White false => White has 1; W1 true; K1 true. No X.\nB) G=Blue, k=F: B1=F,W1=T,K1=F. False count=2 (B1,K1). On/about: Blue has B1 on false + K1 about false =2; White has B1 about false=1; Black