ave a unique solution if we consider **White has two adjacent boxes**, so if G=White, B1 true but also Black's K1? No.\n\nLet's search memory of exact puzzle: \"Blue box: Boxes next to this box contain gems. White box: The blue box contains the gems. Black box: It is not true that the box with two false statements has the gems.\" I think I saw answer \"White box\" maybe? Let's test if answer White TFF under own+about: G=White, B1=T, W1=F, K1=F. Does that make sense? Blue box statement true (White has gems). White box statement false (Blue does not). Black box statement \"It is not true that the box with two false statements has the gems\" false because the box with two false statements is White and it has gems. This uses K1 about White, and W1 false on White = two false statements associated with White. The box with two false statements is White (middle). This seems natural: White has W1 false on it, and K1 false on Black referring to White; so White \"has\" two false statements. Blue has B1 true on it and W1 false about it => one false statement about Blue, not two. Black has K1 false on it => one. So White unique. This is a valid solution.\nAnswer Blue FTF: Blue box statement false (White empty). White statement true (Blue has gems). Black statement false because box with two false statements is Blue and it has gems. Blue has B1 false on it and K1 false about it => two; White has W1 true on it and B1 false about it => one; Black has K1 false => one. Also valid. Which one would a puzzle designer choose? Maybe they would choose White because the \"box with two false statements\" being White (middle) has two false statements **next to it?** Wait White is next to both Blue and Black; B1 true on Blue, K1 false on Black; not both false. Or White's own statement false and Black's statement about White false; Black is next to White? Yes Black is adjacent to White. Blue is not adjacent to Black; K1 on Black about Blue in G=Blue is not on a box next to Blue? Actually Black is not next to Blue, so Blue's two false statements are on itself (B1) and on a non-adjacent box (Black). White's two false statements in G=White are on itself (W1) and on adjacent Black (K1). If \"box with two false statements\" implied the false statements are on/near the box? It doesn't. But maybe the puzzle arranges boxes left-middle-right, and a box \"has\" statements written on boxes next to it? Then White has two adjacent boxes, could have two false statements from neighbors; Blue has only one neighbor, so cannot have two false statements from neighbors. If K1 is on Black, and White is adjacent to Black, then in G=White, White has W1 (on itself) false and K1 (on adjacent Black) false about White = two false statements, both physically on White or neighbor. In G=Blue, Blue has B1 (on itself) false and K1 (on Black) false about Blue, but Black is not adjacent to Blue; K1 is not on a neighbor. Maybe a box \"has\" false statements if they are written on the box itself or on boxes next to it? Then White would have two (W1 on itself, K1 on adjacent Black) in G=White; Blue would have B1 on itself but K1 on Black not adjacent, so only one? Wait S(X)=self+neighbors counts false statements on self and neighbors regardless of aboutness. For G=White,k=F:\nS(Blue)={B1,W1}: B1 true, W1 false => C_B=1.\nS(White)={W1,B1,K1}: W1 false, B1 true, K1 false => C_W=2.\nS(Black)={K1,W1}: K1 false, W1 false => C_K=2. Two boxes count2 (White and Black), not unique. So White not unique if counting self+neighbors; Black also has K1 false on self and W1 false on neighbor White =2. But Black does not have gems, so K1 would be true if X=White? Actually two boxes count2 -> no unique X -> K1 true, contradict k=F. So G=White k=F invalid under self+neighbors. Under own+about, White unique because only statements about White count, not W1 about Blue on neighbor.\n\nWhat if a box \"has\" false statements if they are written on the box itself or on boxes **next to it**, and K1's aboutness irrele