tements\" must have **two false statements displayed on it**, and since none, K1 is true always -> three solutions. But the puzzle claims unique; maybe one of three solutions violates \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE\" because K1 true always and G=Black gives B1=F,W1=F,K1=T => all-false boxes Blue/White, all-true Black. Valid. G=White gives B1=T,W1=F,K1=T => all-true Blue/Black, all-false White. Valid. G=Blue gives B1=F,W1=T,K1=T => all-false Blue, all-true White/Black. Valid. So truly underdetermined.\n\nCould there be a rule that \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" means exactly one box all-true, exactly one all-false, and the remaining is **not all-true nor all-false**? The word \"or a mix\" could imply the remaining is the only one that may be a mix, but it also may be all-true/all-false. If it meant exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false and the third is mix, then with one statement per box no solution, so not.\n\nWhat if \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE\" means at least one of the boxes with **multiple statements** has all true, but boxes with one statement don't count? No, \"ALL of its statements\" for one statement is just the statement. They count.\n\nLet's consider if \"the box with two false statements\" could refer to the box that has **two false statements among the statements in the entire puzzle, i.e., the box whose statement is false and there are exactly two false statements total, so the box is one of the two false boxes?** No.\n\nMaybe the final answer expected by the game is \"gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T\" under K1=true standard? But multiple. The user asks \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" perhaps they expect us to find the unique solution under the standard one-statement interpretation plus **K1 is false because the box with two false statements does not exist?** Wait if K1 false standard? No.\n\nLet's test all five possible assignments against a constraint \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" All five except FFF satisfy. If K1 true always -> three. If K1 false always -> two. If K1 depends on X own+about -> five? Actually own+about with no-X K1=true gives G=Blue FTT, G=White TFT, G=Black FFT plus G=Blue FTF, G=White TFF = five? Wait G=Black k=T under own+about had two boxes count2 no unique, K1 true; yes included in G=Black FFT. So five consistent assignments (all except FFF). So even more.\n\nThus to get unique, need additional constraints beyond stated. Maybe the \"rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" include something like \"The box with two false statements\" must exist and be unique, and **the gems are in the box with two false statements**? No, K1 says it is not true that it has gems; if K1 false then it has gems. But K1 could be true if gems elsewhere. If require K1 false? Not stated.\n\nLet's read user prompt: \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE ...\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\"\n\nThey assert these rules make puzzle uniquely solvable, implying given puzzle has unique solution under these rules. Maybe they expect us to use the rules to solve, not question uniqueness. There must be a unique solution if we interpret K1 correctly. What interpretation of K1 plus B1/W1 yields exactly one consistent assignment satisfying at least one all-true/all-false? Let's search for K1 semantics that yields one of five assignments only.\n\nWe can define K1's truth as a function of G,b,w,k such that equations have unique fixed point. There are many possible. Need infer intended from wording.\n\nK1: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\"\nThis is a statement about \"the box with two false statements.\" To determine which box has two false statements, we need count false statements associated with each box. The natural association is **statements displayed on the box**. Since each box displays one statement, no box has two false statements. Therefore K1 is true (because there is no box with two false statements that has gems). That yields three solutions. But maybe the rule \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE\" requires one box all-false; if K1 true always, all three solutions have at least one all-false. So no unique.\n\nCould it be that \"the box with two false statements\" refers to a box that has two false statements among the **three statements total**, i.e., the box that is **not the box with the true statement** when exactly two statements are false? Wait if there are exactly two false statements, then there are two boxes with false statements; neither has two. But one could say \"the box with two false statements\" = \"the box [that is associated with] the two false statements\" meaning the box that is the **only box not all-true?** No.\n\nWhat if \"two false statements\" means \"two statements that are false\" and \"the box with [those] two false statements\" means \"the box that contains the gems and is mentioned in both false statements\"? Let's see:\n- G=Blue FTF: false statements B1 (about White) and K1 (about Blue). Both false statements about? B1 about White, K1 about Blue. Not same.\n- G=White TFF: false W1 (about Blue), K1 (about White). Not same.\n- G=Black FFT: false B1 (about White), W1 (about Blue). Not same.\nNo.\n\nWhat if false statements B1 and W1 both false in G=Black; they are about White and Blue. The box with two false statements could be the **remaining box Black** because the two false statements are on the boxes next to Black? Black's neighbors: White (W1 false) and Blue (B1 false) -> Black has two neighboring boxes with false statements! Ah! This is the neighbor interpretation we tried: In G=Black, B1 false on Blue, W1 false on White, both adjacent to Black? Black is adjacent to White only, not Blue. Wait Black's neighbors: White only (middle). Blue is not adjacent to Black. So Black has only one neighbor (White) with W1 false; B1 false on Blue not adjacent to Black. So no.\nWhite's neighbors: Blue (B1 false) and Black (K1 true) -> one false neighbor.\nBlue's neighbors: White (W1 false) -> one false neighbor.\nSo no box with two false neighbor statements in G=Black,k=T. Under neighbor-count, no X, K1 true consistent.\n\nWhat if \"box with two false statements\" = box that has two false statements **among the statements written on boxes next to it plus its own**? We did self+neighbors; G=Black,k=T: S(Black)={K1 true,W1 false}=1; S(White)={W1 false,B1 false,K1 true}=2; S(Blue)={B1 false,W1 false}=2 -> two boxes count2, no unique -> K1 true. G=Blue,k=T: S(Blue)={B1 false,W1 true}=1; S(White)={W1 true,B1 false,K1 true}=1; S(Black)={K1 true,W1 true}=0 -> no X -> K1 true. G=White,k=T: S(Blue)={B1 true,W1 false}=1; S(White)={W1 false,B1 true,K1 true}=1; S(Black)={K1 true,W1 false}=1 -> no X -> K1 true. So K1 true all three.\n\nWhat if K1=false under self+neighbors? We found no solutions. So self+neighbors yields K1=true always, three solutions.\n\nWhat if \"box with two false statements\" = box that has two false statements **among statements about it** (aboutness) plus statements **about boxes next to it**? Hard.\n\nLet's attempt to find a semantics where only one of five assignments is consistent. We can set up equations for K1 as \"¬(G = argmax false count)\" with some count definition and see which assignment satisfies. There are five candidate assignments; we need count definition such that exactly one candidate has K1 matching.\n\nCandidate assignments:\n1. G=Blue, b=F,w=T,k=T (FTT)\n2. G=Blue, b=F,w=T,k=F (FTF)\n3. G=White, b=T,w=F,k=T (TFT)\n4. G=White, b=T,w=F,k=F (TFF)\n5. G=Black, b=F,w=F,k=T (FFT)\n\nFor each candidate, false statements set:\n1: {B1}\n2: {B1,K1}\n3: {W1}\n4: {W1,K1}\n5: {B1,W1}\n\nWe need define count per box such that \"the box with two false statements\" X exists and k=¬(G=X). In candidates 2 and 4, k=F => X=G and G has two false statements. In candidates 1,3,5, k=T => either no X or X exists with G≠X.\n\nCandidate 1 (G=Blue FTT): only B1 false. No box can have two false statements if count based on statements; so no X -> K1 true. Valid under standard no-X true.\nCandidate 3 (G=White TFT): only W1 false -> no X -> K1 true.\nCandidate 5 (G=Black FFT): B1 and W1 false. If count based on aboutness: B1 about White false, W1 about Blue false -> Blue and White each one false; Black zero -> no X -> K1 true. If count based on writer: Blue has B1 false, White has W1 false -> no X. If count based on self+neighbors: S(Blue)={B1,W1} both false ->2; S(White)={W1,B1} both false ->2; S(Black)={K1,W1} W1 false ->1; two X no unique -> K1 true. If count based on neighbor statements: White neighbors B1,K1 -> B1 false, K1 true =>1; Blue neighbor W1 false=>1; Black neighbor W1 false=>1 -> no X. So K1 true valid.\nCandidate 2 (G=Blue FTF): false B1,K1. Need X=Blue with two false statements. Possible count definitions:\n- Writer+about: Blue has B1 on it false; K1 about Blue false =>2. White has W1 on? W1 true; B1 about White false =>1. Black has K1 on false =>1. Unique X=Blue. k=F matches G=X.\n- Writer only: Blue B1 false=1; Black K1 false=1; no X -> K1 true, not candidate.\n- About only: Blue W1 true, K1 about Blue false =>1; White B1 false=>1; Black none => no X.\n- Self+neighbors: Blue S={B1,W1}: B1 false,W1 true =>1; White S={W1,B1,K1}: W1 true,B1 false,K1 false =>2; Black S={K1,W1}: K1 false,W1 true =>1 => unique X=White, k=¬(Blue=White)=true, not candidate.\n- Neighbor only: Blue neighbor W1 true=>0; White neighbors B1 false,K1 false=>2; Black neighbor W1 true=>0 => unique X=White, k=true, not candidate.\nSo only writer+about (own+about) supports candidate 2.\nCandidate 4 (G=White TFF): false W1,K1. Need X=White with two false statements.\n- Writer+about: White has W1 on false; K1 about White false =>2. Blue has B1 on true; W1 about Blue false=>1. Black has K1 on false=>1. Unique X=White. k=F matches.\n- Writer only: White W1 false=1; Black K1 false=1 no X.\n- About only: Blue W1 false=1; White B1 true + K1 about White false=1; Black none => no X.\n- Self+neighbors: White S={W1,B1,K1}: W1 false,B1 true,K1 false=>2; Black S={K1,W1}: K1 false,W1 false=>2; Blue S={B1,W1}: B1 true,W1 false=>1 => two X (White,Black) no unique -> K1 true, not candidate.\n- Neighbor only: Blue neighbor W1 false=>1; White neighbors B1 true,K1 false=>1; Black neighbor W1 false=>1 => no X.\nSo writer+about supports candidate 4 as well.\n\nThus own+about uniquely supports k=F candidates 2 and 4, but both.\n\nWhat asymmetry could eliminate candidate 2 or 4? Candidate 2 (G=Blue) has K1 false about Blue; Blue's own statement B1 false about White; White's statement W1 true about Blue; Black's K1 false about Blue. Candidate 4 (G=White) has K1 false about White; White's own W1 false about Blue; Blue's B1 true about White; Black's K1 false about White. Both involve K1 false about the gem box, and the gem box's own statement false about the other non-gem box? Wait:\n- G=Blue: Gem box Blue's own B1 is false about White (the other non-gem). White's W1 true about Blue. So gem box's own statement is false; other non-gem White's statement true.\n- G=White: Gem box White's own W1 is false about Blue (the other non-gem). Blue's B1 true about White. So gem box's own statement false; other non-gem Blue's statement true.\nSymmetric.\n\nWhat if B1 \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" for Blue when G=White is true because White (next to Blue) contains gems. When G=Blue, B1 false because White empty. W1 \"THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\" when G=White false, when G=Blue true. So truth values just swap. The statements are symmetric under exchanging Blue and White labels AND swapping B1/W1 contents? Actually if exchange Blue and White labels, B1 on new Blue (old White) would be \"Boxes next to this box contain gems\" = old White's neighbors Blue and Black contain gems, which is not same as W1. But the content of statements as given are not symmetric under label swap because B1 depends on White's location relative to Blue (one neighbor), W1 depends on Blue only. However, the truth conditions B1=(G=White) and W1=(G=Blue) are symmetric under swapping G=Blue and G=White and swapping B1/W1 truth values. The rest of world (Black) is unaffected except K1 about X. So two solutions mirror.\n\nCould the physical arrangement break symmetry because White is middle adjacent to both Blue and Black, while Blue is left adjacent only to White? B1's truth condition uses only White's gem status, not White's neighbors. W1 uses Blue's gem status. So B1 and W1 truth conditions are symmetric: each is true iff the other named box has gems. The fact White has another neighbor Black is irrelevant to B1/W1 except K1 on Black. K1's aboutness in candidate 4: X=White, and K1 is on Black, which is adjacent to White. In candidate 2: X=Blue, and K1 is on Black, which is not adjacent to Blue. If the phrase \"the box with two false statements\" somehow implies the box is associated with two false statements **on boxes next to it or itself**, then candidate 4 more natural because White's two false statements are W1 on itself and K1 on adjacent Black; candidate 2's two false statements are B1 on itself and K1 on non-adjacent Black. But the statement doesn't mention adjacency; however the puzzle setup gives positions maybe relevant only for B1. Could K1's \"box with two false statements\" be intended as a box that has two false statements **written on it or on boxes next to it**? We tried self+neighbors and candidate 4 invalid because Black also had two false statements (K1 on itself, W1 on neighbor White). Wait if \"has\" means written on it OR on boxes next to it, Black in candidate 