e G=White: W1=F; B1=F. To have at least one all-true, K1 would have to be T. But then false statements B1,W1 give two boxes (Blue and White) each with two false statements (own+about), so \"the box with two false statements\" is not unique; K1 cannot be a well-defined true statement. If K1=F, all statements false, violating all-true requirement. So G≠White.\n- Assume G=Black: W1=F; B1=F; same as G=White for B1/W1; K1? If K1=T, two boxes with two false statements non-unique => invalid; if K1=F, all false invalid. So G≠Black.\n- Therefore G=Blue. Then W1=T, B1=F. With only B1 false, no box has two false statements, so K1 cannot be true (definite description fails); K1=F. Then false statements B1 and K1; Blue has B1 false on it and K1 false about it => unique box with two false statements is Blue, which has gems, making K1 false consistent.\nThis reasoning uses uniqueness presupposition and own+about. We should state \"the only way K1 has a determinate truth value is if exactly one box has two false statements\" maybe.\n\nNeed be careful: In G=White with K1=T, there are two boxes with two false statements (Blue and White), so \"the box\" non-unique -> K1 not determinable; since every statement must be T/F, invalid. In G=Black with K1=T same.\nIf G=White with K1=F, all three false => no all-true invalid.\nThus unique.\n\nLet's write final answer with final line exact.", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- BLUE is leftmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. The statement B1 says “BOXES” plural, but there is only one box next to BLUE; therefore B1 is false.\n- If the gems were in WHITE, then W1 would be false and B1 already false; to have an all-true box, K1 would need to be true, but then two boxes would have two false statements, making “the box with two false statements” non-unique. If K1 were false, no box would be all-true. So gems are not in WHITE.\n- If the gems were in BLACK, the same problem occurs: B1 and W1 false, and K1 cannot be uniquely true/false consistently. So gems are not in BLACK.\n- Therefore gems are in BLUE. Then W1 is true, B1 is false.\n- The only way K1 is determinate is if the unique box with two false statements is BLUE: B1 is false on BLUE, and K1 is false about BLUE. Since BLUE has the gems, K1’s claim “It is not true that BLUE has the gems” is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}
