box has two false statements).\n- W1 true because adjacent Black box is true (K1 true).\n- K1 true because Black box true and gems in Blue, which is false.\nGem in Blue. Exactly one gems. At least one all-true: White (W1 true) and Black (K1 true) are all-true boxes; Blue all-false. At least one all-false: Blue. Great.\n\nNeed present step-by-step reasoning concise.\n\nBut need be cautious: The problem statement says \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Since each box has one statement, \"all statements true/false\" means statement true/false. Our solution has Blue false, White true, Black true -> at least one true (W,K), at least one false (B). Good.\n\nLet's prove uniqueness:\nAssume B1 true? If B1 true, then there exists/refers to box with two false statements that has gems. But each box has exactly one statement, so no box has two false statements; hence B1 cannot be true. Thus B1=false.\nThen Blue box is false. Since at least one box true and at least one false already satisfied by Blue, at least one of White/Black must be true.\nW1 = \"A box next to this box is true\": neighbors Blue and Black. Blue is false, so W1 is true iff Black is true. Thus W=K.\nIf both W and K false, then no true box, violating at least one true. So W=K=true.\nNow K1 true = \"Black box is true AND gems are in a box that is false.\" Since K=true, first conjunct true; so gems must be in a false box. The only false box is Blue (B false, W/K true). Therefore gems = Blue.\nThen B1 false consistent: no box has two false statements (there are only one false statement? Wait there are B=false, W=true, K=true -> exactly one false statement total, not two; no box with two false statements). Good.\nActually B1 false because there is no box with two false statements, not because exactly one false statement. There is only one false statement total, B1 itself. So yes.\n\nNeed final output exact format:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T\n\nNeed ensure \"VERY LAST line output final answer exactly one-line format (nothing after it)\" So final response can include reasoning then final line. Need include step-by-step reasoning, then final line. The final line should be exactly as specified, nothing after. We'll do reasoning in paragraphs before.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n- Each box has only one statement, so a \"box with two false statements\" cannot exist; therefore B1 cannot be true -> B1=F. (Alternatively B1 would assert existence of object that doesn't exist, false.)\n- Blue false. At least one true required, so not both W,K false.\n- W1 is true iff at least one neighbor (Blue or Black) is true. Blue is false, so W1 = K1. Hence W and K same truth value.\n- Since not both false, W=K=true.\n- K1 = \"Black is true AND gems in a false box.\" K true, so gems must be in a false box. Only false box is Blue. Hence gems=Blue.\n- Check consistency: B1 false; W1 true because Black neighbor true; K1 true because Black true and gems in Blue (false). At least one true box, at least one false box satisfied.\n\nNeed final line.", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement, so no box can have “two false statements.” Therefore B1 must be false.\n\nSince B1 is false, Blue is a false box. At least one box must be true, so White or Black must be true.  \nW1 says “a box next to this box is true,” meaning either Blue or Black is true. Blue is false, so W1 is true exactly when Black is true. Thus W1 and K1 have the same truth value. To satisfy “at least one true box,” both W1 and K1 must be true.\n\nK1 says “Black is true and the gems are in a box that is false.” Black is true, so the gems must be in a false box. The only false box is Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
