e'll put reasoning then final line last.\n\nNeed be concise but deductive. Maybe mention \"I include the implicit 'this box has the gems' statement as a statement of each box, because otherwise the phrase 'box with two false statements' cannot refer to any box.\" This is important.\n\nLet's craft reasoning step by step:\n- Let b,w,k be truth values of B1,W1,K1.\n- Let G_B,G_W,G_K be implicit gem claims; exactly one true.\n- B1 is always false: a box with two false statements would have both its displayed statement false and its gem claim false, hence it would be empty, so it cannot also have the gems. Thus b=F.\n- K1 true iff W1 false and White empty: k = ¬w ∧ ¬G_W.\n- W1 true iff exactly two of all six statements are true. Since exactly one gem claim is true, W1 true iff exactly one of b,w,k is true: w = (b+w+k == 1).\n- With b=F, w = (w+k ==1). Possibilities:\n  1. w=F => k=F. Then k=F with w=F forces G_W=T (White has gems) because k = ¬F ∧ ¬G_W = ¬G_W; for k=F, G_W=T. Then no box is all-true (White is mixed: W1 F, G_W T; Blue/Black all false), violating at least one all-true.\n  2. w=T => k=F. Then k=F consistent; to have an all-true box, White must contain gems (G_W=T), making White all-true. Blue/Black all-false. Valid.\n- Therefore gems in White; b=F, w=T, k=F.\n\nWait in possibility 1: k=F and w=F => G_W=T (White has gems). Then White has W1 false and G_W true => mixed. Blue has B1 false and G_B false => all false. Black has K1 false and G_K false => all false. No all-true. Invalid indeed.\nIn possibility 2: w=T,k=F => k=F = ¬T ∧ ¬G_W = 0 regardless G_W, so G_W could be T or F. But need at least one all-true: White has W1 true; to be all true, G_W must be true => White gems. If G_W false, White mixed; Blue/Black all false => no all-true. Thus G_W=T. Then exactly one gem White. Valid.\nThis is solid.\n\nNeed also mention \"The remaining box may be all-false; here Blue and Black are both all-false, so condition satisfied.\" Actually rule says \"remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" meaning after one all-true and one all-false, the third can be all-false. Both Blue/Black all-false okay.\n\nLet's write final response with moderate detail, no excessive.\n\nPotential issue: The user requested \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1\" in final line. We will only list those.\n\nLet's produce final answer.", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three displayed statements. Also treat each box as implicitly asserting “this box has the gems”; exactly one such implicit claim is true.\n\n- A box with two false statements would have its displayed statement false and its implicit gem-claim false, so it is empty. Therefore it cannot be the box containing the gems, so \\(B1\\) is always false.\n- \\(K1\\) says the White box has exactly two false statements. That would require \\(W1\\) false and White empty, so \\(K1\\) is true only in that case.\n- \\(W1\\) says exactly two statements are true total. Since exactly one implicit gem-claim is true, \\(W1\\) is true exactly when exactly one displayed statement is true.\n\nWith \\(B1 = F\\), the only consistent assignment that also gives at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box is:\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n- \\(K1 = F\\)\n- White has the gems.\n\nThen White has both its statements true, while Blue and Black are all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}
