 box\" = \"the two boxes other than the black box\", so Blue and White empty. Thus black contains gems. This yields straightforward.\n\nLet's test all possible gem locations with rules:\nIf gem=BLACK:\nB1: Blue and White empty -> true.\nW1: B1 true -> true.\nK1: White box is blue -> false.\nTruth pattern: B1 T, W1 T, K1 F. At least one T, at least one F. Valid.\nIf gem=WHITE:\nB1: Blue empty yes, White empty no -> false (both must be empty).\nW1: B1 false -> false.\nK1: false.\nAll false -> violates at least one all-true. Invalid.\nIf gem=BLUE:\nB1: Blue empty no -> false.\nW1: false.\nK1: false.\nAll false -> invalid.\nUnique.\n\nNeed final answer.\n\nNeed include step-by-step reasoning. Then final line exactly.\n\nLet's craft concise but deductive. Mention B1 iff gems in BLACK; W1 = B1; K1 false. Then rule at least one true eliminates BLUE/WHITE gem cases; BLACK gem gives T,T,F valid. Final: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure final line exactly \"FINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\" nothing after. Good.", "content": "B1 means the Blue and White boxes are empty, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are in the Black box.  \nW1 says B1 is true, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.  \nK1 says “the White box is Blue,” which is false.\n\nIf the gems were in Blue or White, then B1 and W1 would both be false, and K1 would also be false, leaving no box with a true statement. That violates the rule.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in Black, making B1 true, W1 true, and K1 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
