 are not colored? Actually gem location is one box, not specific colored gem? The statement \"THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\" in color puzzle likely false because white box's color is white. But let's ensure not \"White box is [the] Blue [box]\"? There are boxes labeled BLUE, WHITE, BLACK; white box is not blue. So false.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise, then final line exact.\n\nNeed maybe mention statement truth values:\nB1 = T because if gems in Black, Blue and White empty.\nW1 = T because B1 true.\nK1 = F because White box's color is white, not blue.\n\nLet's make sure at least one all-true: Blue box all true (B1 T), White box all true (W1 T). At least one all-false: Black box all false (K1 F). Remaining box? Actually remaining box after all-true and all-false? There are two all-true (Blue, White) and one all-false (Black). Rule says at least one all-true, at least one all-false; remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix. Here Blue and White both all-true okay, Black all-false; no \"remaining\" issue. Exactly one gem: Black.\n\nPotential nuance: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" This wording might imply exactly one all-true box? Actually \"at least one\" and \"remaining box\" could mean among three boxes, one is all-true, one all-false, third arbitrary. It doesn't forbid more than one all-true/all-false? It says \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" If there are two all-true and one all-false, then which is \"remaining\"? The remaining box after selecting one all-true and one all-false would be all-true, allowed. So okay. But if puzzle designer intended exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false? Wording \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Could be read as: There is at least one all-true, at least one all-false, and the third (remaining) may be anything. It doesn't explicitly require exactly one all-true/all-false, but \"remaining\" suggests there are designated true/false boxes, third arbitrary. If two all-true and one all-false, there is no unique \"remaining\" but third could be all-true. Usually allowed. Our solution has Blue and White all-true, Black all-false; satisfies.\n\nCould there be alternative solution if W1 references \"statement above this one\" spatially: White box middle; \"above this one\" could refer to statement above in same box? But White has only one statement, so \"above this one\" cannot refer to within White box. It likely refers to statement in Blue box because Blue is above/left? Actually boxes arranged left/middle/right horizontally, so \"above\" might mean \"previous\" in reading order: above = Blue's statement. The text \"BLUE (left) box: [B1] ... WHITE (middle) box: [W1] ...\" In vertical list, B1 is above W1. So yes.\n\nLet's perhaps in reasoning state \"W1 refers to B1 (the statement above it in the list).\"\n\nNeed maybe address \"The other two boxes relative to the Black box are empty\" = Blue and White are empty, equivalent to gems in Black. Good.\n\nLet's craft final answer with step-by-step but concise. Final line exactly.\n\nPotential issue: final answer format asks \"gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\" So final line: FINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\nNeed no extra after. Ensure exactly one line.", "content": "Let:\n- B1: “The other two boxes relative to the Black box are empty” means Blue and White are empty, so it is true iff the gems are in Black.\n- W1: “The statement above this one is true” refers to B1, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n- K1: “The White box is blue” is false, since the White box is white.\n\nIf the gems were not in Black, then B1 and W1 would be false, and K1 is already false, leaving no all-true box. Therefore the gems must be in Black.\n\nThen B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
