ts TRUE.\n- At least one box has ALL statements FALSE.\n- Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix.\n- Statement truth values determined by actual state including self/cross-references.\n\nNeed determine gem location and truth values every statement.\n\nWe have 4 statements: B1, W1, W2, K1. K1=F fixed. Box truth values:\nBLUE box has B1 only. So blue box all-true iff B1=T; all-false iff B1=F. Since single statement, it's either all-true or all-false (no mix).\nWHITE box has W1,W2. All-true iff W1=T and W2=T. All-false iff W1=F and W2=F. Mixed if one T one F.\nBLACK box has K1 only. K1=F => black box all-false (since single false statement). So black box all-false always.\n\nGiven at least one box all-true: could be blue if B1=T and/or white if W1=W2=T. At least one all-false: black is all-false satisfies. So okay.\n\nNeed solve consistent truth values.\n\nLet's denote:\nB1 = \"other two boxes empty\" = white empty and black empty = since exactly one gem, white empty and black empty iff gem in blue. Because exactly one of three contains gems; if white and black empty, gems must be in blue. Conversely if gem in blue, other two empty. So B1 = G_BLUE, where G_BLUE true iff gem in blue. So B1 = (gem=BLUE).\n\nW1 = \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" = B1 is true = B1.\nW2 = \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" = W1 is true = W1.\nK1 = \"BLACK BOX IS MIDDLE BOX\" = false always.\n\nThus equations:\nB1 = G_B (unknown boolean)\nW1 = B1 = G_B\nW2 = W1 = G_B\nK1 = F\n\nThen white box statements W1=W2=G_B. So if gem in blue, B1=T, W1=T, W2=T, K1=F. If gem not in blue, B1=F, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F.\n\nNow check rules:\nExactly one gem: either blue, white, black.\nAt least one box all-true:\n- If gem blue: Blue all-true (B1=T), White all-true (W1=T,W2=T), Black all-false (K1=F). Satisfies at least one all-true yes two.\n- If gem white: B1=F, W1=F,W2=F, K1=F => Blue all-false, White all-false, Black all-false. No all-true box. Violates at least one all-true.\n- If gem black: B1=F, W1=F,W2=F, K1=F => same no all-true. Violates.\nSo gem must be BLUE.\n\nThen final B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure no subtlety: K1 \"BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" is false because black box is right. Yes.\n\nNeed maybe consider \"other two boxes are empty\" could mean \"the other two boxes [besides blue] contain no statements\"? No, \"empty\" in context means contain no gems. Yes.\n\nNeed craft step-by-step reasoning and final exact line. The user asks \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\". We can include reasoning before final, then final line exactly. Need ensure final line has no extra after. Use FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F\n\nNeed verify statement IDs only these: B1,W1,W2,K1. Good.\n\nLet's produce concise deductive reasoning.\n\nNeed maybe note \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE\" black box satisfies because K1 false. The remaining box may mix but white/blue all same.\n\nLet's answer.", "content": "Let B1 mean “the other two boxes are empty,” which is true exactly when the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\n- K1 says “BLACK box is the MIDDLE box,” but BLACK is on the right, so K1 is always FALSE.\n- W1 says “B1 is true,” so W1 = B1.\n- W2 says “W1 is true,” so W2 = W1 = B1.\n\nSo:\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=T, W1=T, W2=T, K1=F.  \n  BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n- If gems are in WHITE or BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F.  \n  Then no box is all-true, violating the rule that at least one box must be all-true.\n\nTherefore the gems are in BLUE and all statements are determined.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}
