ements. The statement asserts that the unique box with two false statements contains gems. Since only black can have two false statements, K2 truth value = \"The black box has two false statements (i.e., both K1 and K2 are false) AND the black box contains gems.\" This is self-referential: K2 = (K1 false and K2 false) AND gem=BLACK. Let's examine. If gem=BLACK and K1 false? K1 = black empty = false when gem=black. K2 would be true iff (K1 false & K2 false) implies K2 false and K1 false -> K2 true? Wait equation: K2 = (not K1 AND not K2) AND (gem=BLACK). If gem=BLACK, then K2 = (not K1 AND not K2). Since K1=false (black not empty), not K1=true, so K2 = not K2, impossible. Thus gem=BLACK cannot yield consistent K2? Let's check: K1 false because black contains gems. K2 statement: \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" If K1 false, K2 false => black has two false statements and has gems => statement true, contradiction K2 false. If K1 false, K2 true => black does NOT have two false statements (only one false) and has gems, statement false, contradiction K2 true. So gem=BLACK impossible due K2 self-reference under K1 false. Good.\n\nNeed solve overall.\n\nLet's denote gem location G ∈ {B(blue), W(white), K(black)}.\n\nStatements:\nB1 = (G = WHITE) because only neighbor of blue is white.\nW1 = (G ≠ BLACK) i.e. black empty.\nK1 = (G ≠ BLACK) same as W1.\nK2 = \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\". Need formalize.\n\nBecause only black has 2 statements, the only possible box with two false statements is black. But there may be zero boxes with exactly two false statements (if black not both false). Statement K2 can be interpreted as: The unique box that has two false statements (if any) contains gems. If no box has exactly two false statements, then \"the box with two false statements\" is undefined/false? In logic puzzles, definite description false if non-existent. Also if box with two false statements exists but not black? impossible. So K2 = (K1 is false AND K2 is false) AND (G = BLACK). But wait if black has both K1 false and K2 false, then black has two false statements. Then K2 would assert black has gems. So K2 = (not K1 and not K2) and G=BLACK. This is as above.\n\nAlternatively K2 could mean \"The box with two false statements [i.e., black] has the gems\" without requiring black actually has two false statements? Definite description presupposes existence; truth value false if nonexistent. So same.\n\nHowever, there is subtlety: \"box with two false statements\" might refer to a box that has exactly two false statements among its displayed statements. Since blue/white have one statement each, they cannot have two false statements. So black only candidate. So K2 true iff black has exactly two false statements and contains gems. Since K1 false iff G=BLACK. K2 false iff not both? Let's solve consistent assignments for G and truth values.\n\nWe need satisfy global constraints:\n- exactly one gem box.\n- At least one box all statements true.\n- At least one box all statements false.\n- Remaining box may all-true, all-false, mix.\n\nWe need find unique solution.\n\nLet's examine each gem case.\n\nCase G = WHITE:\nThen BLACK is empty, so W1 = true, K1 = true.\nB1 = \"box next to blue contains gems\": blue neighbor is white; white contains gems => B1 = true.\nK2: black has statements K1=true, K2=?. Black has two false statements? Since K1 true, black cannot have two false statements (max one false). So \"box with two false statements\" does not exist; K2 = false. Thus K2=false.\nTruth values:\nBlue: B1=true -> all true box (blue all-true).\nWhite: W1=true -> all true.\nBlack: K1=true, K2=false -> mix.\nGlobal: at least one all-true yes (blue/white); at least one all-false? None. Violates \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\" So G=WHITE invalid if require at least one all-false. Unless maybe K2 true? Let's check if K2 could be true despite no box with two false statements? It says THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS. If no box with two false statements, definite description false. So K2 false. Thus no all-false. Invalid.\n\nCase G = BLACK:\nThen black not empty => W1=false, K1=false.\nB1 = \"white contains gems?\" false because G=BLACK.\nK2 equation as above: K2 = (K1 false and K2 false) AND (G=BLACK) = (not K2) because K1 false and G=BLACK. So K2 = not K2 impossible. Thus no consistent truth value. Invalid.\n\nCase G = BLUE:\nThen blue contains gems, white empty, black empty.\nB1 = \"white contains gems?\" false.\nW1 = \"black empty?\" true.\nK1 = \"black empty?\" true.\nK2: black statements K1=true, K2=?; black cannot have two false statements because K1 true. Thus K2 = false (definite description nonexistent or black no two false). Truth values:\nBlue: B1=false -> all-false box (since only statement false) yes.\nWhite: W1=true -> all-true box yes.\nBlack: K1=true, K2=false -> mix.\nGlobal constraints satisfied: at least one all-true (white), at least one all-false (blue), remaining black mix. Unique solution G=BLUE, B1=F, W1=T, K1=T, K2=F.\n\nThis seems straightforward. But need ensure \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX\" could include both white and black? Wait \"next to the blue box\" - blue is leftmost, in row of three boxes, boxes next to it: only white? But some might consider a box next to blue as any box adjacent: only white. Black is not next to blue because white between. So B1 = white contains gems. Good.\n\nNeed consider alternative interpretation: The three boxes are maybe not in spatial order? The prompt says BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). So yes.\n\nNeed maybe analyze \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" etc with \"counting a one-statement box as all-true if its statement true, all-false if false.\" Yes.\n\nNeed ensure K2 formalization maybe has another possible interpretation: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" could refer to \"the box that has two false statements\" among the boxes; since black has two statements, if both K1 and K2 false then black has two false statements. But K2 itself is a statement; if K2 false, then black has K1 false and K2 false => two false statements; then K2 would be true if black has gems. This leads impossibility if gem=black. If K2 true, then black does not have two false statements? Actually if K2 true, black statements: K1 true? In G=BLACK K1 true, K2 true => black all-true, no two false; K2 true asserts \"the box with two false statements has gems.\" But no box with two false statements exists, so K2 should be false. Contradiction. In G=WHITE K1 true, K2 false -> black one false; no two false; K2 false. In G=BLUE same. Good.\n\nNeed check if there is any scenario with G=WHITE where black could be all-false if K2 true? No because K1 true. So at least one all-false fails.\n\nPotential issue: W1 and K1 both \"THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\": If G=WHITE or BLUE, black empty, true. If G=BLACK, false. Fine.\n\nNeed perhaps prove uniqueness more rigorously. The final answer expected just final line? User asked think step by step and final line exact. We can provide reasoning concise. Need final line exact one-line: FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F. Ensure no extra after. Could include reasoning before final. Final must be last line exactly. Need no extra after final line.\n\nNeed maybe mention K2 truth value derivation: Since only BLACK has two statements, \"box with two false statements\" can only be BLACK. If BLACK contained gems, K1 false and K2 would have to equal \"K1 and K2 both false\", impossible (K2 = not K2). Therefore BLACK cannot contain gems. Then W1/K1 true if gems not in black. If gems in WHITE, B1 true, W1 true, K1 true, K2 false, yielding no all-false box (blue true, white true, black mix) violating at least one all-false. Thus gems must be BLUE. Then B1 false, W1 true, K1 true, K2 false, with blue all-false, white all-true, black mix.\n\nWait the impossibility for G=BLACK: If G=BLACK, K1=false. Then K2 statement: \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" If K2=false, then black has K1 false and K2 false -> two false statements and has gems -> K2 would be true. If K2=true, then black does not have two false statements (only K1 false) and has gems -> K2 false. Thus no consistent K2. So BLACK impossible.\n\nNeed perhaps address K2 in G=WHITE or BLUE: Since K1 true, black can't have two false statements (only K2 could be false, one false max). Thus K2 false because there is no box with two false statements? Wait if K2 false, black has one false only; no box with two false statements (blue/white have one each cannot). So definite description false; K2 false consistent. Good.\n\nLet's ensure no other all-false box in G=WHITE: Blue B1 true; White W1 true; Black K1 true K2 false => mix. None all-false. So invalid.\n\nAll good.\n\nNeed produce final answer with reasoning.\n\nBut let's thoroughly consider if there could be self-referential paradoxes with K2 in other cases? In G=BLUE, K1 true, K2 false; black has one false. There is no box with two false statements, so K2 false. Fine.\n\nPotential ambiguity: Statement K2 \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" could mean \"There is a box with two false statements and it has the gems.\" In G=BLUE, black has one false (K2 only), so no box with two false statements; K2 false. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" and \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE\" might allow same box to be both? No, a box cannot be all-true and all-false unless zero statements (not case). So need distinct? Actually at least one each, could be same if no statements? Not relevant. Blue has one false, white true, black mix: blue all-false, white all-true. Good.\n\nNeed maybe consider \"statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2\" important for K2.\n\nLet's craft reasoning step-by-step:\n\n1. Because BLUE is leftmost, its only next-door box is WHITE. So B1 is true iff gems in WHITE.\n2. W1 and K1 are identical: true iff gems are not in BLACK (black empty).\n3. K2 refers to \"the box with two false statements.\" Only BLACK has two statements, so if any such box exists, it is BLACK. Thus K2 is true only if BLACK's two statements are both false and BLACK contains the gems.\n4. Test G=BLACK: Then K1=false. K2 would be true iff K1=false and K2=false and G=BLACK, i.e. iff K2 is false. That is a contradiction (K2 = not K2). Hence G=BLACK impossible.\n5. Test G=WHITE: B1=true, W1=true, K1=true. Since K1 true, BLACK cannot have two false statements, so K2=false. Then no box is all-false (BLUE all-true, WHITE all-true, BLACK mixed). Violates at-least-one-all-false. So G=WHITE impossible.\n6. Therefore G=BLUE. Then B1=false (white doesn't have gems), W1=true (black empty), K1=true (black empty), K2=false (no box with two false statements, or black has only K2 false). Patterns: BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, BLACK mixed. Satisfies all rules.\n\nNeed final line.\n\nLet's ensure final answer format exactly: FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F\n\nNeed no final period after. Good.\n\nLet's produce final with reasoning and final line.", "content": "Let the gem locations be tested.\n\n- BLUE’s only neighboring box is WHITE, so B1 means “WHITE contains the gems.”\n- W1 and K1 both mean “BLACK is empty,” i.e. gems are not in BLACK.\n- Only BLACK has two statements, so “the box with two false statements” can only be BLACK.\n- If gems were in BLACK, then K1 would be false, and K2 would be true exactly when K1 and K2 are both false — a contradiction. So BLACK cannot contain the gems.\n- If gems were in WHITE, then B1, W1, and K1 would all be true, and K2 would be false. Then no box would be all-false, violating a rule.\n- Therefore gems must be in BLUE. Then B1 is false, W1 is true, K1 is true, and K2 is false. This gives BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, and BLACK mixed.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00352", "logic_attempt": 1, "timestamp": "2026-08-24T15:33:38", "verdict": {"parse_ok": false, "gem": null, "gem_correct": false, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": false, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": null}, "reasoning": "We need answer solve logic puzzle. Need think carefully. Need obey user asks step by step then final line exact format. Need solve.\n\nWe have three boxes: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. Statements:\nB1: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\" i.e. Not(all statements on Black are true). Equivalent Black has at least one false statement (K1 or K2 false).\nW1: \"THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\" Blue box has one statement B1, so at most one true statement means B1 is false? Since Blue has exactly one statement, at most one true always? Wait \"at most one true statement\" for a box with one statement is always true (0 or 1 true). So W1 is tautologically true if considering statement count? Need be careful: The statement says \"The blue box has at most one true statement.\" Blue box contains only B1, so number of true statements in Blue box is either 0 or 1, always at most one. Thus W1 is true regardless of B1? Usually yes, because a single statement box can't have more than one true statement. So W1 = T always.\nK1: \"THIS BOX IS BLACK\" The Black box (right) is indeed black color? In puzzle, boxes labeled BLUE, WHITE, BLACK likely colors. The black box is black, so K1 is true? But wait \"THIS BOX IS BLACK\" refers to this box (Black right box) is black. It's true by identity? But maybe if color of box is black. Usually yes K1 = T. But need consider \"black box\" means the one named BLACK, and it is black. So K1 T.\nK2: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" There are total 4 statements: B1, W1, K1, K2. Boxes: Blue has 1 stmt, White has 1 stmt, Black has 2 stmts. \"The box with two false statements\" refers to box (among three) containing two false statements. Only Black box has two statements, so only Black box could have two false statements (Blue/White have one each, cannot have two false statements). Thus \"the box with two false statements\" if exists must be Black box. The statement says that box has the gems. Thus K2 is equivalent to \"Black box has gems\" if there is a box with two false statements, but if no box has two false statements then reference maybe nonexistent? Need evaluate truth value in self-referential logic? Need analyze.\n\nRules: Exactly one box contains gems; at least one box all true; at least one all false; remaining may be mix. Statement truth values determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self/cross refs). Need assign gem location and truth values consistent.\n\nNeed determine unique solution.\n\nLet's formalize.\n\nBoxes:\nBlue: statements [B1]. Number true in Blue: tB = 1 if B1=True else 0.\nWhite: [W1]. tW = 1 if W1=True else 0.\nBlack: [K1, K2]. tK = number of true among K1,K2 = K1+K2 (T=1,F=0).\n\nAt least one box all-true: means a box with all statements true:\nBlue all-true iff B1=T.\nWhite all-true iff W1=T.\nBlack all-true iff K1=T and K2=T.\n\nAt least one box all-false:\nBlue all-false iff B1=F.\nWhite all-false iff W1=F.\nBlack all-false iff K1=F and K2=F.\n\nRemaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix.\n\nStatements:\nB1 = ¬(all statements on Black are true) = ¬(K1 ∧ K2) = ¬K1 ∨ ¬K2.\nW1 = \"The Blue box has at most one true statement\" = tB ≤ 1. Since tB ∈ {0,1}, always true. So W1 = True.\nK1 = \"This box is Black\" = True? Need likely fixed true due to box identity. But in these puzzles, could be true because box labeled BLACK is black. We can treat K1 = True. But let's examine if K1 can be false? The box is black; statement \"This box is Black\" true. Unless \"This box is BLACK\" refers to box named BLACK? It is indeed BLACK box. So K1 = True fixed. However, sometimes self-referential statements can be about color? It seems K1 is a factual statement about box color, true. We'll assume K1 = T. But need verify uniqueness with K1 variable maybe. We'll consider both.\n\nK2 = \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" Need define. There are boxes with number of false statements: Blue fB = 1 - B1; White fW = 1 - W1; Black fK = 2 - (K1+K2). Only Black can have exactly two false statements because only Black has two statements; Blue/White cannot have two false statements because only one statement. So \"the box with two false statements\" refers to Black if Black has two false statements (K1=F and K2=F); otherwise no such box exists. How to evaluate K2 if no box has two false statements? The definite description \"the box with two false statements\" might be vacuously? In classic logic, \"The F is G\" is true if there exists unique F and it is G; false if no unique F or not G. But in self-referential puzzles, statements about nonexistent referents? Need infer from puzzle wording: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" means if there is a box with exactly two false statements, then that box has gems; if no such box, statement is false? Usually in logic puzzles, definite descriptions need existence; but since only Black can have two false statements, if Black doesn't have two false statements, then \"the box with two false statements\" doesn't exist, making K2 false? However some puzzles allow \"the box with two false statements\" refers to the box that has two false statements; if none, statement false. Need check consistency with unique solvability.\n\nBut also there is rule exactly one box contains gems; the two false statements box (if any) has gems. Let's derive.\n\nLet K1 = T (color). Then Black has K1 true and K2 variable, so Black cannot have two false statements (only K2 could be false, K1 true => exactly one false). Therefore \"the box with two false statements\" does not exist. Then K2 likely false. But if K2 false, Black has one true (K1) and one false (K2)