nW1=T.\nBlue all-true (B1=T); White all-true (W1=T); Black all-false (K1=F,K2=F). At least one all-true yes; at least one all-false yes. Exactly one gems: not Black (K2 false), so gems either Blue or White? But K2 false only says Black not gems; Blue/White? Need more constraints? Exactly one gem among three: if not Black, could be Blue or White. Need determine? But W1 always true; B1 true; Black all-false. Both Blue all-true and White all-true. Then no statement differentiates Blue vs White? Maybe maybe K2 \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" false means Black (the two-false box) does not have gems, so gems in Blue or White. Not unique. Thus K1=F,K2=F not uniquely solvable unless some hidden assumption? But rules say puzzle uniquely solvable, so there must be unique assignment. Maybe additional rule \"at least one box all true, at least one box all false, remaining box may be ...\" means exactly one all-true? Wait wording: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" There are three boxes; \"The remaining box\" implies among the three, one is all-true, one all-false, and the third is remaining (could be all-true/all-false/mix). It says at least one all-true and at least one all-false; the remaining (third) may be anything. So it's possible two all-true and one all-false as above. But puzzle uniquely solvable maybe combined with exactly one gem and statements leads unique? In K1=F,K2=F case, gems could be Blue or White? But gems location affects statements? W1 and B1 unaffected by gems (except K2). K2 false already; gems in Blue or White both same truth values. So not unique. Thus that assignment cannot be solution if puzzle unique. Unless there is an implicit rule that exactly one box all-true and exactly one box all-false? But user states \"AT LEAST ONE ... AT LEAST ONE ... The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" \"remaining box\" could mean the box not all-true/all-false? Actually if there are two all-true and one all-false, there is no \"remaining\" unique? They might intend among the three boxes, one is all-true, one all-false, and the remaining is unspecified (could also all-true or all-false or mix). So at least one each but third arbitrary. So two all-true allowed. Then K1=F,K2=F would produce non-unique gems. But puzzle claims uniquely solvable, so maybe K1=F,K2=F invalid due to another condition? Let's examine K1=F,K2=T.\n\nCase K1=F, K2=T: Black has K1 false, K2 true => mixed (one false, one true). Black not two false; no box with two false statements? Blue/White have one statement each cannot have two false; Black has one false. So no box has two false statements. Then K2 = \"The box with two false statements has gems\" would be false due nonexistence, contradict K2=T. Thus K2 cannot T when no two-false box. So K1=F,K2=T inconsistent.\n\nCase K1=T,K2=F: Black has one false; no two-false box; K2 false consistent (no box with two false gems? Actually statement false). But no all-false box (Blue? B1? Let's compute: K1=T,K2=F => Black mixed; B1=¬(T∧F)=T => Blue all-true; W1=T => White all-true; no all-false). Violates all-false rule. So invalid.\n\nCase K1=T,K2=T: Black all-true no two-false; K2 T inconsistent; invalid.\n\nThus the only truth-value assignment satisfying at least one all-true/all-false and K2 consistency seems K1=F,K2=F, giving Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false. But gems not unique. However maybe K1=T,K2=F with no all-false invalid; K1=T,K2=T invalid; K1=F,K2=T invalid. So only K1=F,K2=F consistent. But then gems could be Blue or White, not unique. Yet the user says puzzle uniquely solvable rules and asks determine gems. There must be something else: Maybe W1 not tautologically true? Wait W1: \"THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\" If Blue box has one statement B1, at most one true is always true ONLY if we count statements currently? Yes, a box with one sta