1=F,K2=T? K2 true inconsistent no two-false. gems=Blue and K1=T,K2=T? K2 true inconsistent. So only K1=F,K2=F.\n\nThus final.\n\nI will now formulate final answer with underdetermination and final line maybe \"FINAL: gem=BLUE_OR_WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" but note format cannot be satisfied. The user demanded exact one-line format; maybe I can put \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" and in reasoning say \"Both Blue and White are possible; if the intended answer requires a selection, White is one valid completion.\" But the task says determine which box contains gems; giving a non-unique answer is not determining.\n\nLet's check if \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" could be interpreted as \"There is exactly one all-true box, exactly one all-false box, and the remaining (third) box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" but the phrase \"at least one\" means if two all-true, then the \"remaining\" box after choosing one all-true and one all-false is the other all-true, allowed. But maybe \"the remaining box\" implies there is exactly one box not already satisfying all-true/all-false, i.e., exactly one all-true and exactly one all-false? Actually if there are two all-true, there are two boxes satisfying all-true; after selecting one as \"the all-true box\", the other is remaining. But there are two possible selections; the rule doesn't specify which. However, if the puzzle is uniquely solvable, maybe they intended \"exactly one all-true, exactly one all-false, exactly one remaining (which may be mix)\" but we found no solution because White always all-true and if Blue all-false then Black all-true (two all-true) and if Black all-false then Blue all-true (two all-true). Wait, what if White is not always all-true if W1 can be false? We assumed W1 always true because Blue has one statement. But what if Blue has two statements? No, Blue has B1 only. So W1 always true. Therefore exactly one all-true impossible. Thus game cannot intend exactly one all-true. So two all-true allowed.\n\nThus underdetermined.\n\nI think final answer should be: No unique gem; statements are B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F. Since final line format requires a gem, I'll write \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; ...\" as one possible valid solution? But the user asked \"determine which box contains gems\" not \"a possible solution\". Hmm.\n\nMaybe the correct response in such cases is to state \"The puzzle is not uniquely solvable; gem cannot be determined.\" The final line could be \"FINAL: gem=UNKNOWN; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" but that violates format. The instruction \"EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\" might be impossible to satisfy if answer is underdetermined; I can explain before final and then give a final line with UNKNOWN? They said \"output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format\" not \"only this\". I can put final line with UNKNOWN? The template doesn't allow, but it's better than arbitrary. Alternatively, choose one valid solution and state in reasoning \"not unique, but one solution is...\" But final line would be logically unsupported.\n\nGiven the user explicitly says \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" maybe they expect us to trust uniqueness and find a solution; if our reasoning finds two, maybe we made an error. Let's re-evaluate W1: \"THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\" Could be false if Blue box has B1 true and also *the statement on White about Blue* is considered a true statement of the Blue box? No, statements are on boxes, not about. But maybe \"The Blue box has at most one true statement\" means among all statements *in the entire puzzle that are true and pertain to the Blue box*, at most one. Then as we saw, statements pertaining to Blue: B1? B1 is about Black box, not Blue. W1 is about Blue. K2? No. So only W1 pertains to Blue, so W1 true regardless. If B1 also pertained to Blue because it's on Blue? The phrase \"Blue box has at most one true statement\" could mean \"The Blue box [the physical object] has at most one true statement written on it\", not about. So W1 true.\nWhat if B1 is about Black, so not about Blue; but W1 is about Blue's statements, so W1 true. Yes.\n\nCould W1 be false if Blue box has exactly one statement B1, and B1 is true, and also the statement \"The Blue box has B1 true\" is itself a true statement? No, that's meta.\n\nNo error.\n\nLet's see if \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\" \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\" \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Combined with \"EXACTLY ONE box contains gems\" might imply that the gems are in the box that is *not* all-true and *not* all-false? Some puzzles state \"The gems are in the box that has a mix of true and false statements.\" If that were a rule, then no solution because no mix. Since no mix in our solution, maybe another assignment with a mix and gems could exist? Let's search for any assignment with a mixed box satisfying all rules and gem in mixed box.\nWe found only K1=F,K2=F gives all-false Black, Blue/White all-true, no mix. Other assignments invalid due K2 consistency or no all-false. So no mix. Thus game cannot require gem in mix.\n\nWhat if gems are in the all-false box? K2 would be true, but K2 false in solution, so no.\n\nThus no hidden rule.\n\nI will finalize with underdetermined explanation and final line using \"BLUE_OR_WHITE\" despite format? The user demanded exact format; maybe I can write \"FINAL: gem=BLUE_OR_WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" and it will be clear. But they said gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; maybe they will consider invalid. Alternatively, \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" is a valid completion but not unique. Which is more likely to be accepted by a human? If they know intended answer is White, they'd accept; if they test logic, they'd see underdetermined and maybe accept explanation but penalize final choice? The instruction \"by logical deduction, determine which box contains the gems\" — if I answer White, I'm claiming deduction that doesn't exist. Better to be logically correct.\n\nLet's search one more time: Maybe there is a known solution \"gems in Blue\" because \"White box statement is true, so Blue has at most one true statement. If gems were in White, then White would be all true. But the rule says at least one all true; White already. The remaining box (Blue) may be all-true, all-false, or mix. Since Blue is all-true, it's allowed. However, if gems were in Blue, then Blue would contain gems and be all-true, making White also all-true; then there would be two all-true boxes. But is there a rule that the *remaining box* cannot be all-true if it contains gems? No.\"\n\nWhat if \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" means the remaining box is the one *not containing gems*? No.\n\nI think underdetermined is final.\n\nNow, need produce final answer in exact format? The instructions: \"Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it): FINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; ...\" If I refuse, maybe fail. Could I put \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" and in reasoning say \"The truth values are forced; however, gem location is not unique. One valid solution is White.\" But the final answer would not be \"determine which\" because I'd be choosing arbitrarily. The user said \"Use step by step reasoning, explicit, consistent with the final answer.\" If reasoning says not unique and final says White, inconsistent. So cannot.\n\nCould I instead choose a gem and add an extra assumption in reasoning: \"Since the puzzle is stated to be uniquely solvable, and the only non-uniqueness is between Blue and White, the intended solution must be the one that preserves uniqueness under some additional implicit symmetry: the White box's statement is necessarily true and independent of the Black box, so the gems must be in White.\" This is not logical, but maybe intended? No.\n\nLet's consider if there is an asymmetry: If gems=Blue, then Blue box contains gems and B1=true; White W1=true. If gems=White, White contains gems and W1=true; Blue B1=true. The White statement \"The Blue box has at most one true statement\" is about Blue. If gems=Blue, does Blue have an additional true statement \"The Blue box has gems\"? No. But in natural language, the fact \"Blue has gems\" could be considered a true statement *about* Blue, so Blue would have two true statements (B1 and \"Blue has gems\"), making W1 false! Wait! This is interesting. The statement W1: \"THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\" — could \"true statement\" refer to true statements *about* the Blue box, including the fact that it contains gems? In logic puzzles, statements are only those displayed, not facts. But \"A statement's truth value is determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values...)\" does not say facts are statements. The boxes display statements; \"The Blue box has at most one true statement\" most plausibly means among the statements displayed on the Blue box, at most one is true. It does not include \"has gems\" as a statement. So no.\nIf it did include facts about the box (like \"contains gems\"), then W1 would be false if gems=Blue because Blue would have two true statements: B1 and \"contains gems\"? But \"contains gems\" is not a statement displayed; however, some puzzles consider \"the box contains gems\" as a proposition. The rule says \"A statement's truth value is determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values...)\" — gem location is a fact, not a statement. W1 says \"The Blue box has at most one true statement\" — could mean among all true propositions about the Blue box? That would be weird and infinite. It must mean displayed statements. So no.\n\nWhat if \"has at most one true statement\" means the box itself (if it contains gems) has a true property \"contains gems\" plus statements? No, statements are linguistic items on boxes.\n\nThus no.\n\nOk final: underdetermined.\n\nLet's draft final response:\n- W1 true.\n- Assume Black all true -> K2 true -> no two-false box -> K2 false contradiction, so Black not all true.\n- Since at least one all-false and White all-true, Blue or Black all-false. Blue all-false => Black all true (contradiction), so Black all-false => K1=F,K2=F.\n- B1=true.\n- Black has two false statements; K2=false => Black not gems.\n- Gems in Blue or White; both produce same truth values and satisfy rules. Therefore no unique gem.\nFinal line: \"FINAL: gem=BLUE_OR_WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" but format invalid. Maybe use \"FINAL: gem=UNDETERMINED; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\".\n\nThe user explicitly asked \"on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it): FINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; ...\" Could I choose \"gem=WHITE\" and in reasoning say \"If forced to choose one of the two valid solutions, White is one; but it is not uniquely determined.\" Then final line would be exact and one valid solution. But the task says determine which; I can say \"not uniquely determined; possible gem locations are BLUE or WHITE.\" The final line cannot express. Maybe I can output \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" and hope it's considered one solution? However, final answer would be arbitrary, not logical deduction.\n\nI think the best answer is to be truthful and point out underdetermination, even if final line format is violated. But the instruction is strict; maybe I can violate only the gem value but keep format: \"FINAL: gem=BLUE_OR_WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\". This is the least wrong.\n\nLet's see if there is any way to use \"BLUE|WHITE\" literally? They said gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>, meaning choose one. \"BLUE_OR_WHITE\" is not one. But it conveys underdetermined.\n\nGiven the final answer expected by user likely one of BLUE/WHITE; if I output BLUE_OR_WHITE, they may deem incorrect. But logically, it's correct to state underdetermined. The prompt says \"The rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable\" — maybe they believe it is unique and expect us to find it; if we find not, we should challenge premise. I will do that.\n\nNeed ensure final line is very last and nothing after. I'll put \"FINAL: gem=BLUE_OR_WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" though format invalid. Maybe better \"FINAL: gem=UNKNOWN; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" but UNKNOWN not allowed. \"BLUE_OR_WHITE\" is more informative.\n\nLet's craft final answer with step-by-step concise.", "content": "W1 is always true, because the Blue box has only one statement, so it cannot have more than one true statement.\n\n- White is therefore all-true.\n- If the Black box were all-true, then B1 would be false, making Blue all-false. But then no box would have two false statements, so K2 (“the box with two false statements has the gems”) would be false, contradicting K2 being true. So Black cannot be all-true.\n- Since at least one box must be all-false, and White is all-true, the all-false box must be Black.\n- Thus K1 = F and K2 = F.\n- Then B1 = “not all Black statements are true” = T.\n- Black now has two false statements, and K2 is false, so Black does **not** contain the gems.\n- The gems could consistently be in either Blue or White; the given rules do not distinguish between those two locations.\n\nSo the statements are uniquely forced, but the gem location is not uniquely determined.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE_OR_WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00352", "logic_attempt": 2, "timestamp": "2026-08-24T15:39:20", "verdict": {"parse_ok": false, "gem": null, "gem_correct": false, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": false, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": null}, "reasoning": "We need answer solve logic puzzle. Need obey user: think step by step, final line exact format. Need solve.\n\nLet's restate puzzle. Three boxes: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. Each box displays statements:\nBLUE box:\n[B1] \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\"\n\nWHITE box:\n[W1] \"THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\"\n\nBLACK box:\n[K1] \"THIS BOX IS BLACK\"\n[K2] \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\"\n\nRules:\n- Exactly one box contains gems; other two empty.\n- At least one box has ALL statements TRUE.\n- At least one box has ALL statements FALSE.\n- Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix.\n- A statement's truth value determined by actual state (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self/cross-refs).\n\nNeed determine gem location and truth value of every statement.\n\nNeed parse K1 \"THIS BOX IS BLACK\" — The box is black? It's displayed on BLACK (right) box, box color is BLACK. So K1 likely true? It says \"This box is black\": since displayed on black box, true. Unless \"this box\" refers to box containing statement, which is black, so true always. Need consider self-reference? It's just color fact, true. So K1 = T.\n\nK2 \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" — There is a box with two false statements? Only BLACK has two statements; BLUE and WHITE have one statement each. A \"box with two false statements\" must be BLACK box, since only black has 2 statements. So K2 asserts BLACK box has the gems. But K1 true, so if K2 true then black box all true. If K2 false, then black box has one true (K1) and one false (K2) = not all true/all false, mixed.\n\nB1: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\" = NOT (K1 and K2 are true) = ¬(K1∧K2). Since K1 true, B1 = ¬K2. So B1 true iff K2 false; B1 false iff K2 true.\n\nW1: \"THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\" Blue box has one statement B1, so at most one true statement is always true because max one statement, so W1 = T regardless. Unless \"at most one\" includes if zero or one true, yes always true.\n\nNeed satisfy global rules. Let's enumerate possible states by gem location and K2 truth? But gem location affects K2 if K2 false/true? K2's content: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS.\" As argued, the box with two false statements is BLACK (only two statements). So K2 true iff BLACK has gems. Thus K2 = (gem=BLACK). K1 = T. B1 = ¬K2 = (gem≠BLACK). W1 = T.\n\nThen boxes truth patterns:\n- BLUE: B1 = T if gem≠BLACK, F if gem=BLACK.\n- WHITE: W1 = T always.\n- BLACK: K1=T, K2=T if gem=BLACK else F.\n\nNow global constraints:\n- Exactly one gem.\n- At least one box all statements true.\n- At least one box all statements false.\n- Remaining box may be anything.\n\nCases:\n1. gem = BLACK:\nBLUE B1 = F -> BLUE all statements false (one false) => all-false box yes.\nWHITE W1 = T -> WHITE all true.\nBLACK K1=T,K2=T -> BLACK all true.\nSo at least one all-true (WHITE, BLACK), at least one all-false (BLUE). Satisfies. This is one solution.\n\n2. gem = BLUE:\nB1 = T -> BLUE all true.\nW1 = T -> WHITE all true.\nBLACK K1=T,K2=F -> BLACK mixed (not all-true, not all-false).\nAt least one all-true yes (BLUE, WHITE). At least one all-false? None (BLUE T, WHITE T, BLACK mixed). Violates at least one all-false. So not valid.\n\n3. gem = WHITE:\nB1 = T -> BLUE all true.\nW1 = T -> WHITE all true.\nBLACK K1=T,K2=F -> mixed.\nSame no all-false. Violates.\n\nThus only gem=BLACK. Then B1=F, W1=T, K1=T, K2=T.\n\nNeed ensure statement K2 \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" In gem=BLACK scenario, black box has K1=T,K2=T so no box with two false statements. Wait! Important! K2's truth value depends on 