LACK box.\n2. W1 (“The blue box has at most one true statement”) is true, because the blue box has only one statement, so it can have 0 or 1 true statements.\n3. Only the BLACK box has two statements, so “the box with two false statements” can only be the BLACK box.\n4. With K1 true, the BLACK box can never have two false statements: if K2 is true, BLACK has 0 false statements; if K2 is false, BLACK has 1 false statement. Therefore K2 must be false.\n5. Then B1 = “It is not true that all statements on the BLACK box are true” is true, because K2 is false.\n6. Resulting pattern: BLUE is all true, WHITE is all true, BLACK is mixed. No box is all false, violating the rule that at least one box must be all false.\n\nTherefore the puzzle has no consistent solution under the stated rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=NONE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}
