2,K1, constant T, constant F, self, none} and B2 semantics {existential, implication, biconditional, etc.} with rules at-least one all-true/all-false, exactly one gem, and maybe B1=G=WHITE, K1=G=BLUE. See which yield unique solution(s). We already tested some.\n\nLet's define B2 truth functions:\nE = (B1=F ∧ B2=F) (Blue has two false statements)\nExistential (Ex): B2 = E ∧ G=BLUE. Solutions: B2=F always, with constraint not(B1=F and G=BLUE) => B1=T or G≠BLUE. So B2=F.\nImplication (Imp): B2 = E → G=BLUE = ¬E ∨ G=BLUE = B1 ∨ B2 ∨ G=BLUE. Solutions: B2=T always OR (B2=F ∧ B1=F ∧ G≠BLUE). Actually if B2=T always consistent; B2=F only if B1=F and G≠BLUE (because RHS = B1∨F∨G=BLUE; for F need B1=F and G≠BLUE). So two possible truth values for some gems.\nBiconditional (Bic): B2 = E ↔ G=BLUE = (E ∧ G=BLUE) ∨ (¬E ∧ G≠BLUE). Equation B2 = ((B1=F∧B2=F) ∧ G=BLUE) ∨ ((B1∨B2) ∧ G≠BLUE). Need solve.\nLet's solve Bic maybe.\nLet G=BLUE: B1=F, K1=T. Then E = B2=F. B2 = (E ∧ T) ∨ (¬E ∧ F) = E = B2=F. Equation B2 = B2 (tautology). So B2 can be T or F? Wait E = (B1=F ∧ B2=F)= (T ∧ B2=F)= (B2=F). If B2=T, E=F, RHS = (F∧T)∨(T∧F)=F; B2=T would not equal F. So B2=T invalid. If B2=F, E=T, RHS = T∨F=T; B2=F not equal T. So no solution for G=BLUE? Let's compute carefully:\nBic: B2 = (E ∧ G=BLUE) ∨ (¬E ∧ G≠BLUE).\nG=BLUE => B2 = E ∧ T ∨ ¬E ∧ F = E. E = (B1=F ∧ B2=F)= (T ∧ B2=F)= ¬B2. So equation B2 = ¬B2 => no solution. So G=BLUE impossible under Bic.\nG=WHITE: B1=T, K1=F. E = (F ∧ B2=F)=F. ¬E=T. G≠BLUE=T. B2 = F ∨ T = T. So B2=T. Valid.\nG=BLACK: B1=F, K1=F. E = ¬B2. G≠BLUE=T. B2 = (¬B2 ∧ F) ∨ (B2 ∧ T) = B2. Tautology => B2 can be T or F. Need rules.\nSo Bic solutions: G=WHITE B2=T; G=BLACK B2=T or F; G=BLUE none.\nThen box statuses depend W1.\n\nIf W1=B2:\nG=WHITE: B1=T,B2=T,W1=T,K1=F => Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false. Valid at-least (two all-true).\nG=BLACK,B2=T: B1=F,B2=T,W1=T,K1=F => Blue mixed, White all-true, Black all-false. Valid.\nG=BLACK,B2=F: B1=F,B2=F,W1=F,K1=F =