nCould \"BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" be interpreted as \"there exists a box next to the black box that contains gems\" (existential plural)? Since only one neighbor, same.\n\nCould \"BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" mean \"all boxes except black contain gems\"? No, \"next to black\" = adjacent, not all except.\n\nCould \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\" refer to a box that has exactly two false statements *among the statements on all boxes, not necessarily its own*? For example, a box could \"have\" two false statements if two false statements are displayed on it? Only Blue displays two statements. same.\n\nCould \"two false statements\" mean \"two of the statements in the entire puzzle are false and they are in the same box\"? Only Blue can have two statements, so if B1 and B2 are both false then Blue has two false statements. Same.\n\nCould \"the box with two false statements has the gems\" mean \"the box that currently has exactly two false statements [among all four statements?] has gems\" i.e., count false statements in entire puzzle: if exactly two statements are false, the box containing those two false statements (could be Blue if both false, or spread across boxes? But a box can only contain its own statements; White/Black have one statement each, so cannot contain two false statements unless a box has two statements. So same.\n\nThus B2 referent fixed.\n\nLet's search memory of Blue Prince puzzle. I can't access internet, but maybe recall from videos: The answer in Blue Prince \"three boxes\" puzzle is \"Gems are in the black box. Blue statements: false, true. White: true. Black: false.\" This matches known solution with exactly one all-true/all-false/mix. The rules in game might actually be: \"One box contains only true statements, one contains only false statements, and one contains a mix of true and false statements.\" The user's prompt however says \"At least one box has all true; at least one box has all false;