ll-true box. Invalid.\nIf gem in BLACK: B1 false, W1 false, K1 false (White doesn't contain gems), K2 true (Black empty false XOR White middle true -> true) -> Blue all-false, White all-false, Black mixed (F,T) => no all-true box. Invalid.\nIf gem in BLUE: B1 true, W1 true, K1 false, K2 false -> Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false => satisfies all rules. Thus unique.\n\nNeed ensure statement W1 true if B1 true; yes.\n\nLet's craft final answer with step-by-step reasoning, then final line exactly. Desired oververbosity 5, concise. Need on very last line exactly one-line format, nothing after. We'll include reasoning paragraphs then final line.\n\nLet's consider if there is any risk that final answer expected White under K2 inclusive and K1=gem location. Which one is more likely? The presence of \"EITHER\" strongly exclusive; also \"White box is the middle box\" is always true, so if inclusive K2 is always true; that would make K2 a tautology, less likely. Also the rule \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE\" with solution White under inclusive: Blue and White all false, Black all true; this satisfies all but K2 trivial. But K1 \"A box next to black is true\" = White contains gems; K2 always true; then Black all true regardless of K1? Wait if K2 always true and K1=White gems, then Black all true only if White gems. Blue all false, White all false. This is also a coherent puzzle but K2 trivial. However the puzzle statement \"EITHER THIS BOX IS EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" would be an odd way to say \"true\" if inclusive, because the second half is obviously true to solver (White is middle), making K2 always true; perhaps it's a red herring to test whether you treat OR inclusive? But \"Either...or\" in English often means exclusive, so K2 not trivial. The puzzle designer likely intended to exploit that the White box is middle, so K2's truth depends on first disjunct only under exclusive OR, making it equivalent to \"Black is not empty\" i.