clusive or). Black's first statement \"A box next to the black box is true\" refers to the White box being true (i.e., containing the gems) and is false. Yes, I recall reading exactly: \"The first black statement is false because it refers to the white box being the true box [with gems], not the truth of its inscription.\" Maybe they treat \"true\" as \"contains gems\" in this puzzle. So answer Blue likely correct.\n\nLet's ensure final format: \"FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\" exactly one line no extra after. In reasoning, use label EVERY statement id only B1, W1, K1, K2. Good.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning step-by-step:\n1. Exactly one gem location.\n2. White is middle, so in K2, \"White box is middle\" is true. Interpret \"Either...or\" as exclusive OR (as natural wording); thus K2 = NOT(Black empty) = Black has gems? Wait exclusive: P=Black empty, Q=true; K2 = P XOR Q = not P = Black not empty = Black contains gems. So K2 is true iff gems are in Black.\n3. K1: only box next to Black is White; \"White is true\" means White is the gem box. So K1 true iff gems in White.\n4. B1: \"other two boxes (White, Black) empty\" true iff gems in Blue.\n5. W1: references B1, so W1 same as B1.\n6. Test gem cases:\n- Blue: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F. Boxes: Blue all-true; White all-true; Black all-false. Satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false.\n- White: B1=F,W1=F,K1=T,K2=F. Blue all-false; White all-false; Black mixed (T,F). No all-true -> invalid.\n- Black: B1=F,W1=F,K1=F,K2=T. Blue all-false; White all-false; Black mixed (F,T). No all-true -> invalid.\nTherefore unique solution.\n\nNeed be careful: In White case under K2 exclusive, K2 = Black has gems = false. Yes.\nIn Black case K2 = true.\nK1 for White case true because White adjacent to Black and contains gems.\nK1 for Black case false because White doesn't contain gems.\nGood.\n\nLet's produce final.", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- WHITE is the middle box, so in K2 the clause “the White box is the middle box” is true. Using “either … or” as exclusive, K2 is true only if the BLACK box contains the gems.\n- K1: the only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so K1 says WHITE is the gem box.\n- B1 says the other two boxes (WHITE and BLACK) are empty, so B1 is true iff BLUE contains the gems.\n- W1 refers to B1, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F. BLUE and WHITE are all-true; BLACK is all-false. Valid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: Blue and White are all-false, Black is mixed; no all-true box. Invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: Blue and White are all-false, Black is mixed; no all-true box. Invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}
