final answer may be G=BLUE, B1=T,W1=T,K1=F,K2=F.\n\nLet's test both candidate solutions against rules:\nCandidate 1 (K2 inclusive, K1=White gems): gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T. Box statuses: Blue all false, White all false, Black all true. At least one true, at least one false; remaining White all false. Works if OR inclusive.\nCandidate 2 (K2 exclusive, K1=White gems): gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F. Box statuses: Blue all true, White all true, Black all false. At least one true, at least one false; remaining White all true. Works if OR exclusive.\nBoth are valid under respective OR semantics. Which one is \"uniquely solvable\" depends on OR. The statement text \"EITHER THIS BOX IS EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" — In natural English, \"either...or\" often exclusive. The user capitalized EITHER, but not \"exclusive or\". They also included rule \"A statement's truth value is determined by actual state ... including self- and cross-references\" not \"inclusive or\". In many logic puzzles, \"either ... or ...\" is exclusive, while \"or\" alone is inclusive. Since they used \"EITHER\", I lean exclusive => G=BLUE, K2=F.\n\nBut wait: If K2 exclusive and K1=White gems, candidate G=Blue has K2 false because both \"Black empty\" true and \"White is middle\" true. The statement \"Either this box is empty or the white box is the middle box\" in English: If both are true, would a person say that statement is false? Usually \"either...or\" is exclusive: \"Either you eat or you starve\" both can be true? If both true, the exclusive statement is false. Yes. So K2 false.\n\nNow, is K1 \"A box next to black box is true\" under G=Blue false? White is next to Black and is empty (no gems), so if \"true\" means contains gems, false. Then Black all false. Blue and White all true because B1 true and W1 true. This satisfies exactly one all-true? Actually two all-true (Blue, White), one all-false (Black). The rules allow remaining box all-true/all-false/mix; here remaining White all-true, so fine. At least one all-true (yes), at least one all-false (Black). Unique? Check G=White: White has gems -> K1 true; Black empty true XOR White middle true = false => K2 false; B1 false,W1 false; Boxes: Blue all false, White all false, Black mixed [T,F]; no all-true. Invalid. G=Black: K1 false; Black empty false XOR White middle true = true => K2 true; B1 false,W1 false; Blue/White all false, Black mixed [F,T]; no all-true. Invalid. So unique G=Blue. This is a beautiful solution: Black is the all-false box, Blue/White all-true.\n\nWould the puzzle be \"uniquely solvable\" with K2 inclusive? Candidate G=White: K1 true, K2 true -> Black all true; Blue/White all false. Unique because G=Blue all true no false; G=Black no true. Also beautiful: Black all-true, Blue/White all-false. Which one did game intend? Need determine from exact wording of K2: \"EITHER THIS BOX IS EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" If inclusive, because White is middle, K2 is always true regardless of gems. That would make K2's first clause irrelevant except for exclusive? Actually inclusive OR with a true second disjunct is a tautology; puzzles sometimes include tautologies to satisfy all-true boxes. But K2 being always true means in G=White solution, Black box all true because K1 true and K2 true. In G=Blue solution with inclusive, K1 false, K2 true -> Black mixed, no all false? Wait G=Blue inclusive: Blue/White all true, Black mixed [F,T]; at least one all false? None, invalid. So inclusive forces G=White. This solution uses K2 as a tautology; not necessarily bad. Exclusive forces G=Blue and K2 not tautology, more informative. The phrase \"EITHER ... OR ...\" in logic puzzles often means exclusive, and if they wanted inclusive they'd write \"OR\" or \"AND/OR\". The user capitalized EITHER, so likely exclusive. Additionally, the statement \"EITHER THIS BOX IS EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" — Since \"White box is middle\" is a known fact, an inclusive OR would make the statement trivially true; a puzzle designer would probably not include a tautology unless intentional? It could be intentional to make Black all-true in G=White solution, but then K2 is redundant. The exclusive OR makes K2 equivalent to \"Black has gems\", more interesting. I think intended is exclusive OR -> G=BLUE, K2=F.\n\nHowever, we must also consider K1 \"true\" = contains gems. Is that consistent with word \"true\" in rules? The rules say \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE\" — They use \"TRUE\" for statements, not boxes. But K1 says \"A BOX ... IS TRUE.\" In game, a box being \"true\" might indeed mean it contains gems, as the \"correct\" box. The user asks \"determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\" They didn't define \"true box\"; but if K1 meant all statements true, puzzle unsolvable, so must mean contains gems. This is the key. We can mention in reasoning: \"Here 'a box is true' must mean it is the gem box; otherwise no assignment satisfies the all-true/all-false conditions.\" But should we explicitly address ambiguity? The final answer expected one line only after reasoning. Need be concise but deductive.\n\nLet's ensure no other consistent solution with K1=White all-true if W1 not B1? Suppose W1 refers to \"statement above\" = K1? We found multiple. Unique? No. So K1=White all-true cannot yield unique; puzzle solvability forces K1=gem location. Also K2 exclusive vs inclusive: Need decide. The wording \"EITHER ... OR ...\" is exclusive. Let's final answer G=BLUE, B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F.\n\nLet's verify under exclusive OR with K1=White has gems:\n- G=Blue: Blue contains gems; White/Black empty. B1 \"other two boxes empty\" true (White/Black empty). W1 \"B1 true\" true. K1 \"box next to Black is true\" = White has gems? White empty, false. K2 \"Either Black empty or White middle\": Black empty true, White middle true, exclusive OR false. Box statuses: Blue all true; White all true; Black all false. Satisfies at least one all true, at least one all false; remaining White all true (allowed).\n- G=White: B1 false; W1 false; K1 true (White has gems); K2 false (Black empty true, White middle true -> exclusive false). Blue all false; White all false; Black mixed (K1 true K2 false). No all-true box -> invalid.\n- G=Black: B1 false; W1 false; K1 false; K2 true (Black empty false, White middle true -> exclusive true). Blue all false; White all false; Black mixed. No all-true -> invalid.\nUnique.\n\nIf someone uses inclusive OR, solution would be G=White with K2 true. But because statement says EITHER...OR, exclusive is correct. The final answer should be G=BLUE, B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F.\n\nLet's see if user expects final line exactly: \"FINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\" So final line: FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\n\nNeed provide step-by-step reasoning before final line. Need label every statement ID only. Mention \"true box\" in K1 means contains gems? Need be careful: The problem statement didn't define \"TRUE\" for boxes. To avoid ambiguity, in reasoning say \"For K1, 'a box ... is true' must be read as 'contains the gems'; reading it as 'all statements true' makes the stated rules unsatisfiable. Under the intended game meaning, a 'true box' is the gem box.\" But user didn't ask to discuss ambiguity; they expect solution. However, if I don't mention, reasoning might seem to use \"true\" = contains gems, but K1 statement says \"A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" and in solution White is not all-true (W1 false), so indeed K1 true because White contains gems. Need explicitly state: \"K1 is true iff the box next to Black (only White) contains the gems.\" That resolves.\n\nBut what if intended K2 inclusive? Then final would be different. Need be confident. Let's further investigate via known Blue Prince solution online memory. I faintly recall answer in some walkthrough: \"The gem is in the BLUE box. Statements: B1 true, W1 true, K1 false, K2 false.\" Actually yes! I think I saw a solution: \"Blue box has gems. White statement above true. Black statements: both false. Because K2 'Either this box is empty or the white box is the middle box' is false because both are true (exclusive or).\" That rings a bell. The statement uses \"Either ... or ...\" indeed exclusive; solution Blue. The alternative G=White with K2 true inclusive seems less likely because \"Either ... or ...\" exclusive is standard in many puzzle contexts. Also if K2 inclusive, K2 would be true even if Black has gems (Black not empty, White middle true), making K2 always true, less useful. The puzzle designer likely used exclusive OR to make K2 false when both true, giving Black all false in Blue solution. I think final G=BLUE is correct.\n\nLet's ensure \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE\" in G=Blue: Black has K1 false and K2 false, so Black all false. Good. \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements TRUE\": Blue all true, White all true. Good. \"Remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix\": White all true. Good.\n\nLet's craft step-by-step reasoning:\n1. Since only White is adjacent to Black, K1 means White contains gems (under game's \"true box\" = gem box). So K1 = (gem=WHITE).\n2. Because White is the middle box, \"White box is the middle box\" is true. K2 is exclusive OR: K2 = (Black empty) XOR True = not(Black empty) = (gem=BLACK)? Wait if gem=Blue then Black empty true XOR true = false; if gem=White Black empty true XOR true = false; if gem=Black Black empty false XOR true = true. So K2 = (gem=BLACK)? Let's compute: K2 = (Black empty) XOR True = NOT(Black empty) = Black has gems = (gem=BLACK). Yes, because if Black has gems, Black not empty, XOR true => true; if Black empty, XOR true => false. So K2 is true iff gem=BLACK. Wait \"Black has gems\" = gem=BLACK. Yes. So K2 = (gem=BLACK).\n3. B1 = (White empty AND Black empty) = (gem=BLUE). Since exactly one gem, other two empty iff Blue has gems.\n4. W1 = \"B1 is true\" = B1 = (gem=BLUE).\n5. Test gem locations:\n- If gem=BLUE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F (White not gem), K2=F (Black not gem). Box statuses: Blue all T, White all T, Black all F. Valid.\n- If gem=WHITE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F (Black empty true XOR White middle true = false). Blue all F, White all F, Black mixed (T,F). No all-true box -> invalid because at least one box must be all true.\n- If gem=BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F, K2=T (Black empty false XOR White middle true = true). Blue all F, White all F, Black mixed (F,T). No all-true box -> invalid.\nThus unique gem=BLUE, statements as above.\n\nWait K2 = (gem=BLACK) under exclusive, yes. So if gem=BLUE, K2 false; if gem=WHITE, K2 false? Black empty true XOR White middle true = false, yes K2 false; if gem=BLACK, K2 true. So K2 true iff Black has gems. Correct.\n\nNeed ensure K1 = (gem=WHITE) under \"box next to black is true\" = White contains gems. Since only White adjacent to Black. If \"A box next to Black\" could also include Blue if circular? No. So K1 = White has gems = gem=WHITE.\n\nThen final.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning concise. Mention exclusive OR because \"Either...or\" is exclusive. If inclusive, different solution; but likely exclusive. Should I mention \"assuming standard exclusive reading of 'either...or'\"? The user didn't specify; but final answer depends. Could there be a way to determine from rules \"uniquely solvable\" which OR? No, both are uniquely solvable under different OR semantics. The puzzle statement itself uses \"EITHER ... OR ...\" which is standardly exclusive, so use that. Maybe add note: \"I read 'either...or' as exclusive OR, as the wording indicates.\" Good.\n\nLet's produce final with reasoning and final line.", "content": "Let “a box is true” mean that box contains the gems, and read “either … or …” as exclusive OR.\n\n- Only WHITE is next to BLACK, so K1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems: `K1 = (gem=WHITE)`.\n- WHITE is definitely the middle box, so:\n  - K2 = (BLACK empty) XOR (true) = true iff BLACK contains the gems: `K2 = (gem=BLACK)`.\n- B1 is true iff WHITE and BLACK are empty, which happens iff BLUE contains the gems: `B1 = (gem=BLUE)`.\n- W1 says B1 is true, so `W1 = B1`.\n\nTest gem locations:\n\n1. gem=BLUE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F → BLUE all true, WHITE all true, BLACK all false. Valid.\n2. gem=WHITE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F → no box is all true. Invalid.\n3. gem=BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F, K2=T → no box is all true. Invalid.\n\nTherefore the only valid solution is gem in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00376", "logic_attempt": 1, "timestamp": "2026-08-24T16:26:57", "verdict": {"parse_ok": true, "gem": "BLUE", "gem_correct": true, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": true, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": {"BLUE": [true], "WHITE": [true], "BLACK": [false, false]}}, "reasoning": "We need answer logic puzzle. Need solve. Let's restate.\n\nWe have three boxes: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. Each box displays statements:\nBLUE: [B1] \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\"\nWHITE: [W1] \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\"\nBLACK: [K1] \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\"\n[K2] \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\"\n\nRules:\n- Exactly one box contains gems; other two empty.\n- At least one box has all statements true (all-true box).\n- At least one box has all statements false (all-false box).\n- Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- Statement truth value determined by actual state (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self/cross-references).\n\nNeed determine gem and truth values B1,W1,K1,K2.\n\nNeed understand \"A BOX THAT IS TRUE\": Means a box whose displayed statements are all true? Or a box that is \"true\" as a box (all its statements true). Likely box is true iff all its statements are true? In puzzle, \"box is true\" means all statements on that box are true. \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" means one of adjacent boxes (BLUE or BLACK) is an all-true box. \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" means either adjacent box (left BLUE or right BLACK) contains gems? K1 says it is not true that boxes next to WHITE contain gems = neither BLUE nor BLACK contains gems. Since gems exactly one box, that would imply gems in WHITE. Need be careful: \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" plural could mean \"the boxes next to white box contain gems\" i.e., both adjacent boxes? Actually boxes next to the white box are BLUE and BLACK. \"contain gems\" plural might mean both contain gems? But exactly one box contains gems, so both cannot contain gems. It could mean \"the boxes next to the white box [i.e., at least one?] contain gems\" ambiguous. In logic puzzles, \"boxes next to X contain gems\" often means \"the box(es) adjacent to X contain(s) gems\" but plural because there are two adjacent boxes; statement \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" could be interpreted as \"all boxes next to the white box contain gems\" (both adjacent boxes contain gems) which is impossible under exactly-one, so K1 would always true? But exactly one gem, K1 \"It is not true that boxes next to white box contain gems\": if interpreted as \"both adjacent boxes contain gems\", then false always (since impossible both contain gems); K1 true always. That seems too trivial? But puzzle uses \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" maybe means \"the boxes next to the White box [collectively?] contain the gems\" i.e., at least one of adjacent boxes contains gems? Since there is exactly one gem, the statement \"boxes next to the WHITE box contain gems\" is true iff the gem is in BLUE or BLACK (one of the boxes next to White). Because only one gem, \"boxes next to WHITE contain gems\" could mean \"the gems are in a box adjacent to WHITE\", i.e., gem location is left or right. Need infer from K1: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" = \"The adjacent boxes do not contain gems\" = gem is not in Blue or Black => gem in White. That would make K1 equivalent to gem=WHITE. Could be.\n\nSimilarly W1: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" = at least one of BLUE or BLACK is an all-true box.\n\nB1: \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\" = the box containing gems is an all-true box. Since exactly one box contains gems, B1 true iff gem box is all-true.\n\nK2: \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" = all statements on BLACK box (K1 and K2) are true. So K2 truth depends on K1 and itself: K2 = (K1 and K2) ? Actually black box true iff K1 true AND K2 true. K2 statement says \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" i.e., \"Black box is all-true\". Its truth value equals T if Black box all statements true, i.e., K1=T and K2=T; F otherwise. Thus K2 = K1 ∧ K2. This is a self-reference: If K1=F then K2=F. If K1=T then equation K2 = K2, so K2 can be either? Wait if K1=T, Black box truth depends on K2: Black all-true iff K1=T and K2=T => K2=T; so K2 true iff K2 true: both possible? Need evaluate fixed points: K2 = (K1 ∧ K2). If K1=T: K2=K2, both T and F satisfy? Actually if K2=T then RHS T∧T=T, equals K2 T ok. If K2=F then RHS T∧F=F, equals K2 F ok. So K2 can be T or F consistently when K1=T. If K1=F: K2=F ∧ K2 = F, so K2=F only. So K2 free if K1 true? But other rules may constrain.\n\nThis is a self-referential truth value puzzle, need unique solvable maybe via rules.\n\nWe need solve exactly.\n\nLet's formalize.\n\nLet boxes:\nB = Blue, W = White, K = Black (use K for black).\nGems location g ∈ {B,W,K}.\n\nBox \"true\" = all its statements true.\nBlue box has one statement B1; so Blue box true iff B1=T.\nWhite box true iff W1=T.\nBlack box true iff K1=T and K2=T.\n\nStatements:\nB1 = \"The gems are in a box that is true\" = g is in a box that is true. Since exactly one gem, there is a unique gem box; B1 true iff the gem box is true (all its statements true). So B1 = True(gem box). More precisely if g=B then B1 = (Blue box true) = B1? Wait if gem in Blue, statement B1 is on Blue, truth \"gems are in a box that is true\": the box containing gems is Blue, so B1 = Blue box is true = B1 (since Blue true iff B1). So B1 = B1 self-reference if g=B: can be T or F? Let's see: If gem in Blue, B1 asserts \"gems are in a true box\" = \"Blue is true\" = B1 true. So B1 = B1: both T/F possible consistent? If B1=T, Blue box true (since all statements true), statement B1 \"gems are in true box\" true because Blue true, ok. If B1=F, Blue box not true, statement B1 \"gems are in true box\" false because Blue not true, ok. So B1 unconstrained if gem=Blue.\nIf g=W: B1 = \"White box is true\" = W1.\nIf g=K: B1 = \"Black box is true\" = K1 ∧ K2.\nActually \"gems are in a box that is true\" means the specific box with gems is true. So yes.\n\nW1 = \"A box next to the White box is true\" = at least one of Blue box or Black box is true. Adjacent boxes to White are Blue (left) and Black (right). Blue true iff B1; Black true iff K1∧K2. So W1 = B1 OR (K1∧K2). Note if both? At least one.\n\nK1 = \"It is not true that boxes next to the White box contain gems\" = The boxes adjacent to White (Blue and Black) do NOT contain gems. Under exactly one gem, this means gem is not in Blue and not in Black => gem in White. So K1 = (g=W). But if interpreted as both adjacent contain gems? Since exactly one gem, both cannot, so statement \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" would be false always, making K1 true always. But then K1 not informative and W1 etc. Need decide. The puzzle likely intends K1 = gem in White because \"boxes next to WHITE contain gems\" means the gems are in one of the adjacent boxes (plural due to two boxes but singular gem). Need examine wording: \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" Actually if there are two boxes next to white (left and right), \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" could be read as \"the boxes next to the white box (i.e., the set of adjacent boxes) contain the gems\" meaning the gems are located in one of those boxes? In natural language, if exactly one gem, saying \"boxes next to white contain gems\" might mean \"one of the boxes next to white contains the gems\" not both. The negation K1 \"It is not true that boxes next to the White box contain gems\" would mean \"none of the boxes next to White contain gems\" = gem in White. This is a classic clue. We'll adopt K1 = gem is not in Blue and not in Black = gem=White. But we should be mindful: If K1 true iff gem=WHITE, then K1 determines gem directly. Does puzzle unique? Let's test.\n\nAlso K2 = \"The Black box is true\" = Black box all-true = K1 ∧ K2. So K2 = K1 ∧ K2 as above.\n\nNow constraints:\nExactly one gem.\nAt least one all-true box.\nAt least one all-false box.\nRemaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix.\n\nBox all-true:\nBlue true iff B1=T.\nWhite true iff W1=T.\nBlack true iff K1=T and K2=T.\n\nBox all-false:\nBlue false iff B1=F (only statement false).\nWhite false iff W1=F.\nBlack false iff NOT(K1=T and K2=T) i.e., at least one of K1,K2 false. Since K1/K2 statements themselves false/true? Wait \"box all-false\" means every statement on box has truth value False. For Black, all-false iff K1=F and K2=F. Not merely black box not true; all statements false. Important.\n\n\"At least one box has all statements FALSE\" means one box each statement false. For Blue one statement B1=F => Blue all-false. White: W1=F => White all-false. Black all-false: K1=F and K2=F.\n\n\"At least one box has all statements TRUE\": Blue all-true B1=T; White W1=T; Black K1=T and K2=T.\n\n\"Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" means among three boxes, at least one all-true and at least one all-false; third arbitrary.\n\nNeed find assignments satisfying self-referential equations.\n\nApproach enumerate possible gem locations and truth assignments.\n\nLet's denote:\nA = B1 ∈ {T,F}\nC = W1 ∈ {T,F}\nD = K1 ∈ {T,F}\nE = K2 ∈ {T,F}\n\ng ∈ {Blue, White, Black}\n\nEquations:\nK1 = (g == White)? D = [g=W].\nK2 = D ∧ E. So:\nIf D=F -> E=F.\nIf D=T -> E can be T or F (both satisfy E = T∧E = E).\n\nB1:\nIf g=Blue: A = A (no constraint; A can T/F)\nIf g=White: A = C (White box true iff W1=C)\nIf g=Black: A = D ∧ E (Black box true iff K1&K2 true)\n\nW1:\nC = A ∨ (D ∧ E) (Blue true OR Black true)\n\nBox all-true conditions:\nBlue true iff A=T.\nWhite true iff C=T.\nBlack true iff D=T ∧ E=T.\n\nBox all-false conditions:\nBlue false iff A=F.\nWhite false iff C=F.\nBlack false iff D=F ∧ E=F. (Since both statements false).\n\nNeed at least one all-true, at least one all-false.\n\nLet's enumerate by gem lo