if other assignments possible under K1=gem White? We enumerated and found only g=Blue A=T C=T D=F E=F valid. Need ensure no other fixed points with g=Blue A=F? We had g=Blue D=F,E=F,C=A; A free. Need at least one all-true => A=T; so A=F invalid due no all-true (Black all-false; White/Blue false). Yes unique. Good.\n\nNeed provide step-by-step reasoning concise but deductive. Need final line exact format. Need include statement ids B1,W1,K1,K2 only. \"Label EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\" So final line: FINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\n\nNeed be careful with final line exactly one-line, nothing after. But user requested \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer...\" So we can provide reasoning then final line last. Need ensure final answer line exact.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n\n- Let \"box true\" mean all its statements true.\n- Since exactly one gem, if K1 true then gem is WHITE? Wait K1 true means \"It is not true that boxes next to White contain gems\" = no adjacent box contains gems => gem in WHITE. Conversely if gem in WHITE, K1 true; if gem in BLUE or BLACK, K1 false. So K1 = (gem=WHITE).\n- K2 = \"Black box true\" = K1 ∧ K2. Thus if K1 false, K2 false; if K1 true, K2 unconstrained by itself but constrained by other rules.\n\nThen examine gem locations:\n\n1. Suppose gem=WHITE. Then K1=T. K2 can be T or F? Actually K2 = T ∧ K2 => K2 can T or F. If K2=T, Black all-true; if K2=F, Black mixed (since K1 true, K2 false). B1 = White true = W1. W1 = Blue true OR Black true = B1 OR (K1∧K2). So B1 = B1 OR (K1∧K2). If K2=T, B1 = B1 OR T => B1=T, W1=T. Then Blue/White/Black all true, no all-false box -> violates. If K2=F, B1 = B1 OR F => B1 can T/F. If B1=T, W1=T: Blue/White true, Black mix, no all-false -> violates. If B1=F, W1=F: Blue/White all-false, Black mix, no all-true -> violates. So gem=WHITE impossible.\nWait if K2=T then Black all-true, Blue all-true, White all-true -> no all-false indeed. If K2=F,B1=F,W1=F -> Blue/White all-false, Black mix (K1 true,K2 false) -> no all-true. Good.\n\n2. Suppose gem=BLACK. Then K1=F. K2=F∧K2 => K2=F. B1 = Black true = K1∧K2 = F. W1 = B1 ∨ Black true = F ∨ F = F. Then all three boxes all-false (Blue false, White false, Black false because K1=F,K2=F). No all-true box -> violates. So gem=BLACK impossible.\n\n3. Suppose gem=BLUE. Then K1=F, so K2=F. B1 = \"gems are in a box that is true\"; gem box is Blue, so B1 = Blue true = B1, tautologically unconstrained by itself. W1 = Blue true OR Black true = B1 ∨ (K1∧K2)=B1∨F = B1. So W1=B1. Black is all-false (K1=F,K2=F). To satisfy at least one all-true box, need Blue or White all-true, which requires B1=T (and W1=T). Then Blue true, White true, Black false -> at least one true, at least one false. Valid. Hence unique.\n\nNeed mention \"remaining box may be all-true...\" We have two all-true one all-false, allowed? The rule says at least one true, at least one false, remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix. Here the \"remaining\" after choosing Black all-false, there are two all-true; does that violate? It doesn't say exactly one of each. It says at least one each, remaining may be anything. Having two all-true means the remaining (third) is all-true, allowed. So valid.\n\nPotential issue: B1 self-reference in gem=Blue: \"B1 = Blue true = B1\" means B1 can be either T/F consistently. But we choose T due all-true requirement. Good.\n\nNeed final answer.\n\nLet's ensure no hidden constraint \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" might imply exactly one box all-true and exactly one all-false and third mixed? Actually wording: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" This could be interpreted as among three boxes, at least one all-true and at least one all-false; the third (remaining) can be anything. It doesn't say \"exactly one\" all-true/all-false. So okay.\n\nLet's also consider if \"box is TRUE\" could mean something else: \"The gems are in a box that is TRUE\" might mean the box's statement? In Blue Prince, \"true box\" might mean a box whose label is true? Actually \"box is true\" likely all statements on it are true as we assumed. Good.\n\nNeed maybe derive K1 = gem=White explicitly: \"Boxes next to White contain gems\" means at least one adjacent box (Blue or Black) contains gems. Since exactly one gem, K1 true iff neither Blue nor Black contains gems, i.e., gem in White. We should phrase to avoid ambiguity: \"Taking 'boxes next to White contain gems' to mean the gems are in an adjacent box (the only sensible reading given exactly one gem)...\" But user didn't ask to discuss ambiguity; just solve. We can state.\n\nCould there be another solution if K1 interpretation different? The final answer expected by puzzle maybe one of two. Need be certain. Let's search memory: Blue Prince is a roguelike puzzle game; there is a known logic puzzle with boxes Blue, White, Black and statements:\nBlue: \"The gems are in a box that is true.\"\nWhite: \"A box next to the white box is true.\"\nBlack: \"It is not true that boxes next to the white box contain gems.\"\nBlack: \"The black box is true.\"\nI might recall solution from playthrough: Maybe answer was BLUE, B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F? Let's see if known. I think I've seen similar: The correct solution is Blue gems, B1 true, W1 true, K1 false, K2 false. Because K1 says \"It is not true that boxes next to white contain gems\" — if gems in Blue, K1 false; K2 false; Black box false; Blue box true because gems in true box; White box true because Blue next to it is true. Yes.\n\nLet's verify by brute force with equations including K1 = (g=White). Already unique.\n\nNeed maybe address K2 self-reference: If K1 false then K2 false; yes.\n\nLet's produce reasoning step-by-step but concise.\n\nPotential final line must be exactly:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\n\nNeed ensure no extra characters after.\n\nLet's craft final answer with reasoning paragraphs then final line.", "content": "Let a box be “true” if all statements on it are true, and “false” if all statements on it are false.  \n“Boxes next to the White box contain gems” means the gems are in Blue or Black, so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in White.\n\n- If gems were in WHITE: K1 = T. Then Black can never be all-false, and the remaining choices leave either no all-false box or no all-true box. Contradiction.\n- If gems were in BLACK: K1 = F, so K2 = F. Then B1 = “Black is true” = F, and W1 = F. All boxes would be all-false, so no all-true box exists. Contradiction.\n- Therefore gems must be in BLUE. Then K1 = F, so K2 = F. Black is all-false. For an all-true box to exist, B1 must be T, which makes Blue true; then W1 = “a box next to White is true” is also T, since Blue is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00376", "logic_attempt": 2, "timestamp": "2026-08-24T16:27:50", "verdict": {"parse_ok": true, "gem": "BLUE", "gem_correct": true, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": true, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": {"BLUE": [true], "WHITE": [true], "BLACK": [false, false]}}, "reasoning": "We need answer solve logic puzzle. Need be careful. Need produce final line only perhaps after reasoning. Let's reason thoroughly.\n\nWe have three boxes: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. Gems exactly one. Statements:\n\nBlue box: [B1] \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\"\nInterpretation: The gems are in a box that is true. \"Box that is true\" likely means a box whose displayed statements are all true? At least? Need determine. In Blue Prince puzzles, box is TRUE if all statements on it are true? FALSE if all statements false? But rules: AT LEAST ONE box all statements true, AT LEAST ONE box all statements false, remaining box may be all-true/all-false/mix. A statement's truth value determined by actual state including truth values of every statement. \"Box that is TRUE\" likely means the box's statements are all true (i.e., all-true box). Since each box can be all-true, all-false, or mixed. \"box that is TRUE\" = all statements on that box are true. \"box that is FALSE\" = all statements false. Need infer.\n\nWhite: [W1] \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\"\nBoxes next to white: left Blue and right Black. So W1 asserts at least one of Blue box or Black box is an all-true box.\n\nBlack:\n[K1] \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\"\nBoxes next to white: Blue and Black. So K1 asserts it is not the case that boxes next to the white box contain gems. Actually \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" plural? Could mean both boxes next to white contain gems? But only one box contains gems, impossible. So K1 likely means \"The boxes next to white box contain gems [i.e., the gems are in one of those boxes]\" is false. More exactly \