ue OR Black true). (Since at least one of Blue/Black all-true.)\n\nK1 = \"It is not true that boxes next to the white box contain gems.\" Boxes next to white: Blue and Black. So K1 = T iff it is false that (gems in Blue OR gems in Black). Equivalent gems not in Blue and not in Black -> G=White. So K1 = T iff G=White. K1 = F iff G=Blue or G=Black.\n\nK2 = \"The Black box is true.\" So K2 = T iff Black box all-true, i.e., K1=T and K2=T. This is self-referential: K2 = (K1 ∧ K2). Let's solve: If K2=T, then equation K2 = K1 ∧ K2 = K1 ∧ T = K1, so requires K1=T. Thus K2=T implies K1=T. If K2=F, equation F = K1 ∧ F = F, always satisfied regardless K1. So possible pairs: (K1=T,K2=T) or (K1=F,K2=F)? Wait if K2=F, equation holds for K1=T? K1 ∧ F = F, yes K2=F equals F, so (K1=T,K2=F) is logically consistent? K2 statement \"Black box is true\" would be false because Black box not all true (K1 true but K2 false, so mixed). If K1=T,K2=F, Black box mixed, K2 false, consistent. If K1=F,K2=F, Black box all false, K2 false, consistent. If K1=F,K2=T impossible because K1∧K2=F but K2=T. So K2=T iff K1=T; K2=F iff K1=T or K1=F (no restriction). So K2 truth value equals K1? Actually possible assignments: (T,T) and (T,F) and (F,F); not (F,T). Thus K2 can be F even if K1=T; but if K1=F then K2=F. So K2 = K1? No, if K1=T K2 may T or F, so K2 not determined by K1 alone. But note Black box true iff K1=T ∧ K2=T => only (T,T). Black false iff K1=F ∧ K2=F => (F,F). Mixed iff (T,F).\n\nNeed solve global with statements.\n\nLet's enumerate cases by gem location and box true/false statuses, then statements.\n\nAlternative derive.\n\nLet BT = Blue all-true (B1=T), BF = Blue all-false (B1=F) (single, so exactly one).\nWT = White all-true (W1=T), WF = White all-false (W1=F).\nKT = Black all-true (K1=T,K2=T), KF = Black all-false (K1=F,K2=F), KM = mixed (K1=T,K2=F). (K1=F,K2=T impossible)\n\nGlobal constraints: at least one all-true among B,W,K; at least one all-false; third any.\n\nStatements:\nB1 = T iff G is in an all-true box. Since only one gem, B1 = ( (G=B and BT) OR (G=W and WT) OR (G=K and KT) ).\nBut also Blue box all-true iff B1=T => BT = B1.\nAll-false iff B1=F => BF = not B1. So B1 equals BT? Wait if B1=T then Blue true; if B1=F then Blue false. So B1 = BT. Then statement equation becomes:\nBT = (G=B and BT) OR (G=W and WT) OR (G=K and KT).\nThis is strict: BT true iff gem is in a true box. Since if gem in Blue and Blue true, equation true; if gem in White and White true, true; if gem in Black and Black true, true; else false.\n\nSimilarly W1 = WT = (BT OR KT) because boxes next to White are Blue and Black. So WT = BT ∨ KT.\n\nK1 = T iff G=White. So K1 = (G=W).\nK2 = KT? Actually K2 = T iff Black true (KT). And KT = K1 ∧ K2. Since K2 = KT, then KT = K1 ∧ KT => KT implies K1. Equivalent KT = K1 ∧ KT. As above, possible KT true requires K1 true; KT false always. So KT => K1. And K2=KT.\n\nAlso K1 = (G=W). Therefore:\n- If G=White, K1=T.\n  Then KT can be T or F? KT => K1 satisfied. If KT=T => Black true (K1=T,K2=T). If KT=F => Black mixed or false? Black false requires K1=F, but K1 true; so Black mixed (K1=T,K2=F). Thus if G=White, Black cannot be all-false; can be all-true (KT) or mixed (KM).\n- If G=Blue or G=Black, K1=F.\n  Then KT cannot be true (KT=>K1). So Black must be all-false (KF: K1=F,K2=F) because K1=F and K2 must F as K2=KT false. Thus if gems not White, Black box is all-false (both K1,K2 false). Important.\n\nNow solve by gem location cases.\n\nCase G = Blue:\n- K1=F (since not White). Black all-false KF (K1=F,K2=F). So Black false.\n- B1 = \"gems in a true box\": gem in Blue, so B1 true iff Blue all-true (BT). Thus B1=BT.\n- W1 = BT ∨ KT = BT ∨ F = BT. So W1=BT. White all-true iff W1=T => WT=BT.\n- Global at least one all-true: Blue? BT maybe; White? WT=BT; Black false. So need at least one true => BT must be T? If BT=F then Blue false, White false, Black false => no true box, violates at least one all-true. Thus BT=T.\nThen B1=T, W1=T, 