 a box that is true.\" Gem is in Blue, and Blue box has B1=T, so Blue is all-true. Thus B1=T. Consistent.\nW1 = \"A box next to the white box is true.\" Boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Blue is true, Black is false. At least one adjacent true => W1=T. White all-true. Consistent.\nK1 = \"It is not true that boxes next to white contain gems.\" Boxes next to White: Blue and Black. Gem in Blue, so it IS true that a box next to White contains gems. Thus K1=F. Consistent.\nK2 = \"The Black box is true.\" Black has K1=F,K2=F, so all statements false, not true. Thus K2=F. Consistent.\nGlobal: Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false. At least one true yes, at least one false yes, remaining? Actually there are two true and one false; \"remaining box\" concept: one all-true (Blue), one all-false (Black), remaining White all-true (allowed). Yes.\n\nNeed maybe address White all-true vs all-false? White has one statement W1=T, so all-true. Good.\n\nNow we need answer with step-by-step then final line. User requested \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output final answer...\" We can include reasoning and final line exactly. Need not overdo but enough.\n\nLet's produce reasoning:\n\nDefine box true = all statements on it true; false = all statements false; mixed otherwise.\nLet G location.\n\nStatements:\nB1 = \"G is in a true box\".\nW1 = \"Blue or Black is a true box\".\nK1 = \"G is not in Blue or Black\" i.e., G=White.\nK2 = \"Black box is true\".\n\nSince K2 says Black is true, and Black is true iff K1 and K2 are both true:\n- If K2=T then K1 must be T.\n- Therefore if K1=F then K2=F (Black all-false).\nActually if K1=F, K2 cannot be T, so K2=F, Black all-false.\n\nConsider gem locations:\n1. G=White => K1=T. Then if Black true (K2=T,K1=T), white/blue? Could derive invalid because no false box? Need concise:\n   If G=White, K1=T.\n   B1 is true iff White is true (since gem in White), so B1=White-true.\n   W1 = Blue-true OR Black-true = B1 OR Black-true = White-true OR Black-true.\n   White-true = W1 = White-true OR Black-true.\n   This forces either:\n   - White true => Blue true and White true; Black cannot be false because K1=T, so no all-false box; invalid.\n   - White false => then White-true=F requires Black-true=F; then Blue false and White false, Black mixed, no all-true box; invalid.\n   So G=White impossible.\nNeed check \"B1=White-true\" because B1 true iff gems in a true box; if G=White, yes iff White true. Thus Blue true = B1 = White true. Then W1 = Blue true or Black true = White true or Black true. White true = W1 => WT = WT ∨ KT. Cases as above.\n\n2. G=Black => K1=F => Black false (K2=F). B1 = true iff Black true = false, so Blue false. W1 = Blue true or Black true = false, so White false. No true box, invalid.\n3. G=Blue => K1=F => Black false. B1 = true iff Blue true, so B1=Blue-true. If B1=F then Blue false. W1 = Blue true or Black true = Blue true = B1. We used global constraint: Need at least one true box. Black false; if Blue false then White false too (W1=F? Actually W1=Blue true or Black true=F so White false), no true -> invalid. Hence Blue must be true => B1=T, W1=T. Then all constraints satisfied.\n\nThis proves unique.\n\nNeed be cautious: In G=Blue case, W1 = Blue true or Black true = Blue true = B1. If Blue false => W1 false -> White false, no true -> invalid; so Blue true, W1 true. Good.\n\nLet's craft final answer with final line exactly.\n\nPotential issue: final line uses \"gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\". That's our answer.\n\nLet's ensure no other cases overlooked: G=Blue, could Black be mixed instead of all-false? Since K1=F implies K2=F due to K2 self-ref? Let's prove: K2 = Black true = (K1∧K2). If K1=F, equation K2 = F∧K2 = F, so K2=F. Thus Black all-false, not mixed. Yes.\n\nLet's include in reasoning.\n\nNeed \"statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2. One T or F per id.\" final line only.\n\nLet's produce final with reasoning and final line.", "content": "Let a box be “true” if all statements on it are true, and “false” if all statements on it are false.\n\n- K2 says “The BLACK box is true,” so K2 is true exactly when both K1 and K2 are true. Thus, if K1 is false, then K2 must also be false, making BLACK all-false.\n\nConsider the gem locations:\n\n1. **Gems in WHITE**  \n   Then K1 is true.  \n   If WHITE is true, then BLUE is also true, leaving no all-false box (BLACK cannot be all-false because K1 is true).  \n   If WHITE is false, then BLACK cannot be true, leaving no all-true box.  \n   So gems cannot be in WHITE.\n\n2. **Gems in BLACK**  \n   Then K1 is false, so BLACK is all-false.  \n   B1 would be false because BLACK is not true, making BLUE false.  \n   Then W1 is false because neither adjacent box is true, making WHITE false.  \n   No all-true box exists. So gems cannot be in BLACK.\n\n3. **Gems in BLUE**  \n   Then K1 is false, so BLACK is all-false.  \n   B1 is true iff BLUE is true, so BLUE must be true to satisfy “at least one true box.”  \n   Then W1 is true because BLUE, a box next to WHITE, is true.  \n   This works: BLUE true, WHITE true, BLACK false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00376", "logic_attempt": 3, "timestamp": "2026-08-24T16:28:33", "verdict": {"parse_ok": true, "gem": "BLUE", "gem_correct": true, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": true, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": {"BLUE": [true], "WHITE": [true], "BLACK": [false, false]}}, "reasoning": "We need answer solve puzzle. Need think step by step. Let's restate problem.\n\nWe have three boxes left BLUE, middle WHITE, right BLACK. Gems in exactly one. Each box displays statements:\n\nBLUE box: [B1] \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\"\nWHITE box: [W1] \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\"\nBLACK box: [K1] \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\"\n[K2] \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\"\n\nRules: exactly one contains gems. At least one box has all statements true. At least one box has all statements false. Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix. Statement truth values determined by actual state of world (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self/cross-references).\n\nNeed determine gem and truth values B1, W1, K1, K2.\n\nNeed be careful about \"a box that is TRUE\" meaning a box whose displayed statements are all true? In Blue Prince logic, boxes have statements, a box can be \"true\" if all its statements are true, \"false\" if all false, mixed otherwise? Statement B1 \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\" means gem is in a box that is all-true? Need parse. W1 \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" means among boxes adjacent to WHITE (BLUE left and BLACK right) at least one is all-true? K1 \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" means \"It is not true that [the boxes next to the white box contain gems]\". Since exactly one gem, boxes next to white contain gems = gem is in BLUE or BLACK? Actually plural \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" would mean both adjacent boxes contain gems? But only one gem, so impossible, so K1 would always true? Wait need parse: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" Could mean \"The boxes next to the white box do not contain the gems\" i.e., gem is not in either adjacent box => gem is in WHITE. But literal \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" as plural could be \"the box(es) next to white contain gems\" meaning at least one adjacent box contains gems? In logic puzzles, \"boxes ... contain gems\" often means \"the gems are in a box next to white\" i.e., gem is in BLUE or BLACK. The negation \"It is not true that boxes next to the white box contain gems\" = gems are NOT in any box next to white => gems in WHITE. Need consider.\n\nK2 \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" means all statements on BLACK box (K1 and K2) are true. But K2 self-referential? It says black box is true, i.e., both K1 and K2 true. That's typical.\n\nNeed solve.\n\nLet's formalize.\n\nLet boxes: B (blue), W (white), K (black). Gem location g ∈ {B,W,K}. Box truth status: A box is TRUE if all its statements are true. FALSE if all its statements are false. MIXED otherwise. Rules: at least one all-true box, at least one all-false box, remaining maybe all-true/all-false/mixed. Since exactly three boxes, with at least one true and at least one false, possibilities:\n- one true, one false, one mixed\n- two true, one false\n- one true, two false\nCannot all three true/false. Also a box with one statement (B1 or W1) if statement true => box true; if statement false => box false. So BLUE and WHITE each are either true or false, not mixed because only one statement. BLACK has two statements K1,K2; can be true if both true, false if both false, mixed if one true one false.\n\nB1: \"THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\" = g is in a box that is TRUE (all its statements true). So B1 true iff the actual gem box is all-true. Note if gem box is mixed? Then B1 false. If gem box false then false.\n\nW1: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" = There exists at least one box adjacent to WHITE (i.e., BLUE and/or BLACK) whose box is TRUE. Thus W1 true iff BLUE is true OR BLACK is true.\n\nK1: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" = It is not the case that the boxes next to White contain gems = gem is not in BLUE and not in BLACK = gem is in WHITE. So K1 true iff g=WHITE. (Assuming plural \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" means at least one adjacent contains gem? Since exactly one gem, \"boxes next to white contain gems\" would mean gem in blue or black. K1 negates -> gem in white. Good.)\n\nK2: \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" = BLACK box is all-true = K1 true AND K2 true. So K2 is true iff (K1 ∧ K2). This is self-referential equation: K2 = (K1 ∧ K2). If K1 true, equation K2 = K2 (satisfied for both K2 true and false? Wait if K1 true, K2 = K2, so both possible? In classical fixed-point semantics, K2 could be true or false? Need examine self-reference with rules maybe unique. If K1 false, K2 = (false ∧ K2)=false, so K2 false. If K1 true, K2 = K2, not determinate: K2 can be true or false consistently? But statement \"THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\" meaning if K1 true, then black box true iff K2 true: indeed K2's truth value equals whether black box true, which depends on K1 and K2. If K1 true, black box true iff K2 true. So if K2 true => black box true => statement true; if K2 false => black box false => statement false. Both self-consistent? Yes K2=T -> black box true -> statement \"black box is true\" true. K2=F -> black box false -> statement \"black box is true\" false. Both are coherent fixed points? But puzzle rules and other statements may select. Need handle.\n\nActually in such puzzles, statements can be self-referential but truth values determined by actual state including truth values of every statement; there may be multiple self-consistent assignments. The puzzle uniquely solvable means only one assignment satisfying all rules. Need find.\n\nLet's enumerate possible gem locations and truth statuses.\n\nNeed consider box truth values:\nBLUE box has only B1, so status TRUE iff B1=T, FALSE iff B1=F.\nWHITE box has only W1, status TRUE iff W1=T, FALSE iff W1=F.\nBLACK box status: if K1=T and K2=T => TRUE; if K1=F and K2=F => FALSE; if one true one false => MIXED.\n\nB1 = (g is in a TRUE box). So B1 = T iff box containing gem has status TRUE. Since gem box could be BLUE TRUE, WHITE TRUE, BLACK TRUE. Need know statuses.\nW1 = (BLUE TRUE OR BLACK TRUE). W1 T iff Blue box all true OR Black box all true.\nK1 = (g=WHITE). K1 T iff gem in White.\nK2 = (BLACK box TRUE) = (K1=T AND K2=T).\n\nLet's enumerate cases by g.\n\nCase g = BLUE:\nThen K1 = F (since gem not White). K2 equation: K2 = (F AND K2)=F. Thus K2=F. So BLACK has K1=F, K2=F => BLACK box FALSE.\nB1 = \"gems in a box that is TRUE\" = BLUE box is TRUE => B1 equals status(BLUE)=B1 (since blue only statement). So B1 = B1, self-consistent both T and F? Wait if g=BLUE, B1 says gems are in a box that is TRUE. The box containing gems is BLUE. BLUE is TRUE iff B1 true. So B1 = (BLUE is TRUE) = B1. Again self-referential: B1=T => BLUE true => B1 true; B1=F => BLUE false => B1 false. Both coherent. Need constraints from W1 and at least one true/false.\n\nW1 = (BLUE TRUE OR BLACK TRUE). BLACK FALSE always in g=BLUE. So W1 = BLUE TRUE = B1. Thus W1 = B1. WHITE box status = W1 = B1. Now boxes:\nBLUE status = B1 (T/F)\nWHITE status = W1 = B1\nBLACK status = FALSE.\n\nPossible assignments satisfying at least one all-true, at least one all-false:\n- If B1=T: BLUE TRUE, WHITE TRUE, BLACK FALSE. At least one true yes, one false yes. Valid. Then B1=T,W1=T,K1=F,K2=F, gem BLUE.\n- If B1=F: BLUE FALSE, WHITE FALSE, BLACK FALSE. All three boxes false -> violates at least one true. Invalid.\nSo g=BLUE yields unique valid assignment: B1=T,W1=T,K1=F,K2=F.\n\nCase g = WHITE:\nThen K1 = T. K2 equation: K2 = (K1 AND K2)= (T AND K2)=K2, both T/F possible.\nB1 = \"gems in box that is TRUE\" = WHITE box is TRUE = W1 (since White true iff W1 true). So B1 = W1.\nW1 = (BLUE TRUE OR BLACK TRUE). BLUE TRUE iff B1=T = W1? Wait BLUE status = B1 = W1. BLACK status depends K1,K2: K1=T, K2=T => BLACK TRUE; K1=T,K2=F => BLACK MIXED.\nSo W1 = (BLUE TRUE OR BLACK TRUE) = (W1=T OR BLACK TRUE). If BLACK TRUE (K2=T), then W1 = (W1 OR T) = T regardless W1. So W1=T, B1=T. If BLACK MIXED (K2=F), then W1 = (W1=T OR F) = W1, self-consistent both? If W1=T => BLUE TRUE, so W1 true; if W1=F => BLUE FALSE, W1 false. Both coherent? Need check at least one true/false and K2.\nSubcase g=WHITE, K2=T => BLACK TRUE.\nThen K1=T,K2=T BLACK TRUE. W1=T, B1=T. Boxes: BLUE TRUE (B1=T), WHITE TRUE (W1=T), BLACK TRUE. All three true => violates at least one false. Invalid.\nSubcase g=WHITE, K2=F => BLACK MIXED (K1=T,K2=F).\nThen W1 could be T or F? Let's test:\n- W1=T => B1=T: BLUE TRUE, WHITE TRUE, BLACK MIXED. At least one true yes, at least one false? BLACK is mixed not all-false, no box all-false. Invalid (at least one box all false required).\n- W1=F => B1=F: BLUE FALSE, WHITE FALSE, BLACK MIXED. At least one false yes, at least one true? BLACK MIXED not all-true; no all-true box. Invalid.\nSo g=WHITE seems invalid, unless K2=T with all true invalid. Thus no valid.\n\nWait what about K2=T and W1 could be? We derived W1=T forced because BLACK TRUE makes W1 true. Then all three true, invalid. So g=WHITE invalid.\n\nCase g = BLACK:\nThen K1 = F (gem not White). K2 equation: K2 = (K1 AND K2)= (F AND K2)=F. So K2=F. Thus BLACK has K1=F,K2=F => BLACK FALSE.\nB1 = \"gems in a box that is TRUE\" = BLACK box is TRUE. But BLACK is FALSE, so B1 = F. Good determinate.\nW1 = (BLUE TRUE OR BLACK TRUE). BLACK TRUE false, so W1 = BLUE TRUE = B1 = F. Thus W1=F. WHITE box FALSE.\nBoxes: BLUE FALSE (B1=F), WHITE FALSE (W1=F), BLACK FALSE (K1=F,K2=F). All three false => violates at least one true. Invalid.\n\nThus only valid assignment appears g=BLUE, B1=T,W1=T,K1=F,K2=F. Need ensure no hidden nuance: B1 \"GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\" could mean gem is in a box whose statements are all true, yes. g=BLUE with B1=T makes BLUE all-true, so gem in true box, B1 true. W1 \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\": White's adjacent boxes are BLUE and BLACK. BLUE is true, so W1 true. K1 \"It is not true that boxes next to white box contain gems\": gem is in BLUE, which is next to white; so \"boxes next to white contain gems\" is true (at least one adjacent box contains gems), so K1 false. K2 \"The black box is true\": black has K1 false and K2 false => black all false, so K2 false. Good. At least one all-true: BLUE true. At least one all-false: BLACK false. WHITE true also all-true. Remaining? Actually there are two all-true (BLUE, WHITE), one all-false (BLACK). Valid: at least one true, at least one false; remaining box may be all-true/false/mix, but \"remaining box\" if exactly one true and one false? The wording: \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" With three boxes, if at least one all-true and at least one all-false, the remaining can be anything; having two all-true and one all-false is allowed? \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix\" implies among the three, one is all-true, one all-false, one remaining arbitrary. But if two all-true, then there isn't a unique remaining after choosing one all-true and one all-false; still conditions satisfy at least one true and at least one false, remaining (the third box if we choose a particular true and false) could be all-true. So allowed. Good.\n\nNeed final answer exactly one line format. But user requests \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\". We need provide reasoning then final line. Need ensure final line exact.\n\nNeed maybe consider alternative interpretations? Let's explore deeply in case puzzle intended different: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\" could mean \"there exists a box adjacent to White that is TRUE\" (all statements true). We assumed. K1 \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" Could be interpreted \"It is not true that both boxes next to white contain gems\" but since exactly one gem, \"boxes next to white contain gems\" is definitely false if it means both, making K1 always true, impossible with our solution? Actually if K1 always true, then black K2 self etc might different. But phrase \"BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" likely means \"the gems are in one of the boxes next to the white box\" (plural boxes collectively). In Blue Prince, statements often \"A box next to the white box is true\" etc. Let's search memory: This is a known puzzle? Blue Prince has logic puzzles with boxes statements, \"The gems are in a box that is true\", \"A box next to the white box is true\", \"It is not true that boxes next to the white box contain gems\", \"The black box is true\". Need maybe official solution? Let's recall Blue Prince puzzle maybe one of \"truth-teller boxes\" with colored boxes? There might be subtlety \"true box\" means a box with all statements true. We should verify by alternate parsing.\n\nLet's parse K1: \"IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS.\" Since exactly one gem, \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" could mean both adjacent boxes contain gems? Actually \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" in natural English with plural subject \"boxes\" could mean \"the boxes adjacent to white (both of them) contain gems\" but with only one gem, impossible. However in logic puzzles, \"boxes next to the white box contain gems\" often means \"the gems are located in a box next to the white box\" (i.e., one of the boxes adjacent to white contains gems). Because \"boxes\" as plural might refer to the set of boxes adjacent; \"contain gems\" means among them, gems are contained. K1 negation = gems are not in any box next to white => in white. That's our assumption. If instead literal both adjacent contain gems, K1 always true. Then