e whenever K1 false; if K1 true then K2 can be either? But use constraints.\nActually need be careful: K2 = K1 ∧ K2 implies if K1 false then K2=false; if K1 true then K2=K2 (undetermined by equation alone). In final reasoning, we can say if gems not White then K1 false -> K2 false -> Black false. If gems White then K1 true; evaluate possibilities:\nK2=T => Black true, W1 true, B1 true => all three boxes true, violates at least one false.\nK2=F => Black mixed; then W1 and B1? Let's compute if g=White and K2=F: Black mixed; W1 = Blue true or Black true (false) = Blue true = B1. B1 = \"gems in a true box\" = White true = W1. So B1=W1 and W1=B1 -> both same; if W1=T then Blue true, White true, Black mixed => no all-false; if W1=F then Blue false, White false, Black mixed => no all-true. Thus invalid.\nIf gems Black then K1 false -> K2 false -> Black false; B1 false (gem box Black not true); W1 false (Blue false and Black false? Wait W1 = Blue true or Black true; Black false; Blue status=B1=false, so Blue false; W1=false) => all three false, violates at least one true.\nIf gems Blue: K1 false -> K2 false -> Black false. B1 must be true because otherwise Blue false and W1 false leads all false? Actually need show B1 true: If B1 false, Blue false, W1? W1 = Blue true OR Black true = false OR false = false => White false, Black false => all false, invalid. Therefore B1 true, W1=true, Black false; satisfies conditions. This is nice.\n\nNeed perhaps explain K2: \"Black box is true\" => K2 = (K1 and K2). If K1 false then K2 false. If K1 true, K2 can be either but constrained by others.\n\nLet's ensure final answer gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F.\n\nNeed final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F\n\nNeed no extra after.", "content": "Let a box be “true” iff all of its statements are true, and “false” iff all are false.\n\n- K2 says “The black box is true,” so K2 is true only if both K1 and K2 are true. Thus if K1 is false, K2 must also be false.\n- K1 says “It is not true that boxes next to the white box contain gems.” Since the boxes next to white are blue and black, K1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. **Gems in BLACK**: K1 = F, so K2 = F. Black is false. Then B1 says gems are in a true box, but Black is false → B1 = F. Blue is false. W1 needs a true box next to White, but Blue and Black are both false → W1 = F. All three boxes false, violating the required at least one true box.\n\n2. **Gems in WHITE**: K1 = T.  \n   - If K2 = T, Black is true, making W1 true and B1 true → all three boxes true, violating the required at least one false box.  \n   - If K2 = F, Black is mixed. Then W1 and B1 would force either no all-false box or no all-true box. Invalid.\n\n3. **Gems in BLUE**: K1 = F, so K2 = F, making Black false. If B1 were false, Blue would be false; then W1 would also be false, making all boxes false. So B1 must be true, making Blue true. Then W1 is true because Blue is a true box next to White. This satisfies the conditions: Blue true, White true, Black false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}
