rue, Black all-true, Blue all-false, gem in White.\n\nNeed ensure step 8 \"Suppose B1 true, W1 false\" derive contradiction; but what about B1 true, W1 true? Then total true = B1+W1+W2+K1 = 1+1+1+1=4, so W1 \"exactly 3 true\" would be false, contradiction. Thus W1 cannot be true if B1 true. Actually B1 true and W1 true gives 4 true, W1 false. So B1 true impossible entirely? Let's check: If B1=true, W1 could be true -> W1 false contradiction; W1 false -> W1 true contradiction. So B1 cannot be true. Hence B1=false. Then W1 must be true to make exactly 3 true? Since total true = 0 + W1 + 1 + 1 = W1+2; W1 = (W1+2=3) => W1=1. So W1=true. Good. This derivation doesn't need all-true uniqueness beyond consistency.\n\nBut wait if B1=true and W1=false gave total true=3 -> W1 would be true, contradiction; if B1=true and W1=true gave total true=4 -> W1 false contradiction. So B1 cannot be true. Thus B1=F, W1=T, W2=T, K1=T.\n\nNeed make sure B1=false is compatible with its statement content: For B1 to be false, \"the box with no false statements has the gems\" must be false. In solution there are two boxes with no false statements: White and Black. Since only White has gems, not every no-false box has gems, and there is no unique such box; so statement false. Good.\n\nCould there be a scenario gem=White, B1=true, W1=false with B1 interpreted existential? Then B1 true because White all-true has gems, W1 false but total true=3 would make W1 true contradiction; so no. So B1 cannot be true regardless because W1 would conflict. Actually if B1 true and W1 false, total true=3, making W1 true; contradiction. If B1 true and W1 true, total true=4, making W1 false; contradiction. So B1 cannot be true in any gem=White valuation with W2=K1=true. Thus B1 false forced. This is independent of all-true interpretation? It assumes W2,K1 true. Yes.\n\nNeed check gem=White, W1 true, B1 false yields total true=3, W1 true consistent. Good.\n\nThus final.\n\nNeed output final line exactly. Before final