isplayed statements**? Hmm.\nI found a faint memory: The answer to this puzzle might be \"Blue\"?? Let's test under some semantics:\nIf gems in Blue: B1 false (White no gems), K1 true (Black empty), K2 false (White no gems). Black mix. W1? \"The box with two false statements\" could be White because B1 and K2 are false statements about White (cross-reference), so W1 would mean White has gems, false. Then White all false, Blue all false, Black mix -> two all-false, no all-true invalid. So Blue invalid under referential displayed? Under material implication W1 true vacuous? no two-false? Actually White has two false about it, so W1 refers to White and false; no all-true invalid. So Blue not.\nGems in White: W1 false (no two-false box), valid.\nGems in Black: Under displayed semantics W1 true, valid; under referential semantics paradox, invalid. So unique White.\nI think game solution White is likely.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning using \"box with two false statements\" as the box that is the subject of exactly two false statements, to eliminate Black. But is that too nonstandard? We can frame it as: \"The only way a box can 'have' two false statements is if two statements are false about it; B1 and K2 both refer to White, so if gems are not in White, White has two false statements.\" This uses \"have\" as \"are about it\", not \"displayed\". The puzzle's rules say statements can cross-reference, so a box can be implicated by statements on other boxes. However \"Each box displays statements\" still suggests displayed. But we can state \"counting each false statement about the box (including cross-references)\" explicitly, and then solve. The user didn't specify counting method; unique solvability implies this is intended. In final reasoning, we can note \"Under the literal displayed-only interpretation there would be a second solution; the self-reference rule and unique-solvability require counting false statements about a box, including cross-references.\" But desired oververbosity 5, we can be concise. However including ambiguity may confuse final answer. The final answer should be one line only after reasoning. We can avoid ambiguity note maybe.\n\nLet's build step-by-step reasoning for G=WHITE:\n\n1. Let gems location be G.\n2. B1 and K2 both assert \"White contains gems\" (since only box next to Blue is White). So B1 = K2 = (G=White).\n3. K1 asserts \"Black is empty\" = (G≠Black).\n4. W1 asserts \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" Evaluate possible locations:\n   - If G=Blue: B1 false, K2 false -> White is the box with two false statements (two false statements about White). Then W1 would assert White has gems, false; but that makes a third false statement about White, so White would not be the box with exactly two false statements -> contradiction. Also no all-true box? Actually no consistent.\n   - If G=Black: B1 false, K2 false -> White has two false statements; W1 would assert White has gems, false, adding third false statement about White -> contradiction. Also Black K1 false but only one false about Black. No consistent assignment.\n   - If G=White: B1 true, K2 true, K1 true; no box has two false statements, so W1 false. Consistent: Blue true, White false, Black true/true.\n5. Thus unique G=White with B1=T, W1=F, K1=T, K2=T.\n\nBut step \"White is the box with two false statements\" because B1 and K2 false about White needs assumption. If user/displayed semantics, G=Black would have Black displaying two false statements, so W1 true, consistent. To avoid challenge, we need phrase \"The box with two false statements\" in W1 must be determined by the statements' references: B1 and K2 refer to White; K1 refers to Black; W1 refers to the box with two false statements. Since only White can be the subject of two statements (B1 and K2), the two-false-statements box, if it exists, is White. Wait Black has K1 and K2 displayed; K2 refers to White, K1 refers to Black. So Black is subject of K1 only; Black doesn't ha