alse. This is elegant and unique. The puzzle statement \"Each box displays one or more statements\" doesn't say statements about a box count, but the cross-references deliberately create two statements about White. The phrase \"box with two false statements\" could mean \"box about which two statements are false\" because a box can \"have\" statements made about it? In natural language, \"a box with two false statements\" more likely means statements on it, but in a logic puzzle with cross-references, it's ambiguous. The unique solution relies on referential interpretation, so I think intended answer is G=WHITE.\n\nLet's verify G=WHITE under referential semantics doesn't make W1 refer to Black because Black displays two statements but they are true? The statement W1 says \"The box with two false statements has the gems.\" If interpreting \"box with two false statements\" as displayed, Black would have two true statements, so no. If referential, White has zero false, Black zero false, so no. So W1 false.\nUnder G=BLACK referential, White has two false statements (B1,K2) and W1 would be false (White doesn't have gems), adding third false about White -> inconsistency; thus no solution. This matches unique White.\n\nLet's craft final reasoning with this interpretation. Need be careful to label statements and final line exactly.\n\nPotential issue: The user's final line format: \"FINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\" They want one T/F per id. We'll output exactly.\n\nLet's produce step-by-step concise:\n- Only box next to Blue is White, so B1 and K2 are equivalent to \"White has gems.\"\n- K1 is true iff Black does not have gems.\n- Consider W1: \"The box with two false statements has gems.\" The two statements that refer to a single box are B1 and K2, both referring to White. Thus the only possible box that can be \"the box with two false statements\" is White, and it has two false statements precisely when gems are not in White.\n- If gems were in Blue or Black, B1 and K2 would both be false, so White would be the box with two false statements; W1 would then assert White has gems, which is false. But that false W1 would be a third false statement about White, destroying the \"exactly two\" condition, so no consistent solution.\n- Therefore gems must be in White. Then B1 true, K2 true, K1 true (Black empty), and no box has two false statements, so W1 false.\n- Check boxes: Blue all true (B1=T), White all false (W1=F), Black all true (K1=T,K2=T): at least one all-true and one all-false satisfied.\n\nThis reasoning assumes W1 false when no box with two false statements rather than undefined; consistent.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"If gems in White, no box has two false statements, so the definite description fails and W1 is false.\" Good.\n\nLet's also consider if G=BLUE under referential: B1 false, K2 false -> White two false; W1 would assert White has gems false -> third false about White => contradiction; also no all-true? White W1 false, Blue B1 false all false, Black K1 true,K2 false mix -> no all-true actually invalid too. So eliminated.\nG=BLACK: B1 false,K2 false -> White two false; W1 false -> third false -> contradiction; White W1 false all false, Blue false all false, Black K1 false,K2 false all false -> no all-true invalid too. So eliminated.\nActually under referential semantics, G=BLUE and G=BLACK also violate all-true if W1 false? Let's see if W1 false and refers to White, White has W1 false (all false), Blue B1 false (all false), Black K1 true/K2 false (mix) => no all-true. So invalid regardless of exact-two paradox. Under G=BLACK, Black K1 false,K2 false all false, Blue false, White false => no all-true. So invalid. So we don't even need exact-two paradox to eliminate G=BLUE/BLACK? Wait if W1 true in G=BLACK under displayed semantics, White all true, so all-true exists. Under referential semantics, W1 would be false because White doesn't have gems (the box with two false statements is W