ox capable of being 'the box with two false statements'.\" This is clear.\n\nLet's write step-by-step:\n1. There are only three boxes. Positions: Blue-White-Black.\n2. For Blue, the only \"box next to this box\" is White. For Black's K2, the only \"box next to the Blue box\" is also White. Hence B1 and K2 both assert exactly: \"White contains the gems.\" So B1 = K2 = (gem=WHITE).\n3. K1 asserts \"Black is empty\" = (gem≠BLACK).\n4. Consider W1. The only box that can have two false statements is White, because the only two statements that refer to a single target are B1 and K2, both about White. (Black's two displayed statements are not two statements about Black: K2 is about White.)\n5. If gems are not in White, B1 and K2 are both false, so White would be the box with two false statements; W1 would then say White has gems, which is false, creating an inconsistent/self-defeating state and also leaving no all-true box. Thus gems cannot be Blue or Black.\nActually \"also leaving no all-true box\" under W1=false; but if W1 true in displayed semantics, Black would all-true; but we are using referential, W1 false. Need be consistent: \"W1 would then say White has gems, false.\" So White all false; Blue all false; Black? If G=Black, Black K1 false,K2 false all false; if G=Blue, Black mix. No all-true. So invalid.\n6. Therefore gems in White. Then B1 true, K2 true, K1 true (Black empty), and no box has two false statements, so W1 false. Boxes: Blue all true, White all false, Black all true. Satisfies rules.\n\nPotential objection: In G=Black under referential, Black displays K1 false,K2 false so Black has two false displayed statements; why isn't Black the box with two false statements? Because K2 is about White, not Black; but W1 doesn't specify \"displayed statements\". We need justify \"only statements that refer to a single target are B1/K2 about White\" and \"Black's K2 is about White, so Black does not have two statements about it.\" We can mention \"The two statements that can be false about a box are B1 and K2; both are about White. Thus White is the only candidate for a box with two false statements.\" That assumes referential. Good.\nBut what about W1 itself referring to White? It can be a statement about White if White is the box with two false statements, creating self-reference; hence inconsistency when gems not White. Good.\n\nLet's produce final answer in required format exactly last line.\n\nNeed ensure no extra after final line.", "content": "Let gem location be \\(G\\).\n\n- The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so both B1 and K2 mean: “WHITE contains the gems.”  \n  Thus B1 = K2 = true iff \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).\n- K1 means “BLACK is empty”, so K1 = true iff \\(G \\neq \\text{BLACK}\\).\n- W1 refers to “the box with two false statements.” The two cross-referencing statements B1 and K2 both point to WHITE, so WHITE is the only box that can be the subject of two false statements.\n\nIf the gems were not in WHITE, then B1 and K2 would both be false, making WHITE the box with two false statements; then W1 would claim WHITE has the gems, which would be false and create an inconsistent/no-all-true-box situation. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nThen:\n- B1 = True\n- W1 = False, since no box has two false statements\n- K1 = True, since BLACK is empty\n- K2 = True\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}
