TEMENTS\" = a conjunction: global number of false statements among all 4 = 3 AND White box has exactly 2 false statements (W1 and W2 both false? W2 false if conjunction false).\n\nBlack K1: \"A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\" = a box adjacent to White (i.e., Blue or Black? White middle, next boxes are Blue and Black) contains gems. Equivalent gems in Blue or Black, not White.\n\nRules:\n- Exactly one box contains gems; other empty.\n- At least one box has all statements true.\n- At least one box has all statements false.\n- Remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or mix.\n- Statement truth values determined by actual state (gem location and truth values of every statement, including self/cross refs).\n\nNeed determine unique solution? Maybe multiple? Need solve.\n\nLet's denote truth values b1, w1, w2, k1 ∈ {T,F}. Boxes truth profiles:\nBlue has only B1: so Blue all-true iff B1=T; all-false iff B1=F.\nWhite has W1,W2: all-true iff W1=T and W2=T; all-false iff W1=F and W2=F; mix otherwise.\nBlack has K1: all-true iff K1=T; all-false iff K1=F.\n\nGlobal constraints:\nExactly one gem box G ∈ {BLUE, WHITE, BLACK}.\nAt least one box all-true; at least one box all-false.\nStatement contents:\nB1: \"AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\" among all 4 statements? Let N_false = number of false among B1,W1,W2,K1. B1 = T iff N_false ≤ 1; F iff N_false ≥ 2.\nW1: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\". Need formalize.\nW2: \"EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE AND THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\" = (N_false = 3) AND (White has exactly 2 false statements). White has exactly 2 false statements iff W1=F and W2=F (since only two statements). So W2 = T iff N_false=3 and W1=F and W2=F. But W2 appearing in own truth means self-referential: W2 = T iff N_false=3 AND W1=F AND W2=F. But if W2=F then condition W2=F is true? Wait this is tricky: W2 = [N_false=3 AND (W1=F AND W2=F)]. This equates W2 to a proposition involving W2. Need solve fixed point. Let's express:\nLet x = W2 truth value (T=1,F=0). Let y=W1 truth value. Let b=B1, k=K1. Let Fcount = (1-b)+(1-y)+(1-x)+(1-k). W2 = T iff Fcount=3 AND y=0 AND x=0? Actually \"this box has exactly two false statements\" means among W1 and W2 exactly two false statements, i.e., W1=F and W2=F (y=0,x=0). So W2 condition includes x=0. Thus:\nx = 1 iff (f=3 and y=0 and x=0), else 0.\nThis equation implies if x=1 then RHS requires x=0 -> contradiction. Therefore x cannot be 1. Hence W2 must be FALSE always. Because statement asserts its own falsity as part of conjunction; if true then W2 false. So W2=F. Good. Then White box false count: W1 may T/F, W2 always F.\n\nThen W1: \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\". Given W2 false always. Need interpret. Which box has two false statements? White has exactly two false statements iff W1=F and W2=F (both false). Blue has one statement so cannot have two false statements (unless? one statement cannot be two false). Black has one statement cannot. Thus only possible box with two false statements is White, and only if W1=F. So \"the box with two false statements\" exists iff W1=F and W2=F, i.e., White all-false. Then W1 truth: If White has two false statements, the statement says \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\", i.e., White has gems. But note if W1=F then White has two false statements; then W1's content would be \"White has gems\", so for W1=F we need White not have gems. If W1=T then White has not two false statements (only W2 false), so \"the box with two false statements\" doesn't exist; W1 would be false? Need careful: If no box has two false statements, the definite description \"the box with two false statements\" fails; statement likely false. So W1 cannot be T when no such box. Let's formalize:\nExists unique box with exactly two false statements? If none, W1=F. If exists unique X, W1 = T iff gem in X. If multiple? Not possible here except? Could Blue/Black have two false statements? They each have one