ove this one is true.\" Since B2 is the statement above B1 on Blue box (the only other statement), B1 = ¬B2 = F. Therefore Blue box mixed, White box all true, Black box all false. Exactly one gem at White.\nNeed ensure \"B2 above B1\" reasoning: We should state \"The only statement that can be 'above' B1 on the Blue box is B2, so B1 refers to B2.\" But if user listed B1 then B2, might be disputed. We can phrase: \"Because B1 refers to 'the statement above this one', and the Blue box contains exactly B1 and B2, the referenced statement must be B2.\" Actually if B1 is top, no statement above; but for puzzle to be well-defined, B2 must be above B1. We can just say \"B1 refers to B2\" as intended by puzzle's wording. The final answer will be gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure final line exactly one-line format with nothing after. Desired oververbosity 5, step-by-step concise but deductive. Need include final line only at very last. We can include reasoning paragraphs then final line.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n- Let G=W denote gems in White.\n- B2 = (White not empty) = G=W.\n- W1 = (boxes next to Blue contain gems). Blue's only neighboring box is White, so W1 = G=W.\n- K1 = (Black is middle AND a box next to Black is true). Black is right, so Black is not middle; conjunction false → K1=F. Thus Black box all false → at-least-one-all-false satisfied.\n- Need at least one all-true. Black cannot. White all true iff W1=T iff G=W. Blue all true would also require G=W (for B2=T) plus B1=T. So in any case G=W.\n- With G=W, B2=T and W1=T.\n- B1 says the statement above it is not true; the statement above it on Blue box is B2, so B1 = not B2 = F.\n- Check: Blue mixed (F,T), White all true (T), Black all false (F); exactly one gem (White). Valid.\nFinal.\n\nPotential issue: If someone thinks B1 refers to no statement, B1=T. But final answer likely F. Need maybe mention \"Interpreting 'above this one' as referring to the other statement on the Blue box, B2, as required for the reference to be meaningful\" to justify. But final answer should be decisive.\n\nLet's ensure no other hidden interpretations: \"THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE.\" \"A box next to black box is true\" could mean at least one box adjacent to Black (White) is \"true\" = all statements on White box true. Since gem=WHITE -> White all true. But conjunction still false due to first conjunct. K1=F. Good.\n\n\"BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" plural: If gem=WHITE, the only box next to Blue contains gems, so W1 true. If gem=BLUE, White empty so W1 false. If gem=BLACK, White empty so false. Good.\n\n\"THE WHITE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\" = White contains gems because exactly one box contains gems; if White not empty then gem in White. Good.\n\nLet's produce final with final line exactly.\n\nNeed ensure final line format exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F\nNo extra after.", "content": "Let B2 and W1 both depend on the gem location: the White box is not empty exactly when the gems are in White, and the only box next to Blue is White.\n\nK1 is false immediately: Black is the rightmost box, not the middle box, so its conjunction is false. Thus Black is the all-false box.\n\nTo satisfy “at least one all-true box,” the gems must be in White, making White all-true with W1 true, and also making B2 true.\n\nB1 refers to the statement above it on the Blue box, which is B2. So B1 is “not B2,” hence false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
