solutions again. So existential not unique. Therefore \"boxes next to X contain gems\" must mean all adjacent boxes contain gems (universal), so W1 always false. Good.\n\nAlternative: \"boxes next to X contain gems\" could mean \"the boxes adjacent to X (taken together) contain the gems\" i.e., the gem is among them? But exactly one gem, so at least one; nonunique. Thus universal is intended for uniqueness. Good.\n\nThus W1 false always.\n\nNow K2 depends on OR semantics. Both inclusive and exclusive yield unique under universal W1. Need decide.\n\nLet's test inclusive universal solution gem=WHITE:\n- BLUE all true (B1 true)\n- WHITE all false (W1 false)\n- BLACK all true (K1 true,K2 true)\nRule says \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" Here there are two all-true boxes (BLUE, BLACK) and one all-false (WHITE). The \"remaining box\" after at least one true and at least one false: if choose BLUE as all-true, WHITE as all-false, remaining BLACK is all-true (allowed). So valid.\nExclusive universal solution gem=WHITE:\n- BLUE all true\n- WHITE all false\n- BLACK mixed (K1 true,K2 false)\nValid.\n\nWhich one is more likely? The puzzle statement includes K2 \"EITHER boxes next to this box contain gems OR the white box contains the gems\". Since \"boxes next to this box\" for Black = White contains gems. Thus K2 says \"Either White contains gems OR White contains gems.\" If inclusive, redundant true if gem=White; if exclusive, always false (because identical propositions cannot be exclusively-or true). The phrase \"EITHER ... OR ...\" with identical propositions is a common way to express a contradiction/falsehood under exclusive OR, but under inclusive it's just redundancy. The puzzle likely intentionally includes this to test understanding of exclusive OR. Also rule \"remaining box may be mix\" maybe included because of this exact K2 making BLACK mixed. I lean exclusive.\n\nBut let's see user prompt: They provided \"EITHER ... OR ...\" not \"OR\". They also mention \"A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\" They didn't define \"either/or\" but in natural English, \"Either A or B\" often means exclusive. So final K2=F.\n\nNeed produce reasoning step-by-step and final line. Include note: \"Treating 'EITHER ... OR ...' as exclusive (only one disjunct true), matching the 'either/or' wording.\" But if the expected answer from game uses inclusive, final would be marked wrong. Need be certain.\n\nLet's search memory more concretely: Maybe in Blue Prince, \"EITHER...OR\" is actually inclusive? The game's logic might use \"OR\" as inclusive; \"EITHER...OR\" maybe not used with XOR distinction, just stylistic. But the puzzle text uses \"EITHER ... OR ...\" in statements, not \"XOR\". Many players would interpret as inclusive OR in puzzle context because logical statements \"or\" are inclusive by default. However they capitalized \"EITHER\" perhaps just grammar. The rules say \"A statement's truth value is determined by actual state\", not \"standard logical connectives are inclusive/exclusive\". But if they wanted XOR they might write \"EITHER ... OR ... BUT NOT BOTH\"; they didn't. In many formal logic contexts, \"either...or\" is inclusive? Wait, actually in formal logic, \"or\" is inclusive, but \"either...or\" in English can be exclusive. However in mathematical logic, \"p either or q\" sometimes denotes exclusive OR? Let's recall: In propositional logic, \"p OR q\" inclusive; \"p XOR q\" exclusive. \"Either p or q\" is often used for inclusive? Example: \"You can have either soup or salad\" implies not both in restaurant context. But \"Either John or Mary will come\" can include both? Usually not. So natural English exclusive. But in logic puzzles, they often specify \"inclusive or\" if inclusive; \"eithe