|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\" exactly. Use semicolons and spaces? The example: \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T\". We'll use same.\n\nLet's craft step-by-step reasoning concise but deductive:\n1. Positions: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right.\n2. \"Boxes next to BLUE\" = only WHITE, so B1 and K1 are true iff WHITE has gems (because \"BLUE box is middle\" false).\n3. \"Boxes next to WHITE\" = BLUE and BLACK; exactly one gem means both cannot contain gems, so W1 is always false.\n4. \"Boxes next to BLACK\" = only WHITE, so K2 is \"Either WHITE has gems or WHITE has gems\"; with exclusive either/or this is always false.\n5. Test gem locations:\n   - If gem=BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F, K2=F => no all-true box invalid.\n   - If gem=BLACK: similarly all false invalid.\n   - If gem=WHITE: B1=T, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F => BLUE all true, WHITE all false, BLACK mixed; satisfies rules.\nTherefore solution.\n\nNeed be careful: If K2 exclusive false, K2 statement \"Either boxes next to this box contain gems OR white box contains gems\": boxes next to black = white contains gems, so both disjuncts identical. Exclusive OR says exactly one true; since both have same truth value, always false. Good.\n\nLet's produce final answer with reasoning before final line. Final line exactly one line.", "content": "Positions: BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n\n- “Boxes next to the BLUE box” means only WHITE, so B1 and K1 are both true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- “Boxes next to the WHITE box” means BLUE and BLACK both would need gems, impossible with exactly one gem, so W1 is always false.\n- “Boxes next to THIS box” for BLACK means WHITE, so K2 says “Either WHITE contains gems or WHITE contains gems.” Under the exclusive “either/or” reading, this is always false.\n- If gems were in BLUE or BLACK, all statements would be false, violating the all-true requirement. Thus gems must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}
