tatements.\"\n3. Thus B1 and K1 (same assertion) are true iff BLACK contains gems.\n4. Suppose BLACK does not contain gems: then B1 and K1 false; W1 and K2 false; all boxes all-false, violating at least one all-true box. So BLACK must contain gems.\n5. With gems in BLACK, B1 true, K1 true; W1 false, K2 false. BLUE all true, WHITE all false, BLACK mixed, satisfying rules.\nFinal.\n\nNeed output final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F\n\nNeed ensure no extra after.", "content": "Step by step:\n\n1. The physical positions are fixed: BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n2. Therefore:\n   - W1 (“The BLACK box is the middle box”) is FALSE.\n   - K2 (“This box is the middle box”) is FALSE.\n3. These two false statements are both about the BLACK box, so BLACK is “the box with two false statements.”\n4. Therefore B1 and K1 (“The box with two false statements has the gems”) are TRUE if and only if BLACK has the gems.\n5. If BLACK did not have the gems, then B1 and K1 would also be FALSE, making every box all-false and violating the “at least one all-true box” rule.\n6. So BLACK must have the gems, making B1 and K1 TRUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}
