false => mixed.\n- Gems=Black.\nUnique, each statement truth:\nB1=T, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F.\nThis is likely intended.\n\nNeed ensure there aren't other boxes with two false statements about them?\nStatements and their subjects:\nB1: \"The box with two false statements has gems\" — subject is \"the box with two false statements\" (unknown), not directly a fixed box; it's about whichever box has two false statements. It could be about Black if Black has two false statements about it. But B1 itself if true is not false, if false is false about? Need define \"false statements about a box\" count statements whose content concerns that box and are false. B1 concerns \"the box with two false statements\" which depends on count; circular.\nW1: about Black, false.\nK1: same as B1.\nK2: about Black (this box), false.\nSo W1+K2 are two false statements about Black. Are there false statements about Blue or White? B1/K1 might be about Black if Black is box with two false statements; if B1/K1 true, they are not false. If B1/K1 false, they would be false statements about whichever box? Actually if false, \"the box with two false statements has gems\" is false; it would be about the box with two false statements (if exists) saying it has gems, but it doesn't. Could count as false statement about that box. But in solution B1/K1 true. W1 false about Black, K2 false about Black => exactly two false statements about Black. Good.\n\nCould there be false statements about White? W1 is about Black, K2 about Black, B1/K1 about Black (if true true), so none false about White/Blue. So Black uniquely has two false statements.\n\nThis interpretation yields all constraints:\n- Exactly one gems: Black.\n- At least one all true: Blue (B1=T).\n- At least one all false: White (W1=F).\n- Remaining Black mixed (T,F).\n- Self/cross references consistent:\n   * B1 true because Black is box with two false statements (W1 false, K2 false) and has gems.\n   * K1 true same.\n   * W1 false because Black is not middle.\n   * K2 false because Black is not middle.\nNo paradox because K1 true doesn't need Black to have two false displayed statements; it needs Black to be the box referenced by two false statements (W1 and K2). K1 itself being true means it's not a false statement about Black, so Black has exactly two false statements about it (W1,K2), not three. Consistent.\n\nThus final answer likely gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F.\n\nNeed provide step-by-step concise but deductive. Must be careful to explain interpretation: \"box with two false statements\" = box that is subject of two false statements? The user didn't specify, but solution uses that. Need maybe mention ambiguity resolved by consistency with rules. Since if interpreted as displaying two false statements, no solution; but puzzle says uniquely solvable, so intended reading is the box about which two false statements are made. We can state: The two statements W1 and K2 both refer to the Black box and are both false. Thus Black is the box with two false statements. Then B1/K1 are true iff gems in Black. This satisfies all constraints. Also K2 false because black is right, W1 false. Then Black mixed, Blue all true, White all false.\n\nLet's ensure no other false statements about Black: B1/K1 are true, so not false. W1 and K2 are the only false statements about Black; exactly two. Good.\n\nBut need think if B1/K1 statements themselves \"the box with two false statements has the gems\" are about Black and true; they contribute zero false statements about Black. Good.\n\nLet's produce final answer with reasoning step by step and final line exactly format. Desired oververbosity 5, user asked step by step explicit concise. We'll include enough.\n\nNeed ensure final line \"FINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F\" exactly no after.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n\n1. Positions: White is middle; Black is right. Therefore W1 \"Black box is middle\" = False; K2 \"This box is middle\" on Black = False.\n2. These are exactly two false statements about the Black box. Hence \"the box with two false statements\" = BLACK.\n3. The statement \"The box with two false statements has the gems\" (B1 and K1) is true iff gems are in Black.\n4. To satisfy at least one all-true and one all-false, set gems in Black: then Blue box B1=T => all true; White box W1=F => all false; Black box K1=T, K2=F => mixed. All constraints satisfied.\n5. Unique? If gems not Black, B1/K1 false, then Blue/White/Black all? Blue all false, White all false, Black K1 false K2 false all false -> no all-true, violates rule. Thus gems must be Black.\n\nActually check uniqueness: If gems=Blue or White, B1/K1 false, W1/K2 false => Blue all false, White all false, Black all false, no all-true; violates. So unique.\n\nNeed mention \"the other boxes don't have two false statements about them\" maybe W1/K2 only about Black; B1/K1 not false in solution. Fine.\n\nLet's ensure final line exactly with semicolons and spaces.", "content": "The two statements that definitely refer to the Black box are:\n- W1: “The Black box is the middle box” → FALSE, since Black is right.\n- K2: “This box is the middle box” → FALSE, since Black is right.\n\nSo the Black box is the box with two false statements.\n\nTherefore B1 and K1, which say “The box with two false statements has the gems,” are TRUE only if the gems are in the Black box. Placing the gems in the Black box makes:\n- BLUE: B1 = T → all true\n- WHITE: W1 = F → all false\n- BLACK: K1 = T, K2 = F → mixed\n\nThis satisfies every rule uniquely.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}
