ue because there is no such statement. This is plausible: K1 is false. But wait, Black box has one statement; \"above this one\" within black box none, so statement is false. Then solution exists with gem=White. However is K1 \"all statements on black box all false\" yes. White all true. Blue mixed. This satisfies rules: at least one all-true (White), at least one all-false (Black), remaining Blue mixed. B1 false because Blue's adjacent boxes (White and Black) do not both contain gems (White contains gems, Black empty). B2 true because White statement W1 true. W1 true because White contains gems. K1 false because there is no statement above it (or above statement doesn't exist). This seems elegant.\n\nBut wait, does K1 refer to \"the statement above this one\" in the list W1? If so K1 would be W1=T, making Black all-true and then all-false missing. So to have unique solution, K1 must be false. Could the intended meaning be \"above this one\" = within the same box? Since Black box has only K1, there is no statement above, hence false. But why would a statement refer to above within same box if there is none? In many logic puzzles, a statement like \"the statement above this one is true\" in a list refers to previous statement. But here Black box is a separate box, not list. The phrase \"above this one\" could refer to the statement displayed above it on the box? But black box has one statement; no above. So false.\n\nNeed be certain. The user explicitly labels: BLACK (right) box: [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE. Since Black box only has one statement, there is no statement \"above this one\" on the Black box. So K1 is false because the referenced statement does not exist. However, could \"above this one\" refer to the statement above in the global list? The global list has W1 above K1, so it exists. But the phrase is \"on the black box\" perhaps? The statement is on the black box, \"above this one\" likely means above K1 on the same box. Since no other stat