tatement? In logic, a definite description with non-existent referent can be considered false. Yes.\n\nLet's verify all constraints for solution:\nGem = WHITE.\nStatements:\nB1: \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" — Blue's next boxes are White and Black. White contains gems, Black empty. Not both contain gems, so B1 = F.\nB2: \"ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\" — White box has W1 = \"This box contains the gems\"; gem=White so W1=T. Therefore all statements on White true => B2=T.\nW1: \"THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\" — White contains gems => W1=T.\nK1: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" — On Black box, there is no statement above K1, so K1 = F (the referenced statement doesn't exist / cannot be true).\nBox statuses:\nBlue: B1=F, B2=T -> mixed.\nWhite: W1=T -> all true.\nBlack: K1=F -> all false.\nAt least one all-true: White. At least one all-false: Black. Remaining Blue mixed. Exactly one gem: White. Unique? Let's test if other gem assignments possible under same interpretations:\ngem=Blue: W1=F => B2=F; B1=F (not both adjacent contain gems); K1=F. Blue all-false, White all-false, Black all-false => no all-true => invalid.\ngem=Black: W1=F => B2=F; B1=F (White empty, Black has gem but not both); K1=F. Blue all-false, White all-false, Black all-false => no all-true => invalid.\nThus unique gem=White.\n\nNeed present step-by-step reasoning concise. Final line exact format:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F\n\nNeed ensure only final line after reasoning, nothing after final. The user asks \"Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\". So final answer must be last line with nothing after. We can include reasoning before. Must label every statement id. Use T/F.\n\nNeed be mindful: no \"FINAL:\" line followed by extra newline? It's okay. Last line is final. Need not put code block? They said exactly one-line format; avoid code block perhaps. Just plain text final line.\n\nLet's craft reasoning step by step:\n1. Exactly one gem. Blue's adjacent boxes are White and Black. Since only one box contains gems, both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems, so B1 is false.\n2. Black box has only one statement K1; there is no statement above it, so the referenced statement does not exist; K1 is false.\n3. Evaluate possible gem locations:\n   - If gem=Blue: W1 false; B2 false; B1 false; K1 false => all boxes all-false, violates at least one all-true.\n   - If gem=Black: W1 false; B2 false; B1 false; K1 false => same all-false, violates.\n   - If gem=White: W1 true; B2 true; B1 false; K1 false. Blue mixed, White all-true, Black all-false, satisfies all conditions.\nTherefore gem=White, truth values.\n\nNeed include \"the statement above K1\" perhaps: \"Within the Black box there is no statement above K1, so K1 cannot be true.\" But if someone argues \"above\" means W1? We need justify according to puzzle? The statement is on Black box and there is no above statement; but the global list has W1 above. To be safe, we should interpret \"above this one\" as within the same box because Black box has one statement. But is that definitely intended? The user gave \"BLACK (right) box: [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\". They didn't say \"above on the black box\" vs \"above in list.\" We need decide. The final answer depends on K1=F. If K1=W1, no solution, so puzzle only solvable if K1 false. Thus reasoning: Black box has only one statement, so no statement is above K1 on that box; K1 is false. Good.\n\nBut wait, is it legitimate to say K1 false due to no statement above? Some might argue a statement \"the statement above this one is true\" in a list with no above statement is vacuously true? Actually \"The statement above this one is true\" asserts existence of a statement above and its truth. If no statement above, the assertion is false (or at least not true). In classical logic, definite description \"the statement above\" fails to refer, making statement false/not true. So K1=F.\n\nLet's ensure B1 false: Blue's adjacent boxes are White and Black; \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" interpreted as both adjacent boxes contain gems. Since exactly one gem, impossible, so false. We should explicitly state this interpretation.\n\nPotential alternative: If B1 meant \"at least one adjacent box contains gems\", gem=White would make B1 true and Blue all-true; but then K1 false, White all-true, Black all-false; still satisfies at least one each. But then gem=White still unique? Let's check with B1=at least one adjacent and K1=false:\ngem=White: B1=T, B2=T -> Blue all-true; White all-true; Black all-false. Satisfies.\ngem=Black: B1=T (Black adjacent), B2=F -> Blue mixed; White all-false; Black all-false. No all-true => invalid.\ngem=Blue: B1=F, B2=F -> Blue all-false; White all-false; Black all-false. No all-true => invalid.\nUnique gem=White, but B1=T. Which final answer would expected? Need be certain which interpretation Blue Prince uses. The statement \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" — if gem=White, White box next to Blue contains gems; Black box next to Blue empty. Plural \"boxes next to this box\" could refer to the set of boxes adjacent, and \"contain gems\" could mean \"the boxes (as a plural group) contain gems\" maybe true if at least one of them contains gems? Let's analyze natural language more deeply.\n\nIn English, \"Boxes next to this box contain gems\" without quantifier often means \"The boxes next to this box contain gems\" = every box next to this box contains gems. Example: \"Cars in this parking lot have stickers\" means each car has stickers. \"Students in this class speak French\" means each student. So B1 false if only one of two adjacent has gems. If they wanted at least one, they'd say \"A box next to this box contains gems\" or \"One of the boxes next to this box contains gems.\" Since exactly one gem, the plural \"boxes ... contain gems\" is indeed false. So B1=F.\n\nWhat about \"BOXES NEXT TO