E\": On Black box, there is no statement above K1; but what about \"above this one\" might refer to W1 because W1 is directly above in printed list? The user lists:\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\nSo in the textual display, W1 is above K1. In the puzzle, however, boxes are left/middle/right, not vertical. The statement text \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE\" likely refers to something within the black box? But black box has only one statement, so no above. Why would they include a statement referencing \"above\" if there is no above? It's a trick: K1 is false. They might intend \"above\" in the list? But then no solution, so not. Alternatively, maybe \"above this one\" refers to the statement above on the same box only if black box had multiple statements; since it has one, K1 is false. This is the key trick.\n\nNeed maybe in reasoning say \"Since the Black box contains only K1, there is no statement above K1 on that box; therefore K1 is false.\" This addresses ambiguity.\n\nLet's consider if K1 could be considered \"undefined\" rather than false; but puzzle requires truth value T/F, so false.\n\nLet's formulate step-by-step concise:\n\n- Let gem location unknown.\n- B1: Blue's neighboring boxes are White and Black. Since exactly one box contains gems, it is impossible for both neighboring boxes to contain gems. Thus B1 = F.\n- K1: On Black box, there is no statement above K1. So K1 = F.\n- W1 true iff gem=WHITE.\n- B2 true iff W1 true, i.e., iff gem=WHITE.\n- Test gem locations:\n   * gem=BLUE: W1=F, B2=F, B1=F, K1=F -> Blue, White, Black all all-false; violates all-true requirement.\n   * gem=BLACK: W1=F, B2=F, B1=F, K1=F -> same violation.\n   * gem=WHITE: W1=T, B2=T, B1=F, K1=F -> White all-true, Black all-false, Blue mixed. Satisfies all constraints.\n- Hence gem=WHITE and truth values.\n\nNeed final line exactly.\n\nLet's ensure final answer order: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F. Yes.\n\nLet's output with reasoning then final line.", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. B1 says the boxes next to BLUE contain gems. The boxes next to BLUE are WHITE and BLACK, but exactly one box total contains gems, so both cannot contain gems. Therefore **B1 = F**.\n2. K1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but BLACK has only one statement, so there is no statement above K1. Therefore **K1 = F**.\n3. W1 is true exactly if WHITE contains the gems. B2 is true exactly if W1 is true.\n4. Test gem locations:\n   - If gem = BLUE: W1 = F, B2 = F, B1 = F, K1 = F → no all-true box.\n   - If gem = BLACK: W1 = F, B2 = F, B1 = F, K1 = F → no all-true box.\n   - If gem = WHITE: W1 = T, B2 = T, B1 = F, K1 = F → WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false, BLUE mixed. Satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
