false; WHITE mixed (W1=F,W2=T); so only BLACK could be all-true. Thus K1 must be T, making BLACK all-true.\n7. Then W2=T and K1=T => gem in BLUE or (BLACK and K1=F=false) => gem in BLUE.\nThen B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=T.\nCheck: Total true=2 => K1 true; gems in BLUE all-false => W2 true; no box has two false statements (Blue one false, White one false, Black zero) => W1 false; B1 false due \"this box has exactly two true statements\" false; all-true black, all-false blue, mixed white. Good.\n\nNeed ensure W1 false step: \"only WHITE can have two false statements\" — But what if two false statements are distributed one in Blue and one in White? The phrase \"THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\" means a single box containing two false statements. Since Blue and Black have one statement each, only White can contain two. So W1 asserts W1 and W2 are both false and gems in White. Since gems cannot be White, W1 false. But what if gems in White and W1 false W2 false would make W1 true? Self-reference: If W1 true, then statement \"box with two false statements has gems\" true means W1 and W2 both false; but W1 true contradicts W1 false. So W1 cannot be true. Thus W1 false independent of gem. Actually yes, W1's content includes \"W1 is false\" (because box with two false statements includes W1 false), so W1 cannot be true: if W1 true, then W1 false. Thus W1=F directly. Good point: W1 = (W1=F ∧ W2=F ∧ G=WHITE). If W1=T, then right side includes W1=F, contradiction, so W1 cannot be T; hence W1=F. This is stronger and avoids gem not white? But also if W1=F, could gems be white? If G=WHITE and W1=F, W2? We separately handle W2. Good.\nNeed state W1 false by self-reference: it would require itself to be false to be true. So W1=F.\nThen W2 contradiction if false? Let's derive W2=T:\n- Suppose W2=F. Since W1=F, WHITE would have both statements false, hence WHITE would be all-false. Then W2's claim \"gems are in a false box\" would be true if gems in WHITE, but W2 false; so gems not WHITE.