emaining WHITE box is mixed\" satisfying rule.\n\nLet's produce final answer with final line exactly.", "content": "B1 must be false, because BLUE contains only B1, so it cannot have exactly two true statements.  \nW1 must be false, because it would require W1 itself to be false, creating a contradiction.  \nIf W2 were false, then with B1 and W1 false, WHITE would be all-false if it held the gems; BLUE is already all-false; so gems would have to be in BLACK. But then either K1 is false making BLACK all-false (making W2 true), or K1 is true giving exactly one true statement overall (making K1 false). Contradiction. So W2 is true.  \nThus the gems are in an all-false box. BLUE is all-false, while WHITE is mixed. To satisfy “at least one all-true box,” BLACK must be all-true, so K1 is true.  \nWith K1 true, BLACK is not all-false, so the gems cannot be BLACK; they must be in BLUE. Total true statements are then B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=T, exactly two true statements, confirming K1.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}
