l-false box.\"\n\n\"Try gem=White: W2 would say White is false(all-false), requiring W2 false, contradiction. More directly, if White has gems and W2 true then White all-false => W2 false, so W2 false; but then White not all-false so W1 would need be true, yet W1 true would require White all-false with W1 false. Contradiction. So gem not White.\"\n\nActually the phrase \"if White has gems and W2 true then White all-false => W2 false, so W2 false\" is okay: W2 cannot be true because W2 true implies W2 false contradiction; therefore W2 false. Then White not all-false (since W2 false and gems in White means statement \"gems in false box\" false -> White not false). Since W2 false and White has gems, White must not be all-false, so at least one of W1,W2 true => W1 true. But W1 true means the box with two false statements has gems; if White has gems and is not all-false, it doesn't have two false statements; contradiction. Good.\n\n\"Try gem=Black: B1=false, W1=false. K1 true would require W2 true and total true=2; but W2 true would mean Black all-false, i.e., K1 false, contradiction. Thus K1=false and W2=true, but then no all-true box (Blue all-false, Black all-false, White mixed), violating rule. So gem not Black.\"\n\n\"Therefore gem=Blue. Then Blue all-false makes W2 true. W1 false because no box has two false statements. With B1=F,W1=F,W2=T, K1 must be true to make exactly two true statements. This is consistent: Black all-true, Blue all-false, White mixed.\"\n\nNeed check \"W1 false because no box has two false statements\" in final gem=Blue depends on K1 true; we can say after setting K1 true, no box has two false statements, so W1 false consistent. But to derive K1 we assumed W1 false; circular? We can derive W1 false independently? Let's see if gem=Blue, W1 = \"box with two false statements has gems\". Could W1 be true if White all-false and gems in Blue? No, W1 would require gems in the two-false box, but gems in Blue, so W1 false regardless of whether a two-false box exists. Ah yes because gem=Blue, W1's consequent \"has the gems\" false, so W1 false. Good. So W1 false independent. Then K1 = exactly two true: B1 F, W1 F, W2 T -> one true currently; K1 must be T to make total two. Then check no two-false box: White W1 F,W2 T; Blue one false; Black K1 T; yes none. Consistent.\n\nActually if gem=Blue and W2 true because Blue all-false. Then W1 false because even if there were a two-false box, it wouldn't have gems (gems Blue). So K1=T. Then Black all-true. Good.\n\nNeed final line.\n\nLet's ensure no extra after final line.", "content": "B1 is false because the Blue box contains only one statement, so “this box has exactly two true statements” cannot be true. Thus the Blue box is all-false.\n\n- If the gems were in White, W2 would require White to be all-false, which would make W2 itself false — a contradiction. So gems are not in White.\n- If the gems were in Black, B1 and W1 would be false. K1 would then have to be false, making Black all-false and leaving no all-true box. This violates the rule, so gems are not in Black.\n- Therefore gems are in Blue. Blue being all-false makes W2 true. Since the gems are in Blue, W1 is false. With B1 false, W1 false, and W2 true, K1 must be true to make exactly two statements true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}
