alue of EVERY statement\", and K1 would be false because there is no true statement above it. But many puzzle solvers might interpret \"above\" as within the same box, so K1=F. Which answer would be expected by user? Need infer from phrase \"BLACK (right) box:\"—they specify right box, not list order. Since boxes are left/middle/right horizontally, \"above\" cannot refer to other boxes. They might intend K1 to refer to the statement above it *in the black box*, but since only one statement, it's a false claim. Why would they include K1 then? To create an all-false black box regardless of gem? Actually if K1 false, black box all-false; then rules force white all-true because at least one all-true; blue all-false. Then final gem=white, K1=F. This puzzle is simpler: B1 false, W1/W2 true iff white gem, K1 false. At least one all-true forces white gem. K1 false adds black all-false. Still unique but K1 is not cross-referential; it's just a false statement due missing referent. The rules mention cross-references maybe for W2? W2 references other boxes (gem locations) not statements. But rules mention truth values of every statement including self/cross refs; W2 doesn't reference statements. B1 references box position. K1 if broken no. So cross-reference not needed except K1->W2 if intended. The rules explicitly mention \"including self- and cross-references\" maybe to handle K1->W2 (cross-box statement reference). That strongly suggests K1 refers to W2. Because otherwise there is no statement-to-statement reference at all, making that clause unnecessary. W2 refers to boxes empty, not statements. B1 refers to box identity. So K1 must refer to W2, a cross-reference. Therefore final K1=T.\n\nNeed answer accordingly.\n\nLet's craft step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. B1: Blue box is left, not middle, so B1 = False. Therefore Blue box is all-false (since only statement).\n2. Let g denote whether White contains gems. Exactly one gem box.\n   - W1 = g.\n   - W2 says the other two boxes (Blue, Black) are empty. Because exactly one box has gems, this is true iff White has gems. So W2 = g.\n3. K1 refers to the statement directly above it, W2. Thus K1 = W2 = g.\n4. Evaluate box truth:\n   - BLUE: [F] => all-false.\n   - WHITE: [g, g] => all-true if g=True, all-false if g=False.\n   - BLACK: [g] => all-true if g=True, all-false if g=False.\n5. Need at least one all-true box. If g=False, all three boxes all-false, violates. Therefore g=True.\n6. Then White contains gems; W1=T, W2=T, K1=T, B1=F. Boxes: Blue all-false, White all-true, Black all-true; satisfies rules.\n\nFinal line exact.\n\nNeed ensure final line has no extra after. We'll output reasoning then final line.", "content": "B1 refers to the BLUE box, which is on the left, not the middle, so B1 is false.\n\nLet the white box contain the gems be represented as \\(g\\).  \n- W1 is true iff \\(g\\) is true.\n- W2 is true iff the other two boxes are empty, which happens exactly when the white box has the gems, so W2 is also true iff \\(g\\) is true.\n\nK1 refers to the statement immediately above it, W2, so K1 has the same truth value as W2. Thus K1 is also true iff \\(g\\) is true.\n\nIf the gems were not in WHITE, then W1, W2, and K1 would all be false, making every box all-false, which violates the rule that at least one box must be all-true. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nSo: B1 = F, W1 = T, W2 = T, K1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}
