ast one all-true? None. Invalid.\n\nCase G=Blue: no solution for B1.\n\nThus unique solution G=White; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise, then final line exactly.\n\nBut need ensure final answer \"FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T\" exactly one line, no extra after. User asks \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer...\" So we can include reasoning before final line, then final line exactly. Must label every statement id and only these. Good.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n- K2 is always true because black box has two displayed statements, so at most two true statements trivially.\n- Therefore black cannot have two false statements; W1 refers to box with two false statements (only black could have two) so W1 is false.\n- White box has only W1, so White is all-false.\n- Given at least one all-true box required.\n- Need determine gems: If gems in Black, K1 false, Black mix (K1 F, K2 T); White false; Blue? B1 = \"gems in false box\" false because Black not false -> Blue false. No all-true box -> invalid.\n- If gems in Blue, B1 must equal \"Blue is false\" (since gems in Blue). If B1 true, Blue true, so statement false; if B1 false, Blue false, so statement true. Contradiction.\n- Thus gems in White. Then K1 true, K2 true; Black all-true. B1: gems in White (false box) true; White all-false. All rules satisfied.\nFinal.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"White is all-false\" and \"B1 true because gems are in White, which is false box.\"\n\nLet's ensure no issue with \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" In solution, Blue all-true, White all-false, Black all-true, so remaining Black all-true allowed. At least one all-true (Blue/Black), at least one all-false (White). Good.\n\nLet's final.", "content": "K2 is always true because the black box has exactly two statements, so it cannot have more than two true statements. Therefore the black box cannot be “the box with two false statements,” so W1 is false. That makes the white box all-false.\n\nThe gems cannot be in blue: if B1 were true then blue would not be false, but B1 claims it is false; if B1 were false then blue would be false, making B1 true. The gems cannot be in black: then black would be mixed and white all-false, leaving no all-true box. Therefore the gems must be in white, making K1 true, B1 true, W1 false, and K2 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}
