l-false, White all-false, Black mixed => no all-true. If G=White: Blue B1=T all-true, White all-false, Black all-true => no mixed. If G=Blue impossible. So no solution. Since puzzle uniquely solvable, must allow multiple all-true/all-false? Actually statement: \"The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" It doesn't say \"the remaining box is the only one with that property\" just may be. So multiple all-true allowed. Good.\n\nNeed maybe consider if \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements TRUE\" and \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE\" plus remaining may be all-true/all-false/mix means at least one all-true and at least one all-false; no restriction on third. Good.\n\nCould G=White solution have Blue all-true and Black all-true, meaning there is no \"remaining box\" that is mix; but remaining can be all-true, so fine.\n\nLet's produce final answer now.", "content": "K2 is always true: the Black box has exactly two statements, so it cannot have more than two true statements.  \nThus the Black box cannot have two false statements, and since Blue and White each have only one statement, no box has two false statements. Therefore W1 is false, making White an all-false box.  \n\nThe gems cannot be in Blue: B1 would say “Blue is false,” but that would require B1 itself to be false, a contradiction.  \nThe gems cannot be in Black: Black would be mixed (K1 false, K2 true), so B1 would be false, leaving no all-true box.  \nTherefore the gems must be in White. Then B1 is true, K1 is true, and K2 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}
