tly 3 statements false). Blue empty iff gem≠BLUE.\n5. Try gem=BLUE: K1=T, B1=F, W1=F. If W2=F false count=3, making W2=true (since both disjuncts? Actually Blue empty false, exactly3 true -> XOR true), contradiction. If W2=T false count=2 -> W2=false, contradiction. So impossible.\n6. Try gem=BLACK: K1=F, B1=F, W1=F, Blue empty T. If W2=T false count=3 -> W2 = T XOR T = F contradiction. If W2=F false count=4 -> W2=T contradiction. Impossible.\n7. Therefore gem=WHITE. Then K1=F, W1=F, Blue empty T. If W2=T: B1=F, false count=3 -> W2=T XOR T=F contradiction. If W2=F: B1=T, false count=3 -> W2=T XOR T=F consistent. Thus W2=F, B1=T.\nCheck all-true/all-false: Blue all-true, White all-false, Black all-false; satisfies.\n\nNeed final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F\n\nLet's ensure no extra after final.", "content": "Exactly one gem means Blue and Black cannot both contain gems, so W1 is false.\n\nK1 is true exactly when Blue has the gems, because then White and Black are empty.\n\nOnly White has two statements, so “the box with two false statements” can only be White. Thus B1 is true only if White has the gems and both W1 and W2 are false.\n\nTreating “EITHER … OR …” as exclusive OR:\n\n- If gem = BLUE: K1 true, B1 false, W1 false. W2 would be contradictory regardless of its own value.\n- If gem = BLACK: K1 false, B1 false, W1 false, and Blue is empty. W2 is also contradictory.\n- Therefore gem = WHITE.\n\nWith gem = WHITE:\n- K1 = F\n- W1 = F\n- Blue is empty, so W2’s first condition is true.\n- If W2 were T, B1 would be F, giving exactly 3 false statements, so W2 would be F — contradiction.\n- Thus W2 = F, making White have two false statements and the gems, so B1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}
