tradiction. So W2 false; then B1 true because White has two false statements and gems. Boxes satisfy global: Blue all-true, White all-false, Black all-false.\n\nNeed be careful in g=Black W2 true/false contradiction: \"If W2 true, false count=3 => E true, W2 = true XOR true = false\" yes. \"If W2 false, false count=4 => E false, W2 = true XOR false = true.\" Good.\n\nIn g=White W2 true: B1 false because W2 not false; W1 false; K1 false => false count=3 => E true; Blue empty true, so W2 = true XOR true = false contradiction. If W2 false: B1 true; W1 false; K1 false => false count=3; Blue empty true => W2=true XOR true=false consistent. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"At least one all-true/all-false satisfied: BLUE all-true; WHITE all-false; BLACK all-false.\" Good.\n\nLet's ensure final line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F\n\nNo trailing spaces? Fine.", "content": "W1 is false because exactly one box holds the gems, so both neighboring boxes of WHITE cannot both contain gems.  \nK1 is true exactly when the gems are in BLUE, since it claims the other two boxes are empty.  \nB1 refers to WHITE, the only box with two statements; it is true only if WHITE has two false statements and contains the gems.\n\nTesting gem locations:\n- If gems are in BLUE, K1=true, B1=false, W1=false, and W2 becomes self-contradictory.\n- If gems are in BLACK, K1=false, B1=false, W1=false, and W2 again becomes self-contradictory.\n- Therefore gems must be in WHITE.\n\nWith gems in WHITE: K1=false, W1=false, Blue is empty, and exactly three statements are false when W2=false. Thus W2=false, and B1=true because WHITE has two false statements and contains the gems. The boxes satisfy the all-true/all-false rule: BLUE is all-true; WHITE and BLACK are all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}
