e and Black? Wait K1: \"The other two boxes relative to the Blue box are empty\": If gems in Black, then Black not empty, so K1 false yes. B1 false because gems not White. W1 false. W2 first disjunct Blue empty true. Exclusive: W2 = not(T=3). False count = B1 F (1) + W1 F (1) + K1 F (1) + W2? = 3 + (W2?0:1). If W2=T, T=3 => W2 = not(3)=F contradiction. If W2=F, T=4 => W2=not(4)=T contradiction. Correct.\n\nBlue case: G=Blue, K1=true, B1=false, W1=false. W2 first disjunct Blue empty false. Exclusive: W2 = (T=3). False count = B1 F(1)+W1 F(1)+K1 T(0)+W2? =2+(W2?0:1). If W2=F, T=3 => W2=T contradiction; if W2=T, T=2 => W2=F contradiction. Correct.\n\nWhite case: G=White, K1=false, W1=false. B1 = (W2=F and G=W) = W2=F. W2 first disjunct Blue empty true. Exclusive: W2 = not(T=3). False count = B1? + W1 F(1)+ K1 F(1)+ W2? =2+(B1?0:1)+(W2?0:1). If W2=F => B1=T => T=2+0+1=3 => W2=not(3)=F consistent. If W2=T => B1=F => T=2+1+0=3 => W2=not(3)=F contradiction. Good.\n\nNeed final answer line exactly:\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F\n\nLet's ensure no hidden issue: If W2 exclusive, \"Either Blue empty or exactly three statements are false\" with both true => false. We used. If the game's intended logic treats \"Either...or\" as inclusive? Then B/X inclusive had no solution, so puzzle wouldn't be solvable. Therefore exclusive is intended due \"Either\". Good.\n\nLet's perhaps mention \"I take 'Either...or' as exclusive, as natural wording indicates.\" Could be important.\n\nNeed final line exactly one-line format, no extra after. We'll output reasoning paragraphs then final line. Ensure final line is very last.\n\nLet's craft final answer with moderate detail.", "content": "Given W1: “Boxes next to the White box contain gems” — since exactly one box contains the gems, both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems, so W1 is always false.\n\nThe only box with two statements is WHITE, so “the box with two false statements” refers to WHITE, and only if both W1 and W2 are false.