ACK.\n\nW1: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" Above W1 within WHITE box? There is no statement above? Wait WHITE box displays one statement W1. \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" refers to statement above this one: probably B2? Since boxes in order Blue (left), White (middle), Black (right), but statements displayed vertically maybe W1 is below B1/B2? Need interpret. The statement above W1 could be B2? Actually in the layout: BLUE box has B1 and B2; WHITE box has W1 below; BLACK box K1. W1: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" likely refers to statement directly above it in the puzzle text? It might be B2 (the last statement in BLUE box above WHITE). Or maybe \"above this one\" in same box? There is no statement above W1 inside white box. So W1 refers to B2, the statement immediately above? Need examine. The puzzle lists BLUE box statements B1, B2, then WHITE box W1 below, BLACK K1. The statement above W1 could indeed be B2. This is common self-referential puzzle: W1 says \"The statement above this one is true\", referencing B2. Need consider.\n\nK1: \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\" = BLUE and WHITE empty -> gem in BLACK. So K1 = gem in BLACK.\n\nNeed solve fixed point with truth values.\n\nLet's denote gem location G ∈ {BLUE, WHITE, BLACK}. Let B1, B2, W1, K1 truth values.\n\nDefinitions:\nB1 = (G != BLUE and G != WHITE) i.e. G == BLACK? Wait \"other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty\" = Blue empty and White empty => G=BLACK. Yes B1 = (G=BLACK).\n\nB2 = (a box next to WHITE contains gems) OR (BLACK is middle box). Box next to WHITE: BLUE and BLACK. So first = G=BLUE or G=BLACK. Second = false (black is right). So B2 = (G != WHITE). Equivalent G=BLUE or G=BLACK.\n\nW1 = \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" = B2 is true. So W1 = B2.\n\nK1 = \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\" = Blue and White empty => G=BLACK. So K1 = B1 = (G=BLACK).\n\nNow need satisfy rules: exactly one gem; at least one box all statements true; at least one box all statements false; remaining any.\n\nBoxes:\nBLUE box statements: B1, B2. Its state: all true if B1=T and B2=T; all false if B1=F and B2=F; mixed otherwise.\nWHITE box one statement W1. All true iff W1=T; all false iff W1=F (one statement both all true/false impossible? For a single statement box, all true = statement true, all false = statement false; since one statement, it's either all true or all false, not mix).\nBLACK box one statement K1. All true iff K1=T; all false iff K1=F.\n\nNeed at least one all true, at least one all false.\n\nLet's enumerate gem cases:\n\nCase G = BLUE:\nB1 = (G=BLACK)=F.\nB2 = (G != WHITE)=T.\nSo BLUE box mixed (F,T).\nW1 = B2 = T. WHITE all true.\nK1 = (G=BLACK)=F. BLACK all false.\nConditions: all true boxes: WHITE yes. all false boxes: BLACK yes. At least one each. Works. Truth values: B1=F, B2=T, W1=T, K1=F, gem=BLUE.\n\nCase G = WHITE:\nB1=F.\nB2=(G != WHITE)=F (since G=WHITE).\nBLUE all false.\nW1=B2=F. WHITE all false.\nK1=F. BLACK all false.\nAll true boxes: none. Fails at least one true. So invalid.\n\nCase G = BLACK:\nB1=T.\nB2=(G != WHITE)=T.\nBLUE all true.\nW1=B2=T. WHITE all true.\nK1=T. BLACK all true.\nAll false boxes: none. Fails at least one false. Invalid.\n\nThus unique solution G=BLUE, B1=F, B2=T, W1=T, K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure if W1 reference not B2 but something else? Let's consider possibility W1 refers to \"statement above this one\" within WHITE box? There's none, so maybe self-referential? If no statement above, statement false? But puzzle likely W1 refers to B2. Need be certain. The wording in user: \"WHITE (middle) box: [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" It's ambiguous but likely refers to the statement above in the list, B2. Since W1 is in middle box, above it physically in displayed puzzle is BLUE box's B2? Actually Blue box left, White middle, Black right horizontally; \"above\" might mean above in the vertical list? The puzzle text lists BLUE (left) [B1] [B2], WHITE (middle) [W1], BLACK (right) [K1]. So yes above W1 in text is B2. Need maybe \"above this one\" could reference \"the statement above this one\" in same box? There is no above inside white box. So must be B2.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step reasoning concise and final line exactly. Need final answer in one-line format only after reasoning? User asked think step by step, then on very last line output final answer in exactly format. We'll include reasoning then final line. Ensure final line has exactly format with labels B1, B2, W1, K1. No extra after.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements TRUE/FALSE\" rules: We used. Need maybe check \"remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" okay.\n\nLet's craft reasoning with explicit truth functions.\n\nNeed maybe address ambiguity W1 = B2. We'll state \"W1 refers to statement directly above it, B2.\"\n\nLet's produce final answer.\n\nBut let's double-check there isn't alternative interpretation of B1 \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\": Could be \"the other two boxes relative to the black box\" means boxes located relative to black box: left and right? But black box is rightmost, so \"relative to black box\" maybe \"other two boxes [besides the black box] are empty\" = same. \"Relative to\" may mean \"relative to [each other]\"?? Actually phrase \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\" maybe in game original? Could mean \"The other two boxes relative to the black box are empty\" i.e., \"the two boxes other than the black box are empty\" = blue and white empty. Yes.\n\nK1 same \"other two boxes are empty\" = gem in black. Good.\n\nNeed final.", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “BLUE and WHITE are empty” = (gem is in BLACK)\n- B2 = “gem is next to WHITE or BLACK is middle” = (gem is in BLUE or BLACK), since BLACK is not middle\n- W1 = “B2 is true”\n- K1 = “BLUE and WHITE are empty” = (gem is in BLACK)\n\nTest each possible gem location:\n\n- Gem in BLUE: B1=F, B2=T → BLUE box mixed. W1=T → WHITE all true.