em=BLACK.\n\nK1: \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\" on BLACK box. For black box, other two boxes are BLUE and WHITE. Same assertion: BLUE and WHITE empty => gem=BLACK. So K1 true iff gem=BLACK.\n\nB2: \"EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\"\nWhite box is middle. Boxes next to White: BLUE (left) and BLACK (right). \"A box next to the white box contains the gems\" means gem is in BLUE or BLACK. Second disjunct: \"THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" is false, because black is right. So B2 true iff gem ∈ {BLUE, BLACK} OR false = gem ≠ WHITE. Since exactly one gem, B2 true iff gem=BLUE or BLACK; false iff gem=WHITE.\n\nW1: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" Above White box? Need interpret: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE.\" Since W1 is on WHITE box (middle). The statement above this one could mean the statement displayed above? Boxes maybe stacked? But in left-to-right? The white box has one statement W1; above this one could refer to statement above in vertical arrangement? There is no statement above it on same box; maybe \"above this one\" means the statement on the box above? Wait puzzle says boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements. White's statement: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\". Since white is middle box horizontally, \"above\" might mean \"above this one\" in list? Hmm No statement above W1 on WHITE box? W1 is only statement on WHITE. Could refer to statement above in the list of all statements? The statements are arranged:\nBLUE:\n [B1] ...\n [B2] ...\nWHITE:\n [W1] ...\nBLACK:\n [K1] ...\n\"Above this one\" could refer to statement [B2] immediately above W1 in the textual list? Because B2 is above W1 in displayed puzzle. Yes W1 likely references \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\": the statement directly above it in the list, i.e., B2 (the last BLUE statement). Since W1 is below B2 in the puzzle statement. So W1 asserts B2 is true. Need determine.\n\nBut \"above this one\" could also mean physically above on same box? There is no. In game Blue Prince, boxes maybe laid out left-to-right? White middle, BLACK right; statements on boxes? \"above this one\" could mean statement printed above on same box? No. Since only one statement on white, above must refer to previous statement in reading order: B2. Need assume W1 = \"B2 is true.\" We'll verify uniqueness.\n\nNeed solve self-referential truth values.\n\nLet G ∈ {Blue, White, Black} gem location.\nLet truth values:\nB1 = (G == Black)\nK1 = (G == Black)\nB2 = (G != White) because second disjunct false. Actually \"A box next to the white box contains gems\" = G==Blue or G==Black. \"Black box is middle\" = false. So B2 = (G==Blue or G==Black) = G != White.\nW1 = (B2 is true) = B2 = (G != White). Assuming W1 references B2.\n\nThus all statements are determined solely by G:\nIf G=Black: B1=T, B2=T, K1=T, W1=T.\nIf G=Blue: B1=F, B2=T, K1=F, W1=T.\nIf G=White: B1=F, B2=F, K1=F, W1=F.\n\nNow apply box all-true/all-false rules:\nBoxes: BLUE contains B1,B2; WHITE contains W1; BLACK contains K1.\n\nCase G=Black:\nBLUE: B1=T,B2=T => all true.\nWHITE: W1=T => all true.\nBLACK: K1=T => all true.\nAll three boxes all-true. At least one all-true yes; at least one all-false? No, no all-false boxes. Rule requires AT LEAST ONE box has ALL statements FALSE. Thus G=Black invalid.\n\nCase G=Blue:\nBLUE: B1=F,B2=T => mixed.\nWHITE: W1=T => all true.\nBLACK: K1=F => all false.\nAt least one all-true yes (WHITE); at least one all-false yes (BLACK). Valid? Remaining box BLUE mixed allowed. So G=Blue valid.\n\nCase G=White:\nBLUE: B1=F,B2=F => all false.\nWHITE: W1=F => all false.\nBLACK: K1=F => all false.\nAt least one all-true? No, all all-false. Rule requires at least one all-true. Invalid.\n\nThus unique solution G=Blue, B1=F, B2=T, W1=T, K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure W1 interpretation. But if W1 refers to \"statement above this one\" maybe could be B1? Need examine wording: THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE. Since White is middle box horizontally, \"above this one\" could mean statement above in vertical? In the diagram maybe Blue left, White middle, Black right, statements maybe printed on boxes? If boxes are side-by-side, \"above this one\" on middle box might refer to statement above the white box? But there is no above box; maybe \"above\" means \"preceding\" in a sequence? In puzzle text, THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE likely refers to statement immediately above W1 in the list: B2. It says \"above this one\" singular. Since W1 is only statement on WHITE, and directly above it in the listed statements is B2. So yes.\n\nNeed produce step-by-step concise and final line exact.\n\nNeed maybe consider ambiguity: B1 \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\" Could \"relative to black box\" mean boxes positioned relative to black? Black is right, other two relative to it = left and middle? Yes. Could \"relative to black box\" mean \"with respect to black box, the other two boxes are empty\" same as other two boxes = not black = blue+white. Same result. Good.\n\nNeed consider K1 \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\" on BLACK box: other two are BLUE and WHITE. Same as B1.\n\nNeed consider B2 \"EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\": White is middle, boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK. Black is right, so second false. Therefore B2 true iff gem is BLUE or BLACK (i.e., not WHITE). Good.\n\nNow need final output exactly one line with format. They ask \"Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it): FINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\" Also \"Think step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer...\" So in final answer, include reasoning then final line. The last line must be exactly format nothing after. We can write reasoning before, then final line. Need ensure final line only final. Should not include \"FINAL:\" earlier? Fine.\n\nNeed include label every statement id. We'll use concise step-by-step.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n\nLet G=BLUE/WHITE/BLACK.\n\nPositions: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right.\n\nB1 and K1 both assert the two boxes other than BLACK are empty -> equivalent to G=BLACK. So B1=K1 = (G=BLACK).\n\nB2: White is middle, so boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK; \"BLACK is middle\" is false. Hence B2 = (G is BLUE or G is BLACK) = (G ≠ WHITE).\n\nW1: \"The statement above this one\" refers to B2, so W1 = B2.\n\nThen enumerate:\nG=BLACK: B1=T,B2=T,K1=T,W1=T -> all three boxes all-true, no all-false box -> violates at least one all-false.\nG=WHITE: B1=F,B2=F,K1=F,W1=F -> all boxes all-false, no all-true -> violates at least one all-true.\nG=BLUE: BLUE box B1=F,B2=T mixed; WHITE W1=T all-true; BLACK K1=F all-false -> satisfies both at least one all-true and all-false. Valid.\n\nTherefore G=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nNeed ensure \"remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" fine.\n\nPotential issue: The rules say \"AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE. AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE. The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\" In G=Blue, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false, BLUE mixed. Good.\n\nLet's answer.", "content": "Positions: BLUE=left, WHITE=middle, BLACK=right.\n\n- B1 and K1 both say the two non-BLACK boxes are empty, so each is true exactly when the gems are in BLACK.\n- B2: boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK; “BLACK is middle” is false. So B2 is true when gems are in BLUE or BLACK.\n- W1 refers to B2, so W1 has the same truth value as B2.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n- Gems in BLACK: B1=T, B2=T, K1=T, W1=T. No all-false box → invalid.\n- Gems in WHITE: B1=F, B2=F, K1=F, W1=F. No all-true box → invalid.\n- Gems in BLUE: BLUE box is mixed; WHITE box is all-true; BLACK box is all-false → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
