r: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F. Need justify W1 = B2 because \"above this one\" is B2 as listed above W1. We'll state assumption.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\nLet gem location be G ∈ {BLUE, WHITE, BLACK}. Boxes order: Blue left, White middle, Black right.\nInterpret \"above W1\" as B2, the statement immediately above it in the list. Thus W1 ↔ B2.\nB1: \"The other two boxes relative to the black box are empty\" = Blue and White empty ⇒ G=BLACK.\nK1: \"The other two boxes are empty\" (for black) = Blue and White empty ⇒ G=BLACK.\nB2: \"Either a box next to White contains the gems or Black is middle.\" Boxes next to White are Blue and Black; Black is not middle. So B2 = (G=BLUE OR G=BLACK) = G≠WHITE.\nW1 = B2 = G≠WHITE.\n\nEvaluate cases:\nG=BLACK: Blue T,T; White T; Black T. All boxes all-true, none all-false -> violates at least one all-false.\nG=WHITE: Blue F,F; White F; Black F. All boxes all-false, none all-true -> violates at least one all-true.\nG=BLUE: Blue F,T (mix); White T (all-true); Black F (all-false). Satisfies both conditions and exactly one gem. Hence unique.\n\nFinal line.\n\nNeed ensure \"remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix\" okay.\n\nLet's produce final answer with step-by-step and final line exactly.", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, or BLACK.  \nInterpreting W1 as referring to the statement immediately above it in the displayed list, W1 = “B2 is true.”\n\n- B1: “The other two boxes relative to the BLACK box are empty” means BLUE and WHITE are empty, so gems must be in BLACK. Thus B1 = T iff gem = BLACK.\n- K1: “The other two boxes are empty” also means BLUE and WHITE are empty, so K1 = T iff gem = BLACK.\n- B2: “Either a box next to the WHITE box contains the gems or the BLACK box is the middle box.” The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK, and BLACK is not the middle box. So B2 = T iff gem is BLUE or BLACK, i.e. gem ≠ WHITE.\n- W1 = B2 = T iff gem ≠ WHITE.\n\nCheck cases:\n\n1. gem = BLACK:  \n   B1=T, B2=T, W1=T, K1=T. All boxes all-true, but no box is all-false → invalid.\n\n2. gem = WHITE:  \n   B1=F, B2=F, W1=F, K1=F. All boxes all-false, but no box is all-true → invalid.\n\n3. gem = BLUE:  \n   B1=F, B2=T, W1=T, K1=F.  \n   BLUE box is mixed, WHITE box is all-true, BLACK box is all-false → valid.\n\nTherefore the unique solution is:\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00136", "logic_attempt": 1, "timestamp": "2026-08-29T16:14:32", "verdict": {"parse_ok": true, "gem": "WHITE", "gem_correct": true, "truths_correct": false, "count_ok": true, "correct": false, "parsed_truths": {"BLUE": [true], "WHITE": [true], "BLACK": [false]}}, "reasoning": "We need answer solve. Need think carefully. We have logic puzzle with self-referential statements. Need determine gem and truth values.\n\nWe need parse puzzle.\n\nBoxes: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right.\nStatements:\nB1: \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" — Blue box is left, boxes next to it: only White middle? Since Blue left has one adjacent box: White. So B1 says White box contains gems. \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" plural boxes next to this box (maybe left/right neighbors). For Blue, next boxes = White only. So B1 = \"White contains gems\" (assuming \"boxes next\" = adjacent boxes collectively contain gems? Wording \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" could mean the box(es) next to this box contain gems, i.e., at least one adjacent box contains gems? Need examine. \"BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\" if plural but only one box next to left, still natural means the box next to it contains gems. For middle, next boxes are Blue and Black; but W1 is not that. K1: \"THIS BOX IS BLUE\" — Black box says it is Blue, obviously false because Black is not Blue (the box labeled BLACK is black? Wait \"This box is blue\" means color? The boxes are named BLUE, WHITE, BLACK by their colors? There is BLUE box left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. K1 says \"THIS BOX IS BLUE\" = the Black box is Blue? False, because it's BLACK. But could \"blue\" refer to left box identity? No, \"this box is blue\" means its color/name is blue; Black box is not blue. So K1 false.\n\nW1: \"THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\" — Above W1 is B1 (since White middle, above it is Blue left? In display on page, Blue box left, White middle, Black right, W1 says statement above this one is true; above White could be Blue? The arrangement: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). W1's \"statement above this one\" probably refers to B1 in BLUE box, above physically? There is no above within same box; statement above this one = statement in box above? They list BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right. Likely \"above\" means previous statement, i.e., B1. W1 = \"B1 is true.\" Need confirm. In logic puzzles, \"statement above\" means statement printed above it, i.e., B1 (since BLUE list above WHITE?). Yes.\n\nWe have exactly one gem box. At least one box all statements true, at least one all false, remaining mix.\n\nNeed find consistent assignment.\n\nLet variables:\nLet G_B, G_W, G_K? Use gem location exactly one true.\nB1 = \"boxes next to this box contain gems\". For Blue left, adjacent box is White. So B1 = G_W? Need decide if \"boxes next to this box contain gems\" means every neighboring box contains gems? Since only one neighbor, B1 = White contains gems = G_W. If multiple neighbors, would mean all adjacent boxes contain gems. Here Blue only White. So B1 = G_W.\nW1 = \"The statement above this one is true\" = B1 is true = B1 = G_W.\nK1 = \"This box is Blue\" = false always (Black box is not Blue), assuming names are colors. So K1 = F.\n\nThen truth values:\nB1 = G_W\nW1 = B1 = G_W\nK1 = F\n\nNeed exactly one gem among Blue, White, Black. Cases:\n\nGem Blue: G_B true, G_W false, G_K false.\nThen B1 = false; W1 = false; K1=false.\nTruth values by box:\nBlue box only B1 = F -> all false.\nWhite box W1 = F -> all false.\nBlack box K1 = F -> all false.\nBut rule requires at least one all-true box. None all-true. Invalid.\n\nGem White: G_W true.\nThen B1=true; W1=true; K1=false.\nBlue box B1=T -> all true.\nWhite box W1=T -> all true.\nBlack box K1=F -> all false.\nAt least one all-true (Blue, White), at least one all-false (Black), remaining? Actually there are 3 boxes; exactly one all-true? Rule says AT LEAST ONE all-true, AT LEAST ONE all-false, rem