es. If W1 = B1 is true, then W1 = B1. Since B1 = White contains gems. Yes.\n\nLet's craft answer with step-by-step.\n\nNeed output very last line exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F\nNo extra after. Ensure nothing after final line. final answer includes reasoning then final line.\n\nLet's produce final with reasoning paragraphs, then final line.", "content": "Let gem locations be exactly one of BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n- K1 says “This box is BLUE,” but the BLACK box is not BLUE, so K1 is always FALSE.\n- B1 says “Boxes next to this box contain gems.” The BLUE box has only one neighboring box: WHITE. So B1 means “WHITE contains the gems.”\n- W1 says “The statement above this one is true,” meaning B1 is true. So W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE, then WHITE has no gems, so B1 = FALSE, W1 = FALSE, K1 = FALSE. No box has all statements true, so invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK, then WHITE has no gems, so B1 = FALSE, W1 = FALSE, K1 = FALSE. Again no all-true box, so invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE, then B1 = TRUE, W1 = TRUE, K1 = FALSE. This gives at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
