t your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\" So final response can have reasoning before, then final line. Need ensure final line is last, no extra text after.\n\nLet's craft reasoning:\n- White box middle, neighbors BLUE and BLACK.\n- B1 means both adjacent boxes contain gems; but exactly one gem, so impossible -> B1=false always.\n- W1 true iff gem in WHITE.\n- K1 = (gems in a true box) ∧ (W1 true). Since each box has one statement, true box means its statement true.\n- If gem in BLUE: B1 false, W1 false, gem box not true, so K1 false => all false, violates at least one all-true.\n- If gem in BLACK: B1 false, W1 false, K1 = (K1) ∧ false = false => all false, violates.\n- Therefore gem in WHITE. Then W1 true; B1 remains false; gem box WHITE is true and W1 true -> K1 true.\nFinal.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"remaining box may be mix\" not relevant.\n\nLet's final.", "content": "White is the middle box, so the boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK. Since exactly one box contains gems, both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems, so B1 is always false.\n\nW1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\nK1 means: the gem box’s own statement is true, and W1 is true.  \n- If gems were in BLUE, B1 and W1 would both be false, making K1 false too → all boxes false, violating the rule.  \n- If gems were in BLACK, W1 would be false, so K1 would be false regardless → all boxes false again.  \n- Therefore gems must be in WHITE. Then W1 is true, and K1 is also true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
