intended unique solution. Need identify intended K1.\n\nLet's examine exact wording: BLACK (right) box: [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE. In a list of three boxes, BLACK is third; \"the statement above this one\" could be W1 because W1 is above K1 in the textual listing. They also label boxes by position left/middle/right, but statements are listed vertically: BLUE first, WHITE second, BLACK third. \"Above\" in the listing is W1. In many logic puzzles, \"the statement above\" means the previous statement in the list. Since K1 is on black box (right) but the statement above in the list is W1 on white box (middle). Cross-box reference possible. Rules mention cross-references, so K1 referencing W1 is plausible.\n\nBut why would a statement on black box refer to \"the statement above this one\" rather than \"the statement on the white box\"? Because physically in the game, boxes might be arranged vertically? Wait user says BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). So horizontal. \"Above\" not horizontal. Thus \"above this one\" likely refers to the position in the list/statement stack, not box spatial arrangement. The problem statement lists statements vertically; K1's \"above\" is W1. So K1=W1.\n\nCould there be another interpretation: \"THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\" B1: Blue box says \"This box is the middle box.\" Since B1 is on blue box, \"this box\" = blue box. Blue is left, not middle => false. Good.\n\nW1: \"THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\": White box is middle; other two relative are left and right. So W1 = (BLUE empty AND BLACK empty) = gem in WHITE. Good.\n\nK1: If above statement = W1, K1 = truth value of W1. Then solution gem=WHITE, B1=F, W1=T, K1=T. This yields BLUE all false, WHITE all true, BLACK all true. At least one all true (two), at least one all false (one). Valid. Unique? Under K1=W1, yes unique? Let's check if K1=W1 and B1=F, W1=(G=WHITE). Cases:\n- G=BLUE: W1=F,K1=F -> all false -> no true invalid.\n- G=BLACK: same invalid.\n- G=WHITE: W1=T,K1=T -> valid. Unique.\n\nIf K1=false (no referent), solution also unique gem=WHITE,B1=F,W1=T,K1=F. But then K1's content \"The statement above this one is true\" false because no statement above? However if there is no statement above, does statement have a truth value? The rule says truth value determined by actual state including truth values of every statement. If no referent, not determined by statement truth values; but could be determined by world fact \"there is no statement above\", making claim false. But then K1 is not referencing another statement; it's a meta statement about nonexistence. Would rule allow? It says \"including self- and cross-references\", not \"only\"; factual statements about world also allowed. \"The statement above this one is true\" contains a definite description; if no statement above, the proposition is false in standard semantics (or truth-valueless). But puzzle likely assumes reference exists? The rule \"including self- and cross-references\" may require every statement's truth value determined by actual state; if K1 references W1, cross-reference. The phrase \"above this one\" in list indeed cross-references W1, so referent exists.\n\nNeed ensure final answer exact format. Need output reasoning step by step and final line exactly.\n\nLet's formulate reasoning:\n1. There is exactly one gem among BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n2. B1: \"This box is the middle box\" refers to BLUE box; BLUE is left, so B1 is False.\n3. W1: \"The other two boxes relative to WHITE are empty\" means BLUE and BLACK are empty, which occurs iff gem is in WHITE. So W1 = (gem = WHITE).\n4. K1: \"The statement above this one is true\" refers to W1 (the statement listed immediately above it). Thus K1 = W1.\n5. Test gem locations:\n   - If gem=BLUE: W1=False (because WHITE not gem, other two not both empty? Actually BLUE has gem, so other two relative to WHITE are BLUE (not empty) and BLACK empty => W1 false), K1=false, B1=false => all false, violates at least one true.\n   - If gem=BLACK: similarly W1=false, K1=false, B1=false => all false, invalid.\n   - If gem=WHITE: W1=true, K1=true, B1=false => WHITE all true, BLACK all true, BLUE all false. Satisfies at least one true and at least one false.\n6. Therefore gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T.\n\nNeed be careful: In gem=BLUE or BLACK, are all statements false? B1 false, W1 false, K1 false yes. At least one true fails. Good.\n\nNow final line exactly: FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T\n\nNeed ensure no extra after final line.", "content": "B1 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle.\n\nW1 is true exactly when the gems are in the WHITE box, because then the other two boxes are empty.\n\nK1 refers to W1, the statement above it, so K1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then W1 would be false, K1 would be false, and B1 is already false — so no box would have a true statement. That violates the rule.\n\nThus the gems must be in WHITE, making W1 true and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
