ne T or F per id.\" Good.\n\nLet's produce with step-by-step but concise.", "content": "B1 is on the BLUE box and says “this box is the middle box,” but BLUE is left, so B1 is false.\n\nW1 says the other two boxes relative to WHITE are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, W1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE, and false if the gems are in BLUE or BLACK.\n\nK1 says the statement above it is true. The statement above K1 is W1, so K1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=false, W1=true, K1=true → BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-true. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}
