ean \"the black box is (in the blue box?)\"? No, \"is blue\" adjective color. Black box is black, so false. Good.\n\nLet's craft reasoning step-by-step:\n1. Black box on right has only White box next to it. So B1 true iff gems in White.\n2. W1 says B1 true, so W1 has same truth as B1.\n3. K1 says black box is blue, impossible since it is black, so K1 false.\n4. Rule requires at least one all-true box and at least one all-false. K1 already all-false for Black, so need at least one true statement among B1/W1; since they share truth, B1/W1 must be true.\n5. Therefore gems in White; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F.\n\nCheck exactly one gems: yes white.\n\nFinal line.\n\nNeed be careful: The user says \"Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it)\" So final answer line at very end. Do not put explanation after. Fine.", "content": "Black box is on the right, so the only box next to it is the White box. Thus B1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.  \nW1 says B1 is true, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.  \nK1 says the Black box is blue, which is false.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and K1 gives the Black box all-false, B1 and W1 must be true. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}
